window.stylePrompts = {"01-studio-ghibli": "Render the image as a hand-painted Studio Ghibli film frame from the late 1980s. Keep the subject in traditional cel animation, with thin economical outlines, small catchlights, simplified features, and quiet flat shading. Build a much larger world around it in lush gouache: layered foliage, a soft distant landscape, and warm dappled daylight. Leave slight handmade paint variation in the background while the figure stays crisp and graphic.", "02-simpsons": "Render the image as a classic The Simpsons television cartoon. Use bright yellow skin, huge round white eyes with tiny black pupils, a pronounced overbite, and four fingers on each hand. Draw thick uniform black outlines, flat saturated color, and sculptural outlined hair clumps. Keep the setting as simple pastel shapes with no gradients, realistic anatomy, or painterly light.", "03-cubism": "Render the image as a Picasso-style cubist oil painting. Shatter the subject into large geometric planes seen from the front and in profile at once, with features displaced across those planes and a tilted still life locked into the same structure. Limit color to ochre, muted blue, slate gray, and charcoal. Leave linen weave, uneven oil strokes, and dry scumbled pigment visible, never smooth digital facets.", "04-ukiyo-e": "Render the image as an Edo-period Japanese ukiyo-e woodblock print. Draw fine black key lines around flat unmodeled color, using tilted perspective and quiet empty space. Stylize flowing contours as rhythmic printed waves, and reduce the setting to simple screens and a few garden shapes. Print on aged washi in indigo, vermilion, pale green, ochre, and cream, with slight misregistration and organic pigment speckle.", "05-3d-animation": "Render the image as a modern 3D animated feature still in the Pixar and DreamWorks vein. Push proportions toward a very large head, huge expressive eyes, a tiny nose, a small torso, and simplified hands. Use polished glossy materials, sculpted hair, bright cinematic color, and a soft rim light, with the background falling into gentle blur. It should feel like appealing family animation, not a photograph of a real person.", "06-lego": "Render the image as a macro photograph of a physical LEGO minifigure in a brick-built set. Use real minifigure parts: a cylindrical yellow head with a printed face, a separate molded hair piece, a trapezoid torso, hinged arms, C-shaped claw hands, and short block legs. Make every prop and surface interlocking ABS plastic, with studs, seams, and light scuffs. Light it as miniature toy photography, warm and slightly shallow in focus.", "07-claymation": "Render the image as a handmade British stop-motion plasticine puppet in the Aardman style of Wallace and Gromit. Sculpt a bulbous head, a comic oversized nose, a wide grin of separate chunky teeth, and protruding oval eyes. Leave thumbprints, tool marks, seams, and lumps in the clay. Photograph the figure on a slightly crooked miniature set under warm practical lamps, matte and imperfect rather than polished CGI.", "08-pixel-art": "Render the image as 16-bit SNES-era JRPG pixel art. Construct a top-down tile scene from crisp square pixels in about thirty-two flat colors, with stepped shading and obvious tile repetition. Shrink the subject to one small chibi sprite, oversized head and stubby limbs, placed inside a much larger map. Forbid anti-aliasing, blur, gradients, and smooth illustration so every edge stays hard and grid-locked.", "09-manga": "Render the image as a black-and-white shoujo manga panel drawn with a G-pen. Use fine tapering ink lines, oversized sparkling eyes, and elegant exaggerated proportions. Shade only with mechanical screentone dots and untouched paper highlights. Dissolve the background into floral bursts and floating bubbles, and stay strictly monochrome and hand-inked rather than photographic or colored.", "10-pop-art": "Render the image as a Roy Lichtenstein pop-art comic panel. Build it from brutally thick black contours and flat color limited to red, yellow, blue, black, and white. Cover broad areas with huge, regularly spaced Ben-Day dots that stay obviously mechanical. Crop in tight against a flat color field, and do not blend or gradate; the surface should look screenprinted.", "11-art-nouveau": "Render the image as an Alphonse Mucha Art Nouveau lithograph. Stretch the subject into a slender serpentine pose along a tall central axis, with a round ornamental halo behind the head and floral columns at both sides. Draw whiplash curves, flowing drapery, and botanical filigree in fine warm line. Fill flat muted fields of cream, blush, sage, copper, and gold, with delicate period stipple.", "12-van-gogh": "Render the image as a Vincent van Gogh post-impressionist oil of a night terrace beneath a turbulent sky. Cover every surface, figures and sky alike, with thick directional impasto and restless swirling contours. Set cobalt and chrome yellow against ultramarine, ochre, and copper so neighboring colors vibrate. Open the view wide: a steeply receding terrace, spiraling stars, and pools of yellow lamplight, all ridges of paint.", "13-stained-glass": "Render the image as a Gothic cathedral stained-glass window, viewed straight on. Assemble the subject from separate jewel-toned pieces of ruby, cobalt, emerald, amber, and gold, divided by thick irregular black lead. Fit the design inside a pointed lancet and a narrow stone border, with flattened medieval proportions and foliate tracery. Backlight rippled, bubbly glass so it glows, including small bubbles and uneven thickness.", "14-greek-pottery": "Render the image as ancient Greek black-figure painting on the curved belly of a terracotta vase. Band the scene with black meander borders. Render figures as black slip silhouettes in strict profile, each with one frontal almond eye, angular limbs, and fine incised interior detail. Stay almost entirely black on orange clay, with only tiny red and ochre notes, a little glaze sheen, and small worn scuffs.", "15-egyptian": "Render the image as an ancient Egyptian tomb painting on cracked plaster. Use composite view: head and legs in strict profile, one frontal eye, and a frontal torso showing both shoulders. Draw elongated figures in flat ochre, turquoise, rust, and black, with firm outlines and no shading or ground shadows. Frame the register with abstract bands of bars, lozenges, and zigzags, and leave pigment abraded on chalky plaster.", "16-cross-stitch": "Render the image as handmade cross-stitch on cream aida, photographed in a wooden hoop on a table. Reduce the subject to a low-resolution chart of roughly a dozen floss colors, with a blocky silhouette and an oversized round head. Make every colored mark a visible X stitch snapped to the fabric grid, and leave plenty of bare cloth. Show the wood ring, brass screw, rumpled weave, and soft side light.", "17-amigurumi": "Render the image as a crocheted amigurumi doll in soft product photography. Give it an enormous round yarn head, a tiny plump body, stubby limbs, and glossy safety-bead eyes, with features embroidered in thread. Build hair from coiled yarn and turn clothing and small props into crocheted pieces. Shoot close at a three-quarter angle on a blurred cream ground, with warm diffuse light, yarn fuzz, and visible stitches.", "18-low-poly": "Render the image as an extremely low-poly 3D diorama of fewer than five hundred polygons. Model the subject and the entire setting from large flat-shaded triangles, including faceted masses and blocky extremities, with no tiny detail. Use a high isometric view, lots of empty space, and clean peach, lavender, and mint light with simple ambient shadows. Do not smooth the mesh, add outlines, or apply realistic textures.", "19-vaporwave": "Render the image as 1980s vaporwave and synthwave album art. Pose the subject in monumental profile against a sliced chrome sunset, a violet sky, palm silhouettes, and broken white marble busts. Drop a glowing perspective grid across the floor toward a hard vanishing point. Use hot magenta and cyan airbrush color, then overlay VHS scanlines, color bleed, and horizontal glitch bands so the collage feels analog and artificial.", "20-rubber-hose": "Render the image as a 1930s rubber-hose cartoon in the Fleischer tradition, strictly black and white. Use giant pie-cut eyes, extremely bendy noodle limbs, oversized white gloves, bulbous shoes, and a tiny torso in a bouncing pose. Ink heavy hand-drawn contours and fill flat grays over a simple painted background, with squash and stretch. Add film grain, light scratches, and a soft vignette, and introduce no color.", "21-blueprint": "Render the image as an engineering blueprint in white line on saturated cyan grid paper. Place orthographic front and side views of the full subject side by side at matching scale, with no perspective. Add dashed hidden lines, projection guides, extension lines, and arrowed dimension marks on a fine square grid. Keep the sheet flat and unshaded, with wide technical margins and no realistic texture or portrait lighting.", "22-klimt": "Render the image as a Gustav Klimt golden-phase painting. Render the face and hands with naturalistic oil flesh, then flatten everything else into an expansive robe of spirals, eyes, rectangles, and jewel-colored inlays. Merge that garment with a gold-leaf mosaic background so ornament and figure share one shallow space. Keep the design asymmetrical, sensuous, and visibly gilded, with varied metallic texture rather than realistic depth.", "23-south-park": "Render the image as South Park construction-paper cutout animation. Give the subject a big flat circular head, round white eyes with dot pupils, a tiny squat body, stubby limbs, and mitten hands, assembled from simple geometric paper. Use solid unshaded colors and crude cut edges, with no gradients. Set the small figure in a wide snowy town of triangular evergreens, blocky buildings, and a pale sky, staged like limited television animation.", "24-line-art": "Render the image as a minimalist continuous-line drawing on plain off-white paper. Describe the whole subject and its immediate props with one thin unbroken black line, retracing instead of lifting the pen. Keep the figure small, elongated, and low on the sheet, surrounded by a large field of empty paper. Add only one colored mark, a single flat mustard shape, and no shading, hatching, or extra strokes.", "25-gothic-stop-motion": "Render the image as a Tim Burton-like gothic stop-motion puppet. Stretch the subject into spindly limbs, a narrow torso, an oversized head, huge dark-ringed eyes, and pale sculpted skin. Place it in a crooked miniature interior of curling beams and warped furniture. Hold the palette to black, white, and gray, letting one mustard accent be the only real color, under eerie theatrical light on a photographed set.", "26-wes-anderson": "Render the image as a Wes Anderson film still. Center the subject, facing the camera deadpan and upright, in a perfectly symmetrical one-point-perspective wide shot. Match the set on both sides in pastel pink and powder blue, with precise retro props and flat frontal light. Let the environment dominate the frame and keep the image ordered, geometric, and quietly filmic, like a designed tableau rather than a candid photo.", "27-rembrandt": "Render the image as a Rembrandt-style Dutch Golden Age oil portrait. Use a close three-quarter half-length pose against a near-black ground, lit by one warm source from the upper side. Model forms with deep chiaroscuro, soft lost edges, transparent umber and gold glazes, and a little impasto only where the light strikes. Carry fine craquelure and aged varnish across the paint, and show the picture without a frame.", "28-constructivist": "Render the image as a 1920s Soviet constructivist poster. Restrict the palette to vermilion, black, and aged cream, printed with photomontage halftone and rough letterpress texture. View the subject from a heroic low angle, cropped large along a strong diagonal among red wedges, black bars, and a bold circle. Vary the dot screens and let the ink slip slightly out of register, like cut paper rather than a straight photograph.", "29-byzantine-mosaic": "Render the image as a Byzantine church mosaic. Present the subject frontally, stiff and solemn, with a gold halo and a direct iconic gaze. Build the whole field, background included, from thousands of small glass and gold tesserae on a flat gold ground. Keep modeling minimal and the space shallow, so the surface glitters unevenly, each tile slightly irregular, like mosaic set into a wall rather than painted gold.", "30-illuminated-manuscript": "Render the image as a medieval illuminated manuscript on aged vellum. Draw the subject very small inside a large historiated initial, surrounded by burnished gold vine borders. Add marginal drolleries, including a snail and a rabbit, in mineral red, blue, and green with fine pen lines. Keep the scale intimate and the page handmade, with ornament crowding the margins the way a decorated book opening would.", "31-chalkboard": "Render the image as a colorful chalk drawing on a dark green blackboard. Use dusty, broken strokes, finger smudges, and a loose doodled touch, and show the chalk tray along the bottom edge. Let pigment look powdery and erasable, with ghost marks from earlier wiping. The board itself should read as a real photographed surface, matte and uneven, not ink, paint, or a digital brush.", "32-stencil-graffiti": "Render the image as Banksy-like stencil graffiti on a weathered brick wall. Cut the subject into a high-contrast black spray stencil of about two layers, with crisp edges, speckled overspray, and a few drips. Allow a single small red accent and nothing else in color. Photograph the wall straight on so mortar, stains, and chipped paint stay visible behind the graphic.", "33-surrealism": "Render the image as a Salvador Dalí surrealist oil painting. Model every form with smooth academic care, then betray that care: familiar things droop like soft wax, balance on thin crutches, or hang in midair. Open onto a vast dry coastal plain, where cobalt shadows rake toward a knife-sharp horizon beneath a blank burning sky. The paint stays glassy and controlled, strange because it is so lucid.", "34-mexican-folk": "Render the image as Mexican folk painting in the spirit of Frida Kahlo. Set the subject frontally, flat and unmodeled, crowded by monkeys, parrots, marigolds, and cacti that fill every gap. Use dark sure outlines and a hot palette of pink, green, and orange, with pattern instead of perspective. The picture should feel like a small devotional panel, symbolic and decorative rather than naturally lit.", "35-warhol": "Render the image as an Andy Warhol silkscreen. Repeat one portrait nine times in a three-by-three grid, each cell a different clash of flat neon color. Let the photo-derived contour slip slightly off the color blocks, the way a screen misregisters. Keep the inks flat, the contrast harsh, and the repetition industrial, so the sheet looks printed rather than painted.", "36-disney-2d": "Render the image as a 1990s Disney Renaissance animation frame. Draw the subject with large acting eyes, fluid tapered ink, and soft cel shading that suggests a held musical pose. Paint a deep romantic background, architectural and lush, in warm magical light that stays behind the crisp character line. Keep the figure hand-inked and the setting airbrushed gouache, the way a feature cel sits over a painted plate.", "37-cartoon-network": "Render the image as a 2010s Cartoon Network series in the shape language of Adventure Time. Simplify the subject to soft rounded forms, noodle arms, dot eyes, and a small body under thin even outlines. Fill with flat pastel color and almost no shade. Surround it with a candy-colored surreal landscape of blobs, odd plants, and gentle fields of pink, mint, and lavender.", "38-cyberpunk-anime": "Render the image as cyberpunk anime in the vein of Akira and Ghost in the Shell. Place the subject in a rainy neon megacity at night, among stacked signs, holograms, cables, and reflections on wet pavement. Use dense cel drawing, hard black shadows, and sharp rim light. Hold the color to magenta and teal against near-black, and merge sleek mechanical plating into the figure.", "39-victorian-engraving": "Render the image as a nineteenth-century steel engraving for an illustrated book. Build every tone from fine black cross-hatching and parallel lines on warm yellowed paper, with no wash and no gray paint. Use tight documentary detail and dense hatched shadow, as if the scene were lit by gas. Leave the paper color as the highlights so the sheet reads like an aged encyclopedia plate.", "40-art-deco": "Render the image as a 1920s Art Deco poster. Elongate the subject into a streamlined symmetrical figure, and reduce the silhouette to arcs, fans, and chevrons. Place a geometric sunburst or stepped skyline directly behind. Print in flat black, metallic gold, and emerald only, with razor edges and no modeled light, so the design feels like Jazz Age graphic glamour rather than a photograph.", "41-storybook-watercolor": "Render the image as a gentle children's picture-book illustration in watercolor and ink. Draw the subject small and rounded, with a light contour and translucent washes that puddle and bloom. Use a cozy, lightly indicated storybook setting rather than a detailed realistic place. Keep the palette warm and muted, the edges feathered, and the finish tender and handmade, never slick or sharply graphic.", "42-ligne-claire": "Render the image as a Franco-Belgian ligne claire panel in the manner of Hergé. Draw every form with clean black outlines of one even weight and fill them with flat bright color. Give the setting the same clear informational line as the subject, like a tidy European street in even daylight. Use no hatching, no gradients, and no cast shadows, and keep the image a single open panel.", "43-peanuts": "Render the image as a Peanuts daily comic in the style of Charles Schulz. Give the subject a large round head, a tiny body, dot eyes, and a small wobbly expression, drawn with slightly shaky ink. Leave the background nearly blank, with a short ground line at most. Color flat and sparingly so the figure feels spare and a little downcast, like newsprint rather than finished illustration.", "44-keith-haring": "Render the image as Keith Haring street drawing. Reduce the subject to a faceless figure in thick uniform black contour, filled with flat primary color and no shading. Send short motion dashes radiating around the pose so it reads as dancing. Keep the ground empty and graphic, like bold marker on a white wall, energetic and emblematic rather than anatomical.", "45-shadow-puppet": "Render the image as a Lotte Reiniger silhouette-film frame. Cut the subject in profile from black paper, with lace-like interior openings describing costume and detail. Layer ornate curling plants as separate paper shapes around it. Backlight everything against a smooth amber gradient so only the silhouette and its negative spaces remain, like photographed stop-motion papercut rather than a brushed drawing.", "46-voxel": "Render the image as a Minecraft-like voxel world. Build the subject entirely from cubes, with a block head carrying a low-resolution pixelated face, and build the setting from the same cubes. Use blurry sixteen-pixel textures, hard daylight, and strictly axis-aligned geometry. Frame a clean in-game view with no interface, and keep every form blocky rather than smooth, curved, or materially realistic.", "47-renaissance-fresco": "Render the image as a Sandro Botticelli early Renaissance tempera. Elongate the subject into a graceful, nearly weightless pose, with fine contours and drapery lifted by a light wind. Drift a few flowers through pale air and set classical arches behind. Use ivory and soft gold light, delicately modeled features, and a network of fine plaster cracks, like fresco rather than heavy oil.", "48-ink-wash": "Render the image as a traditional Chinese ink-wash landscape on raw rice paper. Reduce the subject to a tiny figure in a pavilion among misty mountains and pines, overwhelmed by empty paper. Use wet and dry black brushstrokes, with fog left as unpainted ground. Place one red seal as a small abstract stamp, and keep the mood spare, calligraphic, and atmospheric rather than tightly illustrated.", "49-toonstruck": "Render the image as a 1996 Toonstruck adventure-game screenshot. Keep the subject photoreal, like captured video, with a faint composite fringe where it meets the drawing. Surround that footage with a garish hand-drawn world of wobbly buildings, screaming saturated color, and a zany cartoon animal companion. Present it as mid-1990s full-motion video, including a plain cartoon inventory strip along the bottom.", "50-pointillism": "Render the image as a Georges Seurat pointillist painting. Make the entire picture, sky and figures alike, from tiny separate dots of pure color that mix only optically. Set a still Sunday park by a river, with parasols and calm, frozen poses. Keep the light luminous and the touch systematic, and do not blend strokes into smooth passages; the dots must stay individually visible.", "51-edward-hopper": "Render the image as an Edward Hopper oil painting with the mood of Nighthawks. Place a solitary subject at a diner counter at night, seen from the street through a broad plate-glass window. Use hard fluorescent light, simplified architectural slabs, and a restrained green and ochre palette. Leave the room slightly empty, and keep the handling quiet and monumental, melancholy rather than photographic.", "52-persian-miniature": "Render the image as a sixteenth-century Persian miniature. Stack garden, pavilion, patterned carpets, flowers, and slim cypress trees in flat tilted perspective, every detail equally sharp. Paint in fine opaque pigment of lapis, vermilion, and gold, with no cast shadows and no fade into depth. Close the scene inside a decorative border so the result feels intimate, patterned, and courtly, like pigment on paper.", "53-muppet": "Render the image as a Jim Henson Muppet on a television studio set. Rebuild the subject as a felt hand puppet: fuzzy fleece, a wide hinged mouth, large ping-pong-ball eyes, yarn hair, and simple stitched features. Sit it in a small practical interior under bright, even studio light. Show real fabric pile and the slight awkwardness of a performed puppet, not digital fur or a cartoon drawing.", "54-animal-crossing": "Render the image as an Animal Crossing-style cozy game view. Turn the subject into a villager with a huge rounded head, a tiny body, and simple marks for features, modeled in soft smooth 3D. Set a pastel island gathering place around it, with one gentle animal neighbor nearby, under a mildly tilted camera. Use even cheerful light and chunky friendly forms, and leave every interface element out.", "55-ps1-graphics": "Render the image as 1997 PlayStation 1 graphics. Reduce the subject to a very low-poly model skinned with blurry low-resolution textures that warp and swim under affine mapping. Light flatly, bury the distance in gray fog, and add pixelated edges, dither, and faint CRT scanlines. Keep polygons obviously angular and textures smeared, like a console screenshot from that hardware, not a modern stylized render.", "56-norman-rockwell": "Render the image as a Norman Rockwell illustration for The Saturday Evening Post. Paint a warm, humorous slice of everyday American life in careful realistic oil, with readable expressions and layered storytelling. Include one small comic mishap in the scene, observed rather than caricatured. Use soft indoor light and a cream paper margin around the picture, with the polished narrative finish of mid-century magazine art.", "57-moebius": "Render the image as science-fiction comic art by Moebius, Jean Giraud. Draw with fine even ink and flat pastel color, leaving large amounts of open air. Set the subject in a vast alien desert that also holds a floating structure and a few strange creatures on the horizon. Keep the line precise and the space dreamlike, without heavy shadow, screentone, or crowded borders.", "58-hanna-barbera": "Render the image as 1960s Hanna-Barbera limited animation. Use thick dark outlines, simple shapes, and flat color, with a pose that could be held on a reused cel. Paint a separate retro-futurist background of boomerang curves and pastel forms, in the spirit of The Jetsons. Imply motion only through the pose, and avoid gradients, texture, and the fuller rendering of later feature animation.", "59-kawaii-sticker": "Render the image as a kawaii die-cut vinyl sticker. Shrink the subject into a super-deformed figure with an oversized head, sparkling eyes, round blush, and tiny limbs, and give one nearby object a simple cute face. Surround the design with a thick white cut-line on a plain ground. Add a glossy plastic highlight so it reads as a printed sticker, not as a full illustrated scene.", "60-tarot-card": "Render the image as a vintage Rider-Waite tarot illustration. Present the subject as a symbolic figure in a shallow emblematic space, with a golden cup, small stars, and a crescent moon. Draw firm black outlines and mute flat color, like a hand-colored woodcut with almost no shading. Enclose the picture in an ornate rectangular border, formal and medieval, a card face rather than a naturalistic scene.", "61-linocut": "Render the image as a two-color linocut on handmade paper. Carve bold shapes with visible gouge tracks and print them in black over a single uneven mustard ink. Keep the forms high-contrast and graphic, with broken edges, slight misregistration, and the paper tooth showing through thin ink. Use no pencil shading and no third color, so the sheet looks pressed by hand rather than drawn.", "62-risograph": "Render the image as an indie risograph zine print. Use fluorescent pink and electric blue inks with translucent purple overprints, coarse stochastic grain, slight color misregistration, and patchy roller coverage on off-white paper. Reduce the subject to simple eccentric flat shapes with intentionally wobbly minimal linework and open paper. Let pink and blue dominate through obvious ink overlaps, with no photographic lighting or modeled volume.", "63-mid-century": "Render the image as a 1950s Mary Blair and UPA mid-century modern illustration. Paint whimsical textured gouache with dry-brush grain, broad matte color, and economical joyful marks. Flatten the subject into geometric shapes with childlike tilted perspective, long tapered limbs, and a tiny head inside crooked interlocking architecture. Pair coral, turquoise, plum, butter cream, and mustard in bold unusual combinations, without realistic shading.", "64-latte-art": "Render the image as hand-etched latte art photographed from directly overhead. Draw the subject only into the milk foam of a ceramic cup, using white microfoam, espresso crema, and fine dark-brown pin-tool strokes. Keep fluid marbling, tiny bubbles, and a creamy meniscus so the image reads as foam, not a printed picture. Fill most of the frame with the round surface and handle under soft window light.", "65-arcimboldo": "Render the image as a Giuseppe Arcimboldo Renaissance composite oil portrait. Assemble the whole figure from individually recognizable fruits, flowers, and vegetables so it reads at once as a still life and a person, with no ordinary skin. Use meticulous botanical detail, amber glazes, subtle craquelure, and sculptural chiaroscuro on a dark umber-black ground. Keep a tight half-length cabinet-painting crop and a calm, knowing expression.", "66-marble-statue": "Render the image as a classical white Carrara marble sculpture photographed in a quiet museum gallery. Carve a freestanding figure on a simple plain plinth, with pure unpainted stone, fine gray veining, polished highlights, and delicate chisel texture in the recesses. Use elegant contrapposto, a low three-quarter viewpoint, and directional spotlights that cast a clear shadow on warm-gray walls. It must read as carved marble, never a living person.", "67-fashion-illustration": "Render the image as a 1950s haute couture fashion illustration in the Vogue editorial manner. Draw a dramatically elongated figure with a small head, impossibly long legs, a tiny waist, and a sweeping diagonal pose on mostly untouched white paper. Use spontaneous India-ink contours, dry-brush gestures, and sparse translucent marker washes in black, gray, and one muted accent. Leave passages unfinished, with bleeding edges and generous asymmetrical space.", "68-tattoo-flash": "Render the image as vintage American traditional tattoo flash on aged ivory paper. Paint the subject as a compact, readable pin-up motif with heavy black outlines, boldly simplified shapes, and solid fills of saturated red, dark green, and golden yellow. Add whip shading, small areas of bare paper, and slight ink irregularity. Surround the main design with clearly spaced classic flash motifs such as roses and a swallow, flat and graphic rather than realistically shaded.", "69-neon-sign": "Render the image as a handmade neon-tube sign photographed at night on a dark, rain-damp brick wall. Reduce the subject to economical bent-glass contours in restrained pink and yellow, with visible bends, standoff clips, and discreet wires. Let luminous bloom and pink-gold reflections pool in the wet mortar. Frame the whole sign obliquely from street level, with generous dark brick around it, so the subject exists only as glowing tubes.", "70-kirby-comic": "Render the image as a 1960s Jack Kirby Silver Age superhero comic panel. Push the subject into monumental, angular, blocky anatomy with extreme foreshortening, a huge foreground hand, and a kinetic twisting pose. Frame it with dense black cosmic energy crackle, concentric bursts, and geometric machinery. Ink with heavy spotted blacks and hard highlights, then print in flat saturated color with coarse halftone dots and slight misregistration on warm pulp paper.", "71-pop-up-book": "Render the image as a photographed children's pop-up book opened on a table. Engineer the scene from layered folding cardstock with V-folds, slots, glue tabs, scored creases, and blank pull tabs rising from the gutter. Render the subject as a flat printed paper cutout among pleated furniture, with whimsical illustrated shapes rather than realistic volume. Use matte mint, cream, peach, and warm yellow stock, visible cut edges, and soft shadows that prove it is paper.", "72-muralism": "Render the image as a Diego Rivera Mexican muralist fresco on a large plaster wall. Give figures monumental rounded forms, broad powerful hands, cylindrical limbs, simplified faces, and weighty postures in shallow compressed perspective. Build a communal panorama of dignified labor behind the main subject, interlocking people and architecture across the wall. Paint with earthy terracotta, ochre, muted deep green, and plaster cream, mineral pigment in a dry matte surface.", "73-vga-adventure": "Render the image as early-1990s LucasArts VGA point-and-click adventure art in the hand-painted language of Monkey Island 2. Use coarse pixel clusters, a limited indexed palette, checkerboard dithering, and hard edges. Let a moody, crooked environment dominate while the subject stays a small full-body sprite, roughly one-sixth of the frame. Translate painterly shapes into low-resolution game pixels, never smooth modern illustration.", "74-cel-shaded": "Render the image as a cel-shaded 3D game still in the manner of Zelda: The Wind Waker. Use bold simplified geometry, an enormous head on a tiny body, huge expressive toon eyes, and mittenlike hands. Shade with crisp two-tone cel shadows and flat saturated color, with no realistic materials. Set a low playful camera against open cyan water, stylized island buildings, and bold spiraling clouds in turquoise, cream, and sea green.", "75-rotoscope": "Render the image as a rotoscoped animation frame in the language of A Scanner Darkly and Waking Life. Keep observed, unglamorous live-action gesture, but rebuild every surface from flat irregular color islands and slightly doubled, unstable hand-traced contours. Let outlines wobble, planes swim, and perspective drift, with angular painterly blocks inside forms and objects. Use uneasy fluorescent lime, dusty mauve, faded teal, and warm ochre, never smooth photoreal texture.", "76-spider-verse": "Render the image as a Spider-Verse stylized 3D animated film frame. Combine sculptural volume with hand-inked comic rendering: halftone dots, coarse screen-print shadows, bold selective contours, sketchy hatching, and deliberate cyan and magenta registration offsets. Use a high oblique camera, strong Dutch tilt, and dramatic foreshortening in a graffiti-colored city at dusk. Keep electric coral, magenta, cyan, violet, and inky navy, with stepped graphic highlights rather than smooth shading.", "77-arcane": "Render the image as an Arcane painterly 3D animation frame in the Fortiche style. Sculpt angular planes and subtly exaggerated expressive anatomy, with hand-painted brush textures baked into the models and graphic brushwork in the shadows. Use an intimate, slightly low cinematic close-up against steampunk-industrial ironwork, pipes, and smoky depth. Light with moody deep teal shadows, rich amber glow, and a hard amber rim.", "78-sin-city": "Render the image as a Sin City graphic-novel panel in uncompromising noir ink. Use only pure solid black and pure solid white, with no gray or halftones, plus one flat spot color on a single motif. Carve bold silhouettes and hard white negative space, and render rain as sharp white streaks. Keep angular features, a high diagonal view, and large areas of empty black.", "79-crayon-drawing": "Render the image as a drawing by a six-year-old with wax crayons on slightly off-white paper. Use a huge round head, a tiny rectangular body, stick limbs, mismatched sizes, and no perspective. Draw wobbly hesitant contours with uneven pressure, chunky broken strokes, and scribbled color that strays far outside the lines, leaving paper gaps. Add a smiling sun in a corner, a ground line, and visible wax buildup from a bright limited crayon box.", "80-etch-a-sketch": "Render the image as a drawing on a real red Etch A Sketch, photographed on a table. Show the whole toy: a glossy red frame, a silver-gray powder screen, and two white knobs along the bottom. Build the picture from one continuous thin gray line, with travel lines linking every part and stair-stepped right angles wherever a curve or diagonal should be. Keep it schematic and slightly clumsy, with no fills, shading, or color on the screen.", "81-kodachrome": "Render the image as a candid 1970s Kodachrome color-slide snapshot. Use warm saturated reds and yellows, rich shadows, fine organic film grain, slightly imperfect framing, and a subtle light leak along one edge. Place the subject in a period interior with chrome trim and window light, caught mid-gesture rather than posed. It should feel like an actual family slide, not a modern photograph with a retro filter.", "82-tintype": "Render the image as an 1860s wet-plate tintype on aged metal, in silvery monochrome only. Pose the subject stiffly upright in a Victorian parlor, with long-exposure stillness, shallow focus, and softly blurred surroundings. Add a deep dark vignette, irregular chemical flow marks, and silver collodion artifacts crowded at the plate edges. Keep restrained midtones, rich darks, and a real metal-plate surface, with no sepia toning.", "83-double-exposure": "Render the image as an artistic photographic double exposure on a clean white ground. Fill a large profile silhouette with a second scene, such as a misty evergreen forest, so trees and fog rise through the shape and edges dissolve. Keep generous white negative space, delicate lost edges, and layered charcoal and cool mist with a few warm accents. Make it one seamless exposure, not pasted rectangles or a filtered photo.", "84-perler-beads": "Render the image as handmade fused Perler bead art photographed straight down on its pegboard. Build every shape from cylindrical plastic fuse beads on a regular grid, with visible round rims, center holes, slightly softened joins, and glossy highlights. Use blocky stylized proportions, a large friendly face, and a limited solid bead palette with no gradients. Leave a small border of empty pegboard under soft craft-table light.", "85-pompeii-fresco": "Render the image as an ancient Roman Pompeii fresco covering the whole frame. Paint on Pompeian red with slender trompe-l'oeil columns and niches, mineral pigments sunk into lime plaster, irregular cracks, and patches of missing plaster. Keep the figure small inside one architectural bay, with flowing contours, restrained earth-pigment modeling, and incomplete surviving details. The surface should be matte and abraded, an edge-to-edge wall fragment rather than oil paint.", "86-aztec-codex": "Render the image as a pre-Columbian Aztec codex painting on warm tan bark paper. Draw a flat profile figure with an oversized angular head, compact folded limbs, and very simplified hands, seated without optical perspective or cast light. Use bold black outlines and flat blocks of turquoise, red, and ochre, plus a few invented nonlinguistic geometric ornaments. Show fibrous paper and slight pigment abrasion throughout.", "87-madhubani": "Render the image as an Indian Madhubani folk painting in bright natural pigments and fine black ink. Outline everything with double lines and fill them with hatching, dots, and parallel strokes, leaving no empty space. Flatten the figure with large almond eyes and ornamental, symbolic proportions, and pack fish, birds, flowers, and leaves into every gap. Use saffron, vermilion, leaf green, deep blue, and black, with no gradients, shadows, or realistic perspective.", "88-delft-tile": "Render the image as a hand-painted Delft blue ceramic tile panel. Use cobalt line and transparent washes on milky white tin glaze, with small floral corner ornaments, slight craquelure, and a discreet glossy shine. Spread a continuous Dutch landscape of windmill, canal, low houses, and clouds across a grid of tiles separated by uneven pale grout. Translate every color into blue on white, and keep the subject modest within the scenery.", "89-origami": "Render the image as a handcrafted origami diorama photographed in soft studio light. Fold the entire subject and setting from crisp colored paper, with strong mountain and valley creases, sharp edges, and exposed paper margins. Let origami geometry set the proportions through faceted planes and simplified angular forms. Surround the small sculpture with pale empty space and soft cast shadows so it reads as a real paper object.", "90-quilling": "Render the image as paper-quilling art photographed in raking macro light. Build the whole picture from narrow colored strips rolled, coiled, and glued upright on their edges to an ivory support. Use varied coil diameters, tight spirals for dense areas, and flowing edge-on contours, with fine shadows under every raised strip. Simplify the subject into ornamental profile shapes rather than realistic surfaces, and build nearby objects from the same scrollwork.", "91-graffiti-mural": "Render the image as a vibrant spray-paint graffiti character mural on rough concrete. Use b-boy cartoon proportions: an oversized head, bent legs, and huge foreshortened sneakers in a cocky crouch. Paint electric cyan, hot pink, orange, lime, and purple fades, thick black outlines, white shine marks, overspray, drips, and abstract angular energy shapes. Show a narrow strip of real pavement at the base so the figure stays flat paint on the wall.", "92-monet": "Render the image as a Claude Monet impressionist oil painting. Break the subject and a spacious garden into loose dabbed brushstrokes, with dissolved edges, luminous color mixing, and visible canvas texture. Let shimmering outdoor light dominate: soft lilac shadows, greens, and reflected water, with a lily pond and a curved Japanese footbridge beyond. Integrate the figure into the atmosphere as part of the landscape, not a sharp isolated portrait.", "93-munch": "Render the image as an Edvard Munch expressionist oil painting in the visual language of The Scream. Stretch the figure into an anxious, elongated shape with long hands and shoulders curling inward on a wavy distorted bridge. Swirl a blood-orange and vermilion sky over a dark fjord, with flat blue-green shadows and thick sinuous brushwork. Let emotion warp anatomy and perspective rather than describe a real place.", "94-magritte": "Render the image as a René Magritte surrealist oil painting. Use immaculate, smooth oil surfaces and matter-of-fact realism for impossible events. Hide the face behind one large floating everyday object, and let bowler-hatted figures in dark suits rain upright through a cerulean sky of distinct fluffy clouds. Keep natural proportions, cool daylight, a frontal composition, and a low stone parapet, calm rather than cartooned.", "95-mondrian": "Render the image as a Piet Mondrian De Stijl neoplasticist painting. Dissolve the subject almost completely into an asymmetric grid of straight black horizontal and vertical lines of varying weight, with pure red, yellow, and blue rectangles on large white fields. Suggest only a faint trace through rectangular relationships, using no curves, perspective, or modeled objects. Keep flat oil-on-canvas geometry in red, yellow, blue, black, and white alone.", "96-frazetta": "Render the image as a 1970s Frank Frazetta fantasy paperback oil painting. Exaggerate heroic musculature in a twisting contrapposto, seen from a low viewpoint in a sweeping triangular pose on a jagged summit under a ferocious sky. Use burnt umber and smoky violet shadows, with blazing amber striking form and battered armor. Paint with bravura oil brushwork and edges lost in atmospheric darkness, without digital gloss.", "97-gta-loading-screen": "Render the image as Grand Theft Auto loading-screen art. Draw bold cel-shaded illustration with heavy black outlines, stylized adult realism, hard-edged shadow blocks, and exaggerated graphic shapes. Use a dramatic low-angle waist-up view against silhouetted palms, low buildings, and a saturated orange-pink Los Angeles sunset. Keep it genuinely drawn game art in a single uninterrupted scene, not a filtered photograph.", "98-seuss": "Render the image as a Dr. Seuss-like whimsical children's book illustration, with eccentric pen-and-ink contours, wiry scratchy hatching, and flat red, blue, and yellow washes on warm cream paper. Stretch the subject into a spindly cartoon with very long thin limbs, a towering wobbly striped hat, and absurd proportions. Surround it with curving impossible architecture, tufted spindly trees, and playful winding perspective, leaving generous paper showing.", "99-cave-painting": "Render the image as a prehistoric Lascaux cave painting on rough rock. Use ochre and charcoal stick figures and animals, pigment-blown handprints, scraped and smudged mineral color, and broad areas of bare stone. Keep human forms schematic and small beside larger beasts, with no clean outlines or modern cartoon anatomy. Warm the wall with dim off-frame torchlight so cracks and grain stay visible.", "100-snow-globe": "Render the image as a glass snow globe on a turned wooden base, photographed as a product. Shrink the subject into a tiny hand-painted figurine inside a detailed miniature diorama, with fine white flakes swirling in the liquid. Show the whole sphere and base from slightly above, with thick curved-glass reflections, edge refraction, and sparkling highlights. Blur a cozy festive background into warm bokeh so the crafted miniature stays the focus.", "101-game-boy": "Render the image as a photograph of an original 1989 Game Boy whose screen shows the subject. Render the on-screen scene as crude four-shade green-gray pixel art with a visible LCD grid, hard edges, and a tiny sprite inside a wider game environment. Photograph the tall off-white shell, dark bezel, cross-shaped pad, and two round buttons lying on a table. Keep the shell in natural light and the screen a dim reflective LCD.", "102-ascii-art": "Render the image as green-on-black ASCII art glowing on a CRT. Construct the subject only from a fixed grid of monospace marks such as @, #, %, *, +, =, and periods, with denser glyphs for darker tones. Use phosphor green, fine horizontal scanlines, slight bloom, and curved black screen corners. The characters themselves are the picture, awkward and abstract, with no illustrated contours underneath.", "103-balloon-animal": "Render the image as a twisted balloon sculpture photographed at a kids' party. Build the whole subject from glossy inflated latex, with pinch twists, knots, tied ends, and specular highlights, in a toy-like anatomy of tubes and loops. Use a playful low angle that shows the full figure and a small balloon prop beside it. Keep saturated party colors, with soft-focus balloons and bunting behind, never a living person made shiny.", "104-fondant-cake": "Render the image as a sculpted fondant cake topper on a tiered celebration cake, shot as bakery photography. Give the figure sweet sugar-paste proportions: a rounded oversized head, a tiny torso, and short limbs, with smooth matte paste, piped icing detail, and a little edible glitter. Show the upper tiers and scalloped borders in shallow depth of field under soft window light. Everything should read as edible craft, not a living person.", "105-bob-ross": "Render the image as a Bob Ross wet-on-wet oil landscape. Make the scenery the point: snowy mountains with palette-knife highlights, a calm lake, a wooden cabin, and happy little evergreens built from layered fan-brush strokes. Keep the subject a tiny mark on the porch or shore, subordinate to the vast view, beneath a soft blended sky. Use forest green, icy blue, and warm timber, with confident wet oil rather than photographic detail.", "106-city-pop": "Render the image as a 1980s Japanese city pop album-cover illustration. Use pastel airbrush gradients, immaculate graphic shapes, glossy lacquer reflections, and a clean retro anime face with stylized proportions. Set a spare summer scene of a mint convertible, palm silhouettes, and a turquoise-to-lavender ocean under a peach sunset. Keep soft coral, mint, lavender, and cream, with crisp edges and a few chrome glints.", "107-bosch": "Render the image as a Hieronymus Bosch panel from a fantastical triptych, late-medieval oil on wood. Crowd a high-horizon garden with tiny fully dressed figures, bizarre hybrid creatures, giant strawberries, and enormous vessels used as boats or towers. Shift scale wildly and paint meticulous detail across the whole surface with translucent glazes and flattened medieval space. Use earthy green meadows, ochre paths, deep red fruit, and pale distant hills on subtly aged panel.", "108-quentin-blake": "Render the image as a Quentin Blake loose pen-and-ink illustration with light watercolor. Draw a gangly, energetic figure with long bent limbs, spare features, and a flailing asymmetrical pose, surrounded by lots of untouched white paper. Use searching broken strokes, quick scribbles, and transparent washes in ochre, watery brown, and a hint of gray-blue. Keep the setting very sparse and the mood spontaneous, like a children's book sketch rather than a finished rendering.", "109-steadman": "Render the image as a Ralph Steadman gonzo ink illustration. Attack the page with wild black splatters, enormous blots, frantic scratchy lines, and savage angular distortion, with bursts of red and yellow on off-white paper. Stretch the subject into an off-kilter caricature with a jutting profile and spidery extremities in a violently asymmetric scene. Leave aggressive negative space and uncontrolled marks, raw ink rather than a polished cartoon.", "110-3d-emoji": "Render the image as a glossy 3D emoji avatar in a Memoji-like style. Use an enormous rounded head, smooth shiny plastic skin, tiny toy-like shoulders, simple features, and a playful wink on a plain soft gradient. Mold chunky sculpted forms with clean highlights and soft ambient occlusion, isolated with no setting. Keep the render rounded and plastic, with no pores, realistic skin, or photographic lighting.", "111-bauhaus": "Render the image as Bauhaus geometric poster art. Construct the subject only from flat circles, semicircles, and rectangles in red, blue, yellow, and black on a cream ground. Hold a strict modular grid, strong asymmetrical balance, and large areas of open paper. Print with flat lithographic edges and a hint of ink on paper, with no shading and no modeled depth.", "112-rosemaling": "Render the image as Scandinavian folk painting on a wooden panel, mixing Norwegian rosemaling with Swedish Dala decoration. Surround a small flat stylized figure with sweeping C- and S-shaped acanthus scrolls, tulips, and teardrop leaves in red, blue, and ochre on a deep blue ground. Use naive proportions and patterned flat shapes with no realistic perspective, so the figure echoes the scrollwork. Show wood grain, opaque paint, and worn edges, viewed straight on.", "113-batik": "Render the image as Indonesian hand-drawn batik, wax-resist dyed cotton seen flat. Integrate an elongated stylized figure into diagonal parang bands and flowering motifs, using indigo, soga brown, ecru, and muted ochre dyes. Show woven fibers, uneven wax outlines, dye bleed, and fine crackle veining across the whole cloth. Keep the forms ornamental and flattened, with intricate repeated fills and hand-waxed line rather than smooth vector art.", "114-marionette": "Render the image as a hand-carved wooden marionette on a miniature puppet-theater stage. Show the full jointed figure hanging from taut strings, with visible wood grain, worn paint, ball-and-pin joints, and a slightly oversized carved head. Frame it in an aged proscenium with gathered velvet curtains and warm amber spotlights. It must read as an articulated puppet, not a living performer in costume.", "115-sand-sculpture": "Render the image as a monumental beach sand sculpture at a festival. Carve the whole subject and its props from packed sand, with deep relief, crisp chisel-like edges, fragile crumbs at the base, and countless visible grains in natural ochre. Use a wide low view that includes beach, sea, and bright sky, plus a plain tape barrier on stakes to show scale. Let raking sunlight cast hard carved shadows.", "116-ice-sculpture": "Render the image as a crystal-clear ice sculpture at a nighttime winter festival. Carve substantial faceted forms with chisel marks, internal bubbles, fine fractures, and frost in the recesses, all optically clear ice. Backlight it with electric blue so the edges glow cyan against a dark navy sky, allowing only subtle warm refraction inside. Keep a close three-quarter view and blurred festival lights, never skin with an ice filter.", "117-courtroom-sketch": "Render the image as a hurried courtroom sketch in pastel and marker on toothy paper. Use coarse broken black contours, smudged chalk, unfinished edges, sparse faces, and searching strokes, as if drawn in minutes during a hearing. Place the main subject in the witness area and suggest the judge and lawyers with only a few loose marks. Leave plenty of pale paper, with broad russet and ochre notes for the woodwork.", "118-ikea-manual": "Render the image as an IKEA-style flat-pack assembly manual illustration. Use only uniform black line on white paper, a friendly bulbous head, stubby limbs, and extremely economical contours. Lay out the subject assembling an object in two sequential diagrams, with exploded parts, an Allen key, and simple curved and straight arrows. Draw the furniture in clean axonometric projection, with lots of white space and no shading.", "119-safety-card": "Render the image as a 1990s airline safety-card illustration. Arrange a few bordered panels of the same simplified seated figure, using bold arrows to show one clear physical action. Draw smooth economical navy outlines, slightly awkward anatomy, flat muted teal, brick, and cream, and only minimal two-tone shading. Keep the poses geometric and legible, like printed instructional art rather than a photograph.", "120-sims-isometric": "Render the image as a The Sims 1 isometric life-sim screenshot from about 2000. Use a fixed overhead orthographic view of a whole room with cutaway walls, a square tile grid, and pre-rendered low-polygon characters. Give the subject a small stiff sprite, chunky raster edges, and baked low-resolution textures, with a bright green faceted plumbob floating above the head. Keep turn-of-the-millennium lighting, without modern gloss or a game interface.", "121-bollywood-poster": "Render the image as a hand-painted 1970s Bollywood movie poster. Use a dramatic diagonal montage: an enormous melodramatic close-up and a smaller action vignette, with heroic foreshortening and larger-than-life features. Paint saturated orange light against deep cobalt and ultramarine, energetic bursts, visible bristle strokes, and imperfect edges on aged paper grain. Fill the sheet as finished poster art, expressive and theatrical rather than photographic.", "122-polish-poster": "Render the image as a 1960s Polish Poster School painting in textured gouache. Reduce the scene to a surreal symbolic metaphor with radically distorted scale, an off-balance figure, and abundant active negative space. Use large flat fields of dirty ivory, black, rusty red, and mustard, with blunt spontaneous brushwork, scumbled pigment, and coarse dry brush on rough paper. Keep the image odd and poetic, not a cute cartoon or a realistic setting.", "123-bayeux-tapestry": "Render the image as a cropped section of the Bayeux Tapestry, a medieval embroidered narrative linen. Use unbleached oat-colored cloth, couched and stem-stitched wool in muted madder rust, ochre, olive, and indigo, with flat filled thread shapes and slight historic irregularities. Lay a long horizontal story band between wide upper and lower borders of stylized animals, plants, and geometric motifs. Figures stay elongated, in profile, and unmodeled, with angular elbows and large simple outlined eyes.", "124-shinkai": "Render the image as a Makoto Shinkai anime film frame. Paint a hyper-detailed luminous sky of towering summer clouds that dominates the view, with radiant lens flare, turquoise and cobalt atmosphere, and glistening reflected light. Draw the subject with graceful anime proportions, delicate features, crisp restrained linework, and cel-shaded color set into meticulously painted scenery. Keep a quiet emotional pause, strong perspective from overhead wires or architecture, and warm sunlit depth rather than a photograph with a filter.", "125-horror-manga": "Render the image as a Junji Ito-like horror manga panel in pure black ink on white paper. Use obsessive meticulous hatching, extreme black masses, and fine pen work, with no color and no photographic base. Tighten into an unnervingly calm close view while forms multiply into impossible labyrinthine spirals that warp the surrounding space. Keep rigid stillness against frantic crosshatching, a quiet psychological dread rather than graphic gore or a filtered photo.", "126-newsprint": "Render the image as a 1920s newspaper halftone photograph printed on yellowed newsprint. Use coarse irregular black halftone dots, soft blurry grain, ink bleed into absorbent aged paper, and worn inexpensive press degradation. Stage a candid flapper-era moment in flat available daylight, with period interior detail and an unposed medium-wide side view. Translate every color into monochrome ink on yellowed paper so it reads as an actual old printed press photo, not a crisp modern picture.", "127-urban-sketching": "Render the image as on-location urban sketching across an open sketchbook, with the spine and center gutter running down the middle. Draw loose energetic fineliner contours, wobbly searching lines, broken perspective, selective detail, and unfinished edges, then lay transparent watercolor washes with pigment pooling and white paper reserves. Keep a wide street view where architecture leads diagonally into depth and the subject stays a small figure inside the place. It should feel like a spontaneous drawn journal spread, never a photograph with a filter.", "128-sargent": "Render the image as a John Singer Sargent bravura oil portrait. Use loose supremely confident brushwork, large economical planes, lost and found edges, and a deep dark atmospheric background. Set an elegant seated three-quarter-length figure on a diagonal, head turned into the light, with painterly highlights on reflective surfaces. Keep a restrained palette of ochre, amber, cream, umber, and near-black so the canvas reads as masterful traditional oil, never a filtered photograph.", "129-south-park-coffee-shop": "Render the image as a South Park construction-paper cutout animation frame inside the town's cozy shop. Use a flat circle head, simple white eyes with dot pupils, mitten hands, stubby legs, and paper-puppet proportions, with flat color layers and slightly jittery cut edges. Stage a wide straight-on full-body view among blocky furniture, a counter, and a simple window. Keep everything crude, flat, and comedic, with no realistic anatomy, photographic detail, or rendered lighting.", "130-family-guy": "Render the image as a Family Guy television cartoon frame. Use a round head with a prominent projecting chin, large white oval eyes, tiny pupils, clean uniform black outlines, and very simple flat cel colors. Frame a waist-up three-quarter view in a pastel suburban Quahog kitchen, with a casual lean, a dry deadpan expression, and static eye-level sitcom staging. It should look like native hand-drawn 2D production art, without realistic proportions, gradients, or photographic texture.", "131-bobs-burgers": "Render the image as a Bob's Burgers cartoon animation frame. Use rounded blobby long faces, tiny black dot eyes, thin slightly wobbly outlines, muted pastel flat colors, and awkward charming proportions with understated expressions. Seat the figure in profile along a receding diner counter, long nose in silhouette, shoulders hunched, with modest wall details and simple diner equipment behind. Keep it native 2D television animation, mundane and warm, without realistic anatomy or rendered light.", "132-rick-and-morty": "Render the image as a Rick and Morty cartoon animation frame. Use an exaggerated tall head, wide white eyes with wobbly irregular pupils and no spirals, a slightly drooly grin, thin jittery black lines, and flat cel colors. Set a tight low three-quarter close view in an alien sci-fi cafe, with a vivid green portal, asymmetrical fixtures, and a purple skyline behind. Contrast acid green, cyan, and purple against the subject, as distorted adult 2D cartoon design rather than realistic lighting.", "133-king-of-the-hill": "Render the image as a King of the Hill cartoon animation frame. Use believable slightly awkward everyday proportions, understated features, clean simple thin ink contours, flat restrained earthy fills, and almost no shading. Place a full-body figure off center in a Texas suburban alley at dusk, beside a weathered fence, with ranch roofs, utility poles, patchy grass, and a muted lavender sky. Keep the mood low-key and ordinary, a hand-drawn television cel, not glossy, dimensional, or photographic.", "134-archer": "Render the image as an Archer television animation frame. Use thick uniform dark outlines, mid-century realistic adult proportions, angular elegantly modeled faces, and flat color with exactly one sharp shadow tone per surface. Compose a slightly low cinematic medium shot in a sleek retro spy-agency office of walnut paneling, venetian-blind shadows, and geometric glass. Hold a restrained self-assured mood in cream, charcoal, deep petrol, and warm wood, with no gradients or photographic realism.", "135-daria": "Render the image as late-1990s MTV animation in the manner of Daria. Use flattened deadpan faces, very heavy-lidded eyes, angular awkward contours, flat muted colors, and economical low-budget cel construction. Frame a tight frontal close crop in a drab suburban pizza parlor, slouched in a vinyl booth, with a tiny dry half-smile and almost no shading. Keep the palette dusty: rust, mauve, dingy cream, and desaturated green, sardonic and unglamorous rather than glossy.", "136-powerpuff": "Render the image as classic Powerpuff Girls animation. Use an enormous circular head, huge oval eyes taking up half the face, a tiny body, stub legs, fingerless rounded nub hands, and extra-thick crisp black outlines. Send the figure flying on a diagonal above a simple geometric skyline under a solid bright cyan sky, with candy-pink, lavender, and white cloud shapes. Fill every shape with opaque flat cel color, no gradients, texture, grain, or realistic anatomy.", "137-gravity-falls": "Render the image as a Gravity Falls cartoon frame. Use thick clean expressive outlines, big round eyes, a small simple body, and rubbery spaghetti limbs, with flat cel fills over a graphic painted background. Set a wide oblique view inside a quirky woodland diner of crooked timber, warm forest greens, and woody browns, with an enormous window onto tangled Pacific Northwest trees. Keep the humor warm and slightly mysterious, never photoreal or dimensional.", "138-dragon-ball": "Render the image as a 1990s Dragon Ball Z animation cel over a painted rocky wasteland. Use aggressive contour lines, sharp angular eyes, sculptural stylized faces, heroic exaggerated anatomy, and hard two-tone cel shadows. Add radiating speed lines and a crackling explosive golden ki aura, with the powered-up form swept into bright gold spikes. Crop a low-angle chest-up heroic view so the aura and cliffs dominate, flat painted energy rather than photoreal rendering or dimensional models.", "139-jojo": "Render the image as a JoJo's Bizarre Adventure color manga illustration. Use extreme fashion posing, elongated proportions, sharply chiseled features, emphatic ink contours, dense crosshatch, and halftone accents in fuchsia, acid green, violet, and cyan. Fill the frame with a full-body diagonal contrapposto from a high oblique view, warped perspective, and alternating psychedelic planes. Scatter abstract angular strokes that only suggest sound-effect rhythm, so the result stays ink-and-color manga rather than a photograph.", "140-over-the-garden-wall": "Render the image as an Over the Garden Wall animation frame. Draw compact old-fashioned cartoon figures with small faces, spare ink outlines, and modest dot eyes, set in a richly hand-painted muted gouache landscape. Use autumnal nineteenth-century Americana: pumpkin fields, bare branches, woodland fog, and a palette of dead-leaf ochre, moss gray, dull russet, and deep brown. Keep the figure small in a wide pastoral view, still, wistful, and antique rather than glossy.", "141-looney-tunes": "Render the image as a 1950s Warner Brothers Looney Tunes theatrical cartoon in the Chuck Jones manner. Use hand-inked cels over a dry-brush painted desert of angular mesas and sparse cacti, with bold graphic silhouettes and comedic negative space. Stage an extreme squash-and-stretch double take: elastic torso, rubber-hose limbs, and eyes popping outward. Let mid-century theatrical color and cartoon acting decide every proportion, never a photograph with a filter.", "142-ren-stimpy": "Render the image as a Ren and Stimpy gross-out 1990s cartoon close-up. Paint a hyper-detailed shock insert that contrasts with ordinary flat animation: bulging uneven eyes, visible capillaries, huge pores, oily skin, tiny hairs, sweat, and warped anatomy. Crowd the frame with a skewed enormous face in sickly yellow, pink, olive, and violet, modeled like airbrushed gouache. Keep it repulsive but funny, meticulously painted cartoon texture rather than photographic realism or injury.", "143-bojack": "Render the image as a BoJack Horseman adult television animation frame. Use flat muted pastel fills, thin clean outlines, simple shapes, stiff restrained poses, and almost no shading. Set a wide architectural view inside a modern Hollywood Hills house, large windows opening onto sun-bleached hills and palms, with an anthropomorphic animal character nearby in the same graphic language. Hold a wry melancholy in dusty teal, peach, mauve, and tan, understated rather than realistic.", "144-spongebob": "Render the image as a SpongeBob SquarePants underwater cartoon frame in Bikini Bottom. Use bright tropical cel colors, rubbery elastic anatomy, lively clean outlines, and painted scenery with flower-shaped clouds and iridescent bubbles. Stage a playful high-angle view over a sandy path toward fanciful seabed buildings, with saturated turquoise distance and lime and coral accents. Let bent perspective and exuberant cartoon acting decide the pose, with clusters of translucent bubbles sweeping the corners.", "145-robot-chicken": "Render the image as a Robot Chicken-like stop-motion frame of cheap articulated action figures on a handmade miniature set. Show visible hinge and ball joints, mold seams, rigid gripping hands, and awkward toy articulation, with real fabric or plastic surfaces at toy scale. Shoot from a low, close, slightly tilted handheld angle across a crude cardboard interior and mismatched miniature furniture. Use practical stage light and shallow miniature focus, irreverent adult stop-motion rather than polished CGI.", "146-gilliam-cutout": "Render the image as Monty Python cutout animation in the Terry Gilliam manner. Collage Victorian engravings and scissor-cut silhouettes with mismatched scales, flat hinged paper limbs, sepia hatch texture, and sudden hand-tinted vermilion, turquoise, and ochre. Include an absurd mechanical contraption and a gigantic engraved foot descending from above, with exploded gears floating in broad paper fields. Keep stage-flat layering, crisp cut edges, and extreme disproportion, with no photographic depth.", "147-yellow-submarine": "Render the image as a 1968 Yellow Submarine-like psychedelic animation frame, with Peter Max pop color. Use flat pop-art shapes, elastic ornamental anatomy, bold ink contours, and irregular hand-painted cel edges in electric orange, fuchsia, acid yellow, emerald, and cobalt. Float the subject on a diagonal through dense trippy flowers, paisley swirls, wavy checkers, and rainbow bands. Allow selective airbrushed rainbow gradients inside graphic shapes, with no realistic depth or photographic anatomy.", "148-fantastic-planet": "Render the image as René Laloux's Fantastic Planet from 1973. Use fine pen-hatched cutout animation, delicate crosshatching, flat layered paper silhouettes, and a muted palette of ochre, sage, dusty rose, and slate blue. Set a small foreground figure against a vast alien ecology of bulbous plants and organic arches, with enormous blue giants standing far off like silent hills. Keep an eerie calm, dry paper grain, and restrained wash, not generic fantasy concept art.", "149-borderlands": "Render the image as Borderlands video-game art. Use bold cel-shaded shapes, very thick irregular hand-inked black outlines, angular exaggerated anatomy, etched crosshatch shadows, and distressed gritty comic texture. Set a low wide view along a dusty wasteland bar of rusted corrugated metal and cracked surfaces, where dusty orange light clashes with industrial teal shadow. Push stylization hard so every surface reads as original game concept art, not a photograph with a filter.", "150-disco-elysium": "Render the image as Disco Elysium game art, a melancholy expressionist oil scene. Use thick brushy strokes, scraped paint, broken edges, and rough color masses in jaundiced light and deep petrol-blue shadow. Compose an elevated isometric view into a gloomy shabby seaside-city bar, with a diagonal counter, dirty harbor windows, and the figure kept small in the room. Add a large painterly portrait inset with rough brushed edges and no interface chrome, so the image stays hand-painted rather than a clean render.", "151-hades": "Render the image as Supergiant's Hades game art, a heroic character portrait. Use bold angular ink outlines, sharp sculptural plane changes, crisp blocks of saturated red and teal, and nearly black shadows. Light the figure with intense supernatural rim light, graphic flame shapes, and glowing embers against an abstract underworld glow. Frame a slightly low bust with theatrical proportions and stylized angular anatomy, polished hand-drawn action-game illustration rather than a photograph.", "152-sugimori": "Render the image as 1990s Japanese watercolor character art in the Ken Sugimori manner, on a pure white page. Use thin slightly irregular ink contours, flat economical shapes, softly pooled transparent watercolor, unpainted paper, and pale lilac-gray shadows. Give the full-body figure a slightly enlarged head, compact torso, long simplified legs, and a small original round creature companion beside it. Keep abundant white space and a printed character-sheet feeling, with no environment and no dimensional rendering.", "153-beavis-butthead": "Render the image as a Beavis and Butt-Head 1990s MTV animation frame. Use intentionally crude jittery outlines, a lumpy enormous head, a huge protruding overbite with metal braces, narrow slouched shoulders, and spindly limbs. Sit the figure in a shabby living room on a stained sagging couch, an old boxy television in the foreground, in drab muddy olive, dirty beige, and dull brown. Keep flat cel fills, a vacant grin, and low-budget adult cartoon construction rather than polished vector art.", "154-pokemon-anime": "Render the image as a late-1990s Pokémon television anime frame. Use clean hand-inked outlines, flat cel color with one crisp shadow tone, large shiny eyes, and simplified expressive faces against softly painted grass, trees, and clouds. Stage a sunlit outdoor route in a three-quarter mid view, one arm in a playful battle-ready point, with a small original creature companion nearby. Keep buoyant period television animation, not a photograph or glossy dimensional picture.", "155-magical-girl": "Render the image as a 1990s magical-girl anime transformation sequence. Use elegant fine ink outlines, flat cel color, luminous airbrush sparkle, long willowy limbs, a tiny stylized nose, and large glittering eyes. Float the full figure in a balletic diagonal pose through a pastel pink, lavender, and aqua void filled with streaming ribbons, stars, and hearts. Keep theatrical magical highlights and flowing negative space, never photographic realism or modern dimensional rendering.", "156-trigger-anime": "Render the image as a Studio Trigger action-anime impact frame, with the angular energy of Kill la Kill and Gurren Lagann. Use extreme foreshortening, violently skewed perspective, sharp exaggerated anatomy, thick bold ink shapes, and chunky black graphic shadows. Limit the palette to neon red, deep black, and piercing cream, with explosive speed lines and abstract geometric shards. Push a tight worm's-eye diagonal so a foreground form looms huge, genuine hyper-energetic key animation rather than soft prettiness.", "157-klaus": "Render the image as a Klaus (2019) animated feature frame. Draw expressive angular characters with asymmetrical stylized proportions and sculpted facial planes, then wrap them in soft painted volumetric light that gives the flat drawing luminous depth. Set an exaggerated snowy Nordic village of tall crooked timber buildings at dusk, with a warm lantern glow against cold blue and violet snow. Keep hand-painted backgrounds and graphic shapes, not a photograph and not smooth CGI.", "158-paper-mario": "Render the image as a Paper Mario-style game scene. Make the subject a completely flat paper-thin cutout with a thick white outer contour, dark cartoon outlines, solid bright fills, and no shading, while the world is folded 3D papercraft. Use a comically large head and tiny body, and bend one corner to show the thin white paper reverse. Shoot a slightly low oblique gameplay view among cardstock buildings, accordion foliage, and hard little cast shadows.", "159-hd-2d": "Render the image as an Octopath Traveler HD-2D role-playing game scene. Place a small low-resolution pixel-art sprite, with crisp visible square pixels and squat sprite proportions, inside a detailed 3D miniature diorama. Use a wide elevated three-quarter view of a medieval tavern, cinematic bloom around glowing lanterns, and tilt-shift blur on the near and far scenery. Keep the room dominant, in brown, teal, and amber, a hybrid of 2D sprite and 3D environment rather than a full-screen painting.", "160-myst": "Render the image as a Myst (1993) pre-rendered adventure-game still. Build early-1990s raytraced CGI with simple polygon geometry, hard specular highlights, repeating mapped textures, and a slightly dithered limited-color finish. Frame a static eye-level view of an eerily quiet symmetrical island library, glossy floors and unlabeled shelves, with a window onto turquoise water and a rocky isle. Composite the subject as a small grainy live-action video figure with faint edge fringing and mismatched lighting, not modern realistic rendering.", "161-flash-animation": "Render the image as an early-2000s Newgrounds Flash animation still. Use economical vector shapes, uniform thin black strokes, obvious linear and radial gradient fills, big simple eyes, and hinged symbol-like limbs frozen in an awkward tween. Frame a bold exaggerated low angle in web-banner teal, purple, and orange, with a sparse angular background. It should look like a scrappy 2002 browser cartoon, not a polished modern illustration.", "162-ms-paint": "Render the image as a crude MS Paint drawing made awkwardly with a mouse on a pure white canvas. Use the classic default 16-color palette, jagged aliased black pencil lines, shaky overlapping contours, and paint-bucket fills that leave accidental white gaps. Add a few spray-can speckles, mismatched limbs, and naive inconsistent perspective, with lots of untouched canvas. It should look endearingly amateur and unfinished, not a stylish illustration or antialiased artwork.", "163-clip-art": "Render the image as 1990s office clip art on a solid white page. Use simple flat vector shapes, thick black outlines, crude stock-art gradient fills, a cheerful generic pose, and a plain gray offset drop shadow. Give the full-length figure simplified comical proportions, a short body, and large expressive hands, surrounded by broad empty white. It should look like a 1995 newsletter graphic, not a modern polished character illustration.", "164-moomin": "Render the image as a Tove Jansson Moomin illustration. Use delicate pen-and-ink outlines, fine patient hatching and stippling, and calm muted blue-green watercolor washes on open paper. Set tiny figures in a wide Scandinavian valley of waving grasses, mossy stones, birches, and distant hills, alongside soft rounded white hippo-like creatures. Keep whimsical storybook proportions, quiet companionship, and a printed ink-and-wash feeling, not a modern cartoon screenshot.", "165-asterix": "Render the image as a Franco-Belgian Asterix comic in the Albert Uderzo manner. Use thick-to-thin flowing black outlines, big bulbous noses, wildly expressive caricature, compact rubbery anatomy, bright flat color, and very sparse cel shadows. Stage a lively wide Gaulish village of round thatched huts, with Roman soldiers reacting comically behind a foreground figure. Keep strong graphic silhouettes, sunny greens and ochres, and a clear azure sky, hand-inked bande dessinée rather than a photograph.", "166-calvin-hobbes": "Render the image as a Calvin and Hobbes Sunday comic in the Bill Watterson manner. Use energetic brush-and-ink lines of lively variable width, delicate transparent watercolor, playful stylized anatomy, and exuberant cartoon gestures on white paper. Compose one large Sunday splash panel with the action small inside an expansive airy landscape and generous negative space. Include a stuffed tiger companion with floppy plush limbs and a playful face, whimsical rather than cinematic or vector-clean.", "167-garbage-pail": "Render the image as a 1980s Garbage Pail Kids gross-out trading card. Paint with airbrush and gouache: waxy doll-like surfaces, lurid glossy highlights, a sickly pastel gradient, and grotesque proportions with an oversized head and tiny chubby body. Use a tight frontal composition inside a rounded off-white border and a blank red-rimmed name banner. Keep mischievous dark humor and crisp painted edges, a sticker illustration rather than a photo of a real card.", "168-fantasy-card": "Render the image as a Magic: The Gathering-style epic fantasy card illustration. Paint with dramatic chiaroscuro, visible oil and gouache brushwork, complex magical atmosphere, and heroic stylized anatomy. Frame the scene in a thin ornate antiqued-bronze card border with blank ornamental cartouches, and stage a low-angle full-body figure on a broken stone dais amid storm and spell light. Use deep teal and indigo shadows against hot amber magic, dynamic diagonals, and painterly edge control, never a photograph or dimensional render.", "169-postage-stamp": "Render the image as a vintage engraved postage stamp in a single dark ink on ivory paper. Fill most of the view with one straight-on stamp, perforated on all four sides, and an ornamental intaglio frame around a small oval cameo. Engrave with delicate tapered burin strokes, parallel curved hatching, and fine crosshatching, plus a faded circular postmark of geometric lines and wavy bars crossing one corner. Keep the print flat and tactile, with fibrous paper and tiny ink irregularities, not a photographic portrait.", "170-banknote": "Render the image as an intricate banknote engraving in green and violet ink on pale ivory cotton paper. Center a wide horizontal note in a straight-on view, with a finely engraved portrait vignette on one side and interlocking guilloche rosettes, spirographic medallions, and dense security line fields around it. Use exquisite steel-engraved crosshatching, micro-texture of abstract dashes, uneven ink pressure, and visible paper fibers. Keep every open area ornamental and abstract, clearly fantasy security printing rather than a copy of real currency.", "171-matryoshka": "Render the image as a traditional Russian matryoshka still life. Turn the subject into a set of lathe-turned wooden nesting dolls in decreasing sizes, pear-shaped bodies and oversized flat round faces, with no separate realistic limbs. Paint glossy lacquer folk florals, berries, and apron patterns in deep red and dark green over visible brushwork. Show the whole set on a wood table from a slightly elevated angle, one doll opened to reveal the pale hollow interior.", "172-tiki-mug": "Render the image as a handcrafted ceramic tiki cocktail mug. Sculpt the subject into a squat cylindrical vessel with deeply carved exaggerated features, geometric planes, chunky spiral forms, and a glossy drip glaze in amber, rust, and cream. Leave the top open and filled as a tropical drink, with a paper umbrella and bamboo straw, photographed close on a bamboo bar. Keep handmade irregularities, pooled glaze, and crackle, a crafted tiki face rather than a portrait printed on a cup.", "173-lite-brite": "Render the image as a glowing Lite-Brite toy picture photographed in a dark room. Build the entire picture from colored translucent pegs pushed into a black perforated board, with discrete round glowing heads and clearly black gaps, no smooth painting inside the board. Show the plastic frame at a slight oblique angle, a few loose pegs nearby, and simplified toy proportions dictated by the peg grid. Use restrained halation and nostalgic dim-room light so it reads as a real constructed toy.", "174-3d-chalk": "Render the image as anamorphic 3D sidewalk chalk art, photographed from the one angle where the illusion works. Draw on flat pavement with dusty pigment, hatching, rubbed blends, and paving joints running through the picture, elongating forms so they seem to rise from a pit. Place the artwork large in a real pedestrian street, with people behind it for scale and an open chalk box at the edge. Use soft daylight and street-art documentation, not a sculpture or a real excavated hole.", "175-toast-art": "Render the image as edible toast art in an overhead breakfast photograph. Burn the subject into one thick slice using only pale crumb, golden toast, mid-brown, and dark scorched detail, with the porous bread still visible and no ink or paint. Simplify shapes into bold stencil-like masks and uneven burn gradients, the slice slightly rotated on a plain plate with crumbs and a butter knife nearby. Use close food photography, raking morning light, and a crisp crust.", "176-coffee-stain": "Render the image as a painting made only with brewed coffee on off-white watercolor paper. Use transparent tan washes, dark pooled espresso edges, tide marks, blooms, granulation, ring stains, and accidental drips, all in monochrome brown with untouched paper. Keep the drawing loose and airy, calligraphic brushwork and elongated illustrative forms, photographed from directly above. Set a small cup and a stained brush at the paper's edge so the material stays obvious, not a sepia photo filter.", "177-caravaggio": "Render the image as a Caravaggio Baroque oil painting with extreme tenebrism. Drop the background into pitch black and carve the lit forms and cloth from a single hard diagonal beam of light. Use a tight half-length group in a rough tavern, forceful twisted gestures, overlapping figures, and unidealized features in dark earth pigments and warm flesh. Paint with layered oil glazes and sculptural chiaroscuro so it reads as an original Baroque canvas, never a filtered photograph.", "178-vermeer": "Render the image as a Johannes Vermeer Dutch interior oil painting. Use quiet measured geometry, soft daylight from a leaded window at the left, pearl-like pinpoints of light, ultramarine blue, and lemon yellow. Set the figure back in a spacious domestic room with a luminous plaster wall, a calm absorbed task at a table, and a glimpse of tiled floor. Keep delicate diffused contours and restrained glazes, profound stillness, an authentic painted surface rather than a photograph.", "179-turner": "Render the image as a JMW Turner Romantic oil painting. Let dissolving atmospheric light swallow the scene: swirling golden haze against a blue-gray storm, incandescent yellows, ochres, and whites, with ships and solid forms melting into vapor. Keep any figure tiny at the lower edge, almost lost in weather, while the sky occupies nearly the whole canvas. Use scumbling, thin translucent washes, impasted flashes, and broken brushwork, the sublime overcoming detail.", "180-rothko": "Render the image as a Mark Rothko color field painting. Stack two monumental softly edged luminous rectangles, one dull gold and one deep rust, inside a subtly glowing warm reddish-brown ground. Feather their perimeters into thin translucent veils of stained pigment so the surface breathes, matte and uneven, with immense quiet. Let any subject survive only as a near-invisible tonal ghost inside the color, never a drawn contour or a poster-like shape.", "181-basquiat": "Render the image as a Jean-Michel Basquiat neo-expressionist painting. Use bold oil stick and abrasive scrawls on rough canvas: an oversized masklike head, a three-point crown, skeletal diagram marks, crossed-out nonverbal shapes, and nervous scratching. Layer ragged patches of saturated red, turquoise, black, ivory, and ochre with pentimenti and exposed canvas tooth. Keep the figure raw, frontal, and asymmetric, an improvised painting rather than a photograph or a tidy illustration.", "182-kandinsky": "Render the image as a Wassily Kandinsky abstract composition. Dissolve the subject into circles, triangles, arcs, thin black lines, and floating color notes arranged with musical rhythm on a softly painted warm ivory ground. Spread motifs across the whole surface in counterpoint, with large and small circles, diagonal movement, and generous airy intervals. Use vivid blue, vermilion, lemon yellow, violet, and green in flat pictorial space, a hand-painted modernist surface with no photographic perspective.", "183-toulouse-lautrec": "Render the image as Henri de Toulouse-Lautrec's Belle Époque cabaret lithograph. Use bold economical outlines, flat silhouettes, rubbed crayon, and spattered ink in olive green, vermilion, and dense black on muted tan paper. Compose a wide oblique interior with a cropped dark foreground, a deep diagonal floor, and a red curtain reduced to a graphic mass. Keep incisive caricatured profiles, elongated proportions, daring crops, and vintage lithograph edges with slight misregistration.", "184-schiele": "Render the image as an Egon Schiele expressionist drawing. Use nervous wiry broken contour lines in graphite and dark ink, with exposed construction strokes and patchy translucent gouache in ochre, pale pink, and brick red. Leave broad areas of warm bare paper as the light, with dry uneven edges and no modeled photographic shading. Pose the subject in an angular, contorted, asymmetrical arrangement of elongated limbs and large expressive hands, off-center on an otherwise empty sheet.", "185-okeeffe": "Render the image as a Georgia O'Keeffe modernist oil painting. Enlarge organic forms into monumental close-ups of undulating petals that read like sculptural landscapes, with a narrow opening onto a distant desert mesa. Blend ivory, coral, dusty pink, burgundy, ochre, and desert turquoise in smooth sensual gradients and seamless painterly transitions. Flatten space into rhythmic curved planes, precise and quietly abstract rather than outlined or cartooned.", "186-lempicka": "Render the image as a Tamara de Lempicka Art Deco portrait. Build glossy metallic volumes from cubist-smooth planes, elongated sculptural anatomy, and sharp architectural folds, with hard angular highlights and cool emerald shadows. Paint the surface like meticulously blended enamel against austere stylized architecture and a sleek machine-age automobile. Keep the mood cool, monumental, and geometric rather than soft, loose, or photographic.", "187-futurama": "Render the image as a Futurama cartoon frame. Draw clean black outlines, flat cel colors, and minimal shadow shapes, with round faces, large circular eyes, and a mouth set naturally under a small nose with no overbite. Place the scene in retro-futuristic New New York, among cyan, mint, and coral towers, flying cars, and transparent transport tubes under a bright sky. Keep it a simplified adult animated sitcom, not a painting or a photograph.", "188-hey-arnold": "Render the image as a 1990s Hey Arnold Nickelodeon cartoon. Give the subject an oversized football-shaped head on a small body, with thick irregular outlines and thin cartoon limbs. Paint a grungy city brownstone stoop in warm muted ochre, faded terracotta, dusty teal, and brown, with crooked railings, grimy brick, and paper grain in the gouache background. Keep the perspective playfully distorted and offbeat rather than clean or glossy.", "189-samurai-jack": "Render the image as a Samurai Jack animation frame. Build the picture from outline-free flat color shapes, severe graphic simplification, and elongated angular anatomy with bold silhouettes. Stage an extreme cinematic panorama: a low distant horizon, layered dark mountain bands, and a huge sky of vermilion, amber, and an immense pale sun. Use a deep charcoal foreground and broad negative space, with no photographic texture or soft shading.", "190-avatar": "Render the image as an Avatar: The Last Airbender animation frame. Use anime-influenced Western hand-drawn linework, angular yet believable faces, lively gesture, and restrained cel shading. Set an Earth Kingdom interior of green ceramics, carved timber, and circular lattice, painted in warm jade, ochre, and amber afternoon light. Let flowing elemental swirls arc around the action like bending choreography, against a spacious painterly background.", "191-invader-zim": "Render the image as an Invader Zim cartoon frame. Use thick jagged black outlines, viciously angular spiky silhouettes, absurdly oversized eyes, a massive head on a tiny wiry body, and sharp clawlike hands. Warp the view into a tilted gothic suburb of crooked pointed houses, leaning fences, and a sickly green sky. Dominate the palette with acid green, bruised purple, and hard black shadow wedges in flat creepy-cute cel color.", "192-steven-universe": "Render the image as a Steven Universe animation frame. Use soft pastel peach, lavender, seafoam, and muted yellow, with simple rounded shapes, delicate colored outlines, and extremely gentle flat shading. Draw bouncy rounded cartoon anatomy against airy painted environments and dreamy pink skies. Keep the light soothing and diffuse, with no heavy shadows, glossy three-dimensional rendering, or sharp anime linework.", "193-peppa-pig": "Render the image as a Peppa Pig-like preschool cartoon. Use extremely simple flat shapes, thick slightly irregular colored outlines, and no shading or realistic volume. Draw a side-profile head with both eyes on the same visible side, a simple protruding nose, stick limbs, and a tiny smile. Place the figure on a bright green rolling hill under a clear blue sky beside a plain muddy puddle, in cheerful primary colors and lots of empty space.", "194-bluey": "Render the image as a Bluey-like preschool cartoon. Use soft rounded flat geometric shapes, an oversized rounded head, chunky simple hands, and clean outlines with almost no shading. Set a warm Queensland house veranda of timber boards, a pale blue railing, and peach-cream walls, with a leafy subtropical garden beyond. Keep sunny pastel color, gentle painted background texture, and a cozy domestic mood rather than glossy three-dimensional animation or detailed anime.", "195-satoshi-kon": "Render the image as a Satoshi Kon anime film frame, in the vein of Paprika and Perfect Blue. Use realistic adult anime proportions, carefully observed faces, crisp delicate ink lines, and meticulously painted backgrounds. Let ordinary objects spill into an impossible dream parade that shifts scale while the draftsmanship stays coherent. Favor deep teal shadows, brick red, amber, and creamy highlights under uncanny cinematic lighting, not a photograph or a three-dimensional render.", "196-evangelion": "Render the image as a Neon Genesis Evangelion 1990s cel-anime frame. Use economical crisp ink lines, hard-edged cel shadows, sharp angular facial planes, and a hint of analog film grain. Stage it like a stark title card: a tight foreground crop against a vast moody city of power lines, apartment blocks, and a distant slender mecha silhouette. Cut a narrow band of searing orange sunset through deep purple-blue shadow and near-black geometric masses.", "197-one-piece": "Render the image as an Eiichiro Oda One Piece manga and anime frame. Exaggerate rubbery stretchy limbs, an oversized expressive face, and a huge toothy grin, drawn with bold variable ink outlines and energetic graphic shapes. Use bright adventurous cel colors, with turquoise sea, open sky, and a pirate-ship deck of sails, curved masts, and railings. Keep the anatomy fantastical and hand-drawn, never photographic or softly painted.", "198-naruto": "Render the image as a Naruto anime action cel. Use angular lively linework, sharp clean shadow blocks, athletic stylized anatomy, and wind-swept motion strokes. Add orange-and-black ninja clothing, a forehead headband with a simple leaf mark, and spiky dynamic hair. Leap the action across Hidden Leaf Village rooftops of warm tile, green trees, and a distant carved mountain, in graphic midday light of ochre, leaf green, and black.", "199-lupin": "Render the image as a 1970s Lupin III anime caper frame. Draw lanky elegant proportions, long thin limbs, a witty long-nosed profile, and loose jazzy ink contours with imperfect analog cel linework. Use flat restrained 1970s color, terracotta buildings, a powder-blue sky, and a small yellow vintage getaway car leaning into a bend. Keep the background loosely gouache-painted, with long graphic shadows and plenty of environmental space.", "200-fleischer-superman": "Render the image as a 1940s Fleischer Superman theatrical cartoon. Paint a dark Art Deco retro-futurist skyline of stepped pinnacles, lit windows, and searchlight beams, and send a streamlined figure flying on a dramatic diagonal above it. Draw solid rotoscoped heroic anatomy as hand-inked cels, with a plain flowing cape, hard theatrical rim light, and deep blue-black shadows. Contrast flat character paint with rich atmospheric backgrounds in midnight blue, burgundy, and golden ochre.", "201-street-fighter": "Render the image as a Street Fighter II 16-bit arcade fighting-game screen. Render crisp chunky pixel art with limited colors per sprite, strong dark outlines, and stepped shading, and show a large full-body fighter in a wide side-on combat stance on a vivid layered stage with spectators behind. Place two colorful health bars of plain rectangular pixels along the top. Keep saturated turquoise, brick red, cobalt, and gold, like early-1990s sprite animation rather than smooth vector art.", "202-diablo": "Render the image as Diablo-like dark fantasy action-RPG art. Use a steep isometric view down into a gothic dungeon of broken arches, worn stone tiles, and pools of black shadow, with scattered glowing embers. Keep the figure relatively small among the architecture, rendered in gritty painterly textures of dark red, tarnished ochre, and ashen stone. Let oppressive chiaroscuro and a smoldering ember glow define the forms, like old pre-rendered game assets rather than a close portrait.", "203-nes-zelda": "Render the image as a primitive 1980s 8-bit NES Zelda-like top-down game screen. Build a flat orthographic overworld from repeated tiles: solid green grass, simple two-tone bushes, a sandy path, and a black cave mouth in a rocky wall. Draw the subject as a very tiny sprite in only a few flat colors, with chunky unaliased pixels and no gradients or shading. Keep the whole view limited, hardware-simple, and free of perspective, like an enlarged nearest-neighbor bitmap.", "204-katamari": "Render the image as Katamari Damacy game art. Build a whimsical candy-colored world from chunky low-poly models, flat textures, angular geometry, and toy-like proportions with a stubby body and an oversized head. Show a low three-quarter view of a huge lumpy ball of stuck-on everyday objects being rolled through a surreal plaza. Use bright pink, lime, sky blue, lavender, and orange under simple cheerful early-2000s console lighting, not smooth cinematic rendering.", "205-cuphead": "Render the image as Cuphead-like 1930s rubber-hose game art. Draw bouncy figures with pie-cut eyes, white gloves, and flexible tube limbs, inked like a vintage theatrical cartoon over a soft watercolor background. Stage an exaggerated boss-fight showdown with squash-and-stretch poses, antique high-contrast color, and grainy old-film texture. Make it feel like painted cels photographed from a worn print, not modern three-dimensional animation or digital anime.", "206-hollow-knight": "Render the image as Hollow Knight-like hand-drawn gothic 2D game art. Reduce the subject to a tiny cloaked silhouette with a simple mask and delicate bug-like proportions, drawn in fine ink against vast empty space. Set dark blue caverns, hanging ruins, and small glowing lanterns in a melancholy atmospheric wash. Keep the line fragile and illustrative, with deep navy, charcoal, and pale glow rather than bright cartoon color.", "207-team-fortress": "Render the image as Team Fortress 2 game art. Illustrate with a 1960s spy-movie poster sensibility: exaggerated comic proportions, painterly brush shading, and strong rim light on simplified forms. Use a warm retro-industrial setting of metal, warning stripes, and slightly grimy machinery. Keep the color bold but a little dusty, with graphic silhouettes and illustrated volume rather than realistic materials or flat cel fills.", "208-okami": "Render the image as Ōkami game art. Render forms with sumi-e ink-brush outlines over simplified volumes, sitting on a warm parchment texture. Add celestial calligraphic strokes, ink splatters, and flowers blooming along the motion, as if a paintbrush were rewriting the world. Use a limited palette of black, cinnabar, gold, and soft washi cream, with flat graphic light rather than realistic materials or photographic detail.", "209-undertale": "Render the image as Undertale-like indie RPG art. Use low-resolution pixel art that stays mostly black and white, with only a few sparse hits of color and chunky unaliased edges. Stage a battle-encounter layout: a plain rectangular box, a small heart, and a quirky monster shape facing across the box. Keep the drawing crude, playful, and graphic, like a tiny handmade game screen rather than a polished illustration.", "210-monument-valley": "Render the image as Monument Valley game art. Build impossible Escher-like isometric architecture from clean pastel geometric towers, arches, and stairs that connect in contradictory ways. Keep surfaces matte and untextured, in dusty pink, sand, peach, and muted teal, with soft even light and long simple shadows. Place the subject as a tiny silent figure on an impossible staircase, dwarfed by the architecture and free of surface detail.", "211-infrared": "Render the image as a false-color infrared photograph. Turn foliage white and pale pink, push the sky toward deep maroon or near-black, and let living surfaces glow pale and luminous. Use the surreal look of infrared film, with high contrast, slight grain, and hues no ordinary camera records. Keep an outdoor setting so plants and sky carry the effect, under soft daylight rather than studio lighting.", "212-holga": "Render the image as a Holga toy-camera photograph. Use soft plastic-lens blur, muddy midtone detail, and a heavy dark vignette that swallows the corners. Add light leaks, color fringing, and saturated cross-processed color that slides toward teal shadows and warm highlights. Keep the exposure dreamy and imperfect, with slight overlap and none of the sharpness of a modern digital camera.", "213-disposable-camera": "Render the image as a late-1990s disposable-camera snapshot. Blast the scene with harsh direct flash, hard black shadows, red-eye, and slightly blown highlights. Use cheap color-negative grain, soft focus, and casual party clutter falling off into darkness behind the subject. Add a small orange date-stamp blot in one corner as an illegible graphic smear, not as readable type.", "214-cctv": "Render the image as a grainy black-and-white CCTV security still. Shoot from a high ceiling corner with strong fisheye distortion, low resolution, and muddy contrast. Add surveillance noise, interlaced smear, and a small timestamp blot of illegible block shapes in a corner. Keep the light flat and fluorescent, the framing indifferent, and the image utilitarian rather than composed or cinematic.", "215-polaroid": "Render the image as a Polaroid instant photograph. Give it washed-out warm color, soft focus, slight chemical fade, and gentle flash falloff. Present the picture as a physical print with a wide white border that is thicker along the bottom edge. Keep the mood casual and a little overexposed, with muted shadows and the soft imperfect surface of instant film.", "216-cyanotype": "Render the image as a cyanotype sun print. Reduce the scene to white shapes and Prussian-blue tones on textured watercolor paper, as if the subject and nearby plants were contact-exposed in sunlight. Overlap botanical leaf silhouettes, soft brushed edges, and uneven chemical pooling across the sheet. Keep the surface flat and graphic, with no full-color paint and no photographic midtones beyond blue and white.", "217-lego-mosaic": "Render the image as a flat LEGO mosaic built from round 1x1 plates on a baseplate. Translate every tone into a limited brick palette, with the picture formed entirely by visible circular studs on a strict grid. Photograph the finished mosaic at a slight angle so the studs catch real light and a bit of the baseplate edge shows. Keep it a physical construction, not a smooth digital pixel portrait.", "218-pyrography": "Render the image as pyrography burned into a pale wooden slice. Draw with scorched lines and stippled shading, from pale toast to deep char, as if a heated metal tip had traced the forms. Leave the wood grain visible in the light areas and show the natural bark edge around the disc. Keep the image tonal and handmade, using only the burned browns of the wood and no added ink or paint.", "219-bottle-caps": "Render the image as a street-art mosaic of hundreds of colorful metal bottle caps nailed to a wooden board. Build the tones from the caps' printed colors and circular rims, with visible gaps of wood between them. Let the caps catch daylight and cast tiny shadows so the surface reads as a large physical installation. Keep the image chunky, graphic, and assembled, photographed straight on rather than painted.", "220-chocolate": "Render the image as a pastry-chef chocolate showpiece. Sculpt the subject in glossy dark and milk chocolate, with smooth molded volumes, a sharp tempered sheen, and piped white-chocolate detail. Set the piece on a simple stand under warm display light that reflects off the curved surfaces. Keep every material edible-looking, from matte cocoa dust to mirror-shine ganache, with no painted illusion of other substances.", "221-topiary": "Render the image as a garden topiary sculpture. Translate the subject into dense trimmed boxwood, with leafy texture, crisp sheared edges, and a slightly irregular handmade silhouette. Place it in a formal garden of gravel paths and clipped hedges under soft daylight. Keep the color living green, with pockets of interior shadow between the leaves and no smooth stone or metal surface.", "222-string-art": "Render the image as string art on a dark wooden board. Place a field of small nails and wrap colored threads between them so straight lines accumulate into the subject. Use dense crossings for darker tones and sparse strands for highlights, letting the wood show through. Keep every contour a taut straight thread, and photograph it as a physical craft object rather than a drawn illustration.", "223-cereal-box": "Render the image as a vintage 1980s cereal-box mascot illustration. Draw a bouncy airbrushed character with glossy highlights, thick friendly outlines, and saturated primary color in a simple breakfast scene. Add a dynamic splash of milk and flying flakes, with the soft studio shading of printed food packaging. Keep it cheerful, commercial, and slightly exaggerated, like a mascot leaping off a box front.", "224-jazz-record": "Render the image as a 1950s Blue Note jazz record sleeve. Crop a high-contrast black-and-white photograph into a bold duotone and lock it against a flat geometric color block in deep blue, teal, or orange. Use a strong asymmetric layout, tight cropping, and a generous empty field of color, in the Reid Miles tradition. Keep the print flat, graphic, and mid-century, with hard edges and no soft collage texture.", "225-matisse-cutouts": "Render the image as a late Henri Matisse paper cut-out. Build the picture from bold flat shapes of gouache-painted paper, with organic seaweed and leaf forms in cobalt blue, coral, yellow, and emerald. Reduce the subject to a simplified cut silhouette with visible scissor edges, slight overlaps, and a few pinning marks. Keep the ground a flat paper field, with no shading, drawn outline, or depth beyond layered sheets.", "226-modigliani": "Render the image as an Amedeo Modigliani portrait. Elongate the face into a narrow oval, stretch the neck, tilt the head, and paint the eyes as almond slits without pupils. Use muted earth pigments, soft dry contours, and a warm flat background of ochre or dusty blue with almost no setting. Keep the surface quietly modernist and mask-like, simplified rather than anatomically realistic.", "227-chagall": "Render the image as a Marc Chagall dream painting. Let the subject float above a small village of tilted rooftops, joined by a fiddler, a goat, and a crescent moon. Use deep blue night air and jewel-like patches of red, green, and gold, painted with naive folk-poetry drawing. Keep scale playful and gravity optional, with soft oil color rather than sharp modern illustration.", "228-hockney": "Render the image as a David Hockney California pool painting. Use flat acrylic planes, a bright turquoise pool crossed by squiggly lines of reflected light, and a crisp modernist house with palm trees. Keep shadows hard and local, colors sunny and unblended, and the drawing clean and graphic. Favor a still, sunlit calm over visible texture, drama, or photographic detail.", "229-van-eyck": "Render the image as a Jan van Eyck Northern Renaissance oil painting. Describe every surface with microscopic clarity, from fabric and wood grain to distant reflections, in glossy jewel-like color over a luminous oil glaze. Set an ornate interior and include a small convex mirror that reflects the room. Light the scene with cool clear daylight, precise and enamel-smooth rather than loose or modern.", "230-hundertwasser": "Render the image as a Friedensreich Hundertwasser painting. Avoid straight lines: turn buildings into spirals, onion domes, and irregular windows of uneven size, and let the subject merge into the swirling facades. Use vivid unmixed color, metallic gold and silver accents, and organic contours like living architecture. Keep the surface playful, decorative, and rhythmically irregular, with no perspective grid or realistic shading.", "231-kusama": "Render the image as a Yayoi Kusama infinity installation. Cover the subject and the surrounding room in relentless red and white polka dots until the form starts to dissolve into the pattern. Place everything in a mirrored infinity room, where dotted pumpkins repeat into endless reflections. Use flat graphic color, hard edges, and hypnotic repetition rather than naturalistic light or texture.", "232-botero": "Render the image as a Fernando Botero painting. Inflate every volume into smooth, plump, balloon-like forms, with small features and calm, nearly expressionless faces. Set a sunlit plaza of rounded architecture, where tiny objects are dwarfed by oversized rounded hands. Use creamy blended oil, warm earth color, and soft even light, with weight and serenity rather than motion or sharp caricature.", "233-wyeth": "Render the image as an Andrew Wyeth egg-tempera painting. Open a vast dry meadow in muted ochre, gray, and brown, with a lonely farmhouse on a low hill and grasses described blade by blade. Place the subject small in the landscape, often turned away, under a pale still sky. Keep the surface dry, precise, and melancholy, with thin tempera strokes rather than lush oil color.", "234-rugrats": "Render the image as a 1990s Rugrats Nickelodeon cartoon. Draw huge heads, scribbly uneven outlines, quirky off-model shapes, and saturated odd colors in flat cel fills. Use a toddler-height low angle looking up through a cluttered domestic room, so furniture looms and the proportions feel slightly wrong. Keep the line playful and wobbly, like hand-drawn Saturday-morning animation rather than a polished feature.", "235-batman-tas": "Render the image as a Batman: The Animated Series frame. Paint light shapes onto black, as if drawn on dark paper, with sharp angular jaws, long shadows, and Art Deco architecture. Set a blood-red sky, distant police blimps, and a rooftop or alley staged in noir silhouette. Use a limited palette of black, gray, crimson, and pale highlights, with theatrical lighting and clean graphic shapes.", "236-tmnt-1987": "Render the image as a 1987 Teenage Mutant Ninja Turtles Saturday-morning cartoon. Use bold black outlines, flat saturated cel color, and simple limited-animation poses, with a slightly soft VHS look. Set a brick sewer lair in bright green, purple, and orange, with pipes, a shaft of manhole light, and casual pizza-box clutter. Keep the drawing chunky and cheerful, not modern anime or painterly shading.", "237-pingu": "Render the image as Pingu claymation. Build smooth glossy plasticine forms with simple round volumes, stubby limbs, and small beak-like mouths, photographed as real stop-motion. Set a snowy igloo interior in bright white and blue, with soft even light and a slight fingerprint texture in the clay. Keep each pose readable and childlike, with no drawn outlines, digital fur, or realistic anatomy.", "238-scooby-doo": "Render the image as a 1969 Scooby-Doo Hanna-Barbera cartoon. Use limited animation, thick even outlines, flat cel color, and simple repeated poses. Paint a spooky nighttime background in green and purple, with a crooked haunted mansion and a boxy mystery van nearby. Keep the figures graphic and slightly static against a more detailed gouache background, in the early Saturday-morning mystery-cartoon look.", "239-he-man": "Render the image as a 1980s Filmation cartoon in the He-Man mold. Use stiff heroic poses, heavy rotoscoped muscles, hard black outlines, and flat saturated cel color. Set a fantasy landscape with a skull-faced castle and skies that feel like reused painted backgrounds. Keep the light even and graphic, the anatomy exaggerated and statuesque, and the motion limited rather than fluid.", "240-cowboy-bebop": "Render the image as a Cowboy Bebop anime frame. Use slim realistic adult proportions, crisp thin linework, and restrained cinematic shading with a jazzy noir mood. Set a worn spaceship interior in muted teal, amber, and dusty gray, with a thin haze hanging in the light. Keep the framing filmic and slightly weary, like a late-1990s hand-painted anime still rather than a bright action cartoon.", "241-attack-on-titan": "Render the image as an Attack on Titan anime frame. Use heavy thick outlines, intense hatched shading, and strained expressions under a turbulent sky. Stage a towering medieval wall with a colossal humanoid silhouette looming just above it, and add vertical maneuvering-gear straps and cables on the foreground figure. Keep the palette desaturated stone, olive, and blood red, with weight and dread rather than clean cheerful color.", "242-doraemon": "Render the image as a Doraemon manga frame in the classic Fujiko Fujio style. Use very simple round shapes, large circular eyes, short clean outlines, and soft flat color with almost no shading. Set a tidy Japanese suburban room with tatami mats, a low table, daylight from a window, and a small round blue robotic cat as a companion. Keep the drawing gentle, readable, and childlike rather than detailed anime.", "243-kyoani": "Render the image as a Kyoto Animation slice-of-life anime frame. Use soft bokeh, warm afternoon glow, and a gentle pastel interior with a delicately painted background. Render the eyes glossy and highly detailed, with fine hair highlights and restrained cel shading over otherwise realistic proportions. Keep the mood quiet and luminous, like a 2010s television anime still rather than action manga or a flat preschool cartoon.", "244-mob-psycho": "Render the image as a Mob Psycho 100 anime frame. Draw the subject with a crude, simple, slightly awkward design and uneven linework, then surround that figure with explosive psychedelic psychic energy. Let the power become wildly colorful experimental animation: smeared color, distorted space, and chaotic effects that outshine the figure. Keep a sharp contrast between plain everyday drawing and overwhelming supernatural color.", "245-tron": "Render the image as a 1982 TRON film frame of early optical-compositing computer-world cinema. Place the subject in a black digital void traced by hard luminous neon cyan and orange circuit lines, primitive geometric grids, and angular light-cycle trails. Use backlit high-contrast film with optical glow and slight analog grain. Keep the look primitive and geometric rather than modern glossy sci-fi rendering.", "246-metropolis-1927": "Render the image as a 1927 Metropolis silent-film still in pure black and white. Build a German Expressionist Art Deco city of looming miniature skyscrapers and forced perspective under hard theatrical stage lighting. Use silver-gelatin tones, heavy black eye makeup, fine film scratches, grain, and uneven exposure that suggests flicker. It should feel like handmade monumental cinema, not a modern portrait with a retro filter.", "247-technicolor-musical": "Render the image as a genuine 1950s Technicolor Hollywood musical film still. Use extraordinarily saturated three-strip reds, cobalt blue, emerald, and warm yellow, with glamorous midcentury studio cinematography. Stage the subject on an obviously painted backdrop under a theatrical spotlight, caught in cheerful choreography and a sweeping pose. The result should feel photochemical and lavish, not a modern photograph with color grading.", "248-saul-bass": "Render the image as a Saul Bass film poster with no typography. Reduce the subject to stark flat hand-cut paper shapes and a jagged angular silhouette against a bold red, black, and white field. Use slightly imperfect scissors-cut edges, matte screenprint ink, and strongly asymmetric negative space. No photography, realistic shading, gradients, or simulated depth.", "249-shaw-brothers": "Render the image as an authentic 1970s Shaw Brothers kung fu film still on a colorful studio soundstage. Use a tight off-kilter crash-zoom, a painted mountain backdrop, vermilion pillars, and jade-green and cobalt set pieces under hard theatrical key light. Apply saturated 1970s color timing, organic 35mm grain, and slight optical zoom streaking near the edges. It should feel caught mid-action, not like a modern lifestyle portrait.", "250-n64": "Render the image as Nintendo 64-era 3D platformer graphics in a floating castle hub world. Turn the subject into a very low-polygon model with an oversized faceted head, a tiny torso, and chunky limbs, seen from a wide elevated third-person camera. Use smooth Gouraud vertex shading, blurry bilinear textures, baked shadows, a short draw distance, and bright primary colors. No modern ambient occlusion, depth of field, or realistic surfaces.", "251-sonic": "Render the image as a Sonic the Hedgehog Sega Genesis 16-bit side-scrolling platformer screenshot. Build the scene from crisp low-resolution pixel tiles: checkerboard hills, a loop-de-loop, geometric palm trees, and a saturated blue sky. Render the subject as a fast sprite with an oversized head, a tiny body, huge striding legs, horizontal speed streaks, and afterimages. Use a limited rich palette, stepped pixel edges, and clear parallax planes.", "252-doom": "Render the image as a Doom (1993)-like first-person shooter screenshot of coarse software-rendered pixels. Show a low-resolution brown hellish corridor with rough repeated wall textures, chunky perspective, banded sector lighting, rusty metal, and sparse ember-red light. Anchor the view with a large pixelated foreground weapon sprite in the classic centered position. Use digitized sprite shading, a restricted 256-color feel, and crunchy dithering, without modern lighting.", "253-journey": "Render the image as Journey by thatgamecompany game art. Reduce the subject to a small minimalist cloaked traveler with an extraordinarily long flowing red scarf, set on the crest of an immense sunlit dune. Use vast pale gold dunes, luminous sand particles, distant abstract ruins, and a solitary mountain with a thin vertical beam of light. Render warm shimmering backlight, soft atmospheric shapes, and clean cel-like shaded 3D so the landscape dominates.", "254-among-us": "Render the image as Among Us game art. Convert the subject completely into a bean-shaped crewmate with no human face or proportions: a compact body, little backpack, two short stumpy legs, and a broad glossy visor. Draw it with chunky dark outlines, clean solid fills, and only a few basic cel shadows inside a slate-purple spaceship cafeteria. Keep the look boldly graphic and flat, not 3D or painterly.", "255-fortnite": "Render the image as a Fortnite-like battle-royale character skin in a promotional 3D render. Use stylized elongated athletic cartoon proportions, a bold asymmetrical heroic pose, and a strong low camera against a cyan and violet lobby spotlight. Polish the forms with saturated color, sculpted materials, a clean silhouette, and rim light over a simple pool of floor light. No photographic surface detail, interface graphics, or realistic rendering.", "256-grim-fandango": "Render the image as Grim Fandango's 1998 pre-rendered low-poly adventure-game art. Turn the subject into a calavera with an angular geometric skull, hollow eye sockets, and long thin bone hands, staged in a noir Art Deco Land of the Dead. Use blocky early 3D geometry, grainy pre-rendered textures, orange marigolds, brass Deco details, and sharply cut teal-black chiaroscuro. Keep it witty and macabre, not a modern realistic skeleton.", "257-oregon-trail": "Render the image as an authentic Oregon Trail Apple II game scene. Restrict the whole picture to six artifact colors: black, white, green, purple, orange, and blue, built from chunky enlarged low-resolution pixels. Use hard pixel steps, sparse dithering, and highly simplified sprites in a side-on prairie with a covered wagon and blocky hills. No antialiasing, gradients, or polished modern pixel illustration.", "258-persona-5": "Render the image as Persona 5 game key art. Build a fierce collage of slashing red, black, and white asymmetrical shapes, jagged starbursts, paper-cut planes, bold ink contours, and halftone patches. Pose the subject as a stylish anime figure in a dramatic twisting crouch with exaggerated foreshortening and sharp cel shading. Let screen-printed graphic contrast dominate, with blank menu-like shapes and no readable lettering.", "259-hollywood-glamour": "Render the image as a 1940s Hollywood studio glamour photograph in the manner of George Hurrell. Use black-and-white large-format silver gelatin, butterfly key light from above, the classic small butterfly shadow, luminous skin, and a rim light edging the silhouette. Keep silky rich shadows, velvety deep blacks, and fine film grain against a dark seamless background. No color anywhere and no modern grading.", "260-tilt-shift": "Render the image as a tilt-shift miniature-effect photograph. Shoot from a steep high angle so a real street scene looks like a tiny toy model, with an extremely narrow horizontal band of crisp focus and strong creamy defocus above and below. Saturate the sunny colors and tidy the architecture until the scale reads as miniature. It should remain a real photograph, not a physical diorama on a base.", "261-light-painting": "Render the image as a long-exposure light-painting photograph made in a pitch-dark alley. Draw the subject's outline in vivid glowing neon trails of cyan, magenta, and gold, with looping ribbons, sparks, and motion streaks as the main subject. Reveal only a faint flash of the figure against dim brick and reflections on wet pavement. Use irregular LED thickness, overexposed spark points, and ghosting rather than conventional portrait light.", "262-thermal": "Render the image as an authentic thermal imaging camera view, not a photograph with a rainbow filter. Replace natural color with a false-color heat map: warm bodies glow orange and yellow, the hottest spots go white-hot, and cold surroundings fall into deep blue and indigo. Use soft low-resolution infrared texture, quantized heat contours, and diffuse temperature gradients. Temperature alone determines the palette, with no scale, numbers, or interface.", "263-laser-yearbook": "Render the image as a kitschy 1980s school yearbook studio portrait. Use a formal straight-on chest-up pose, an awkward earnest smile, and big feathered volume against a navy backdrop crisscrossed by electric blue and pink laser beams. Apply a soft-focus portrait lens, diffused frontal flash, blooming highlights, faded chromogenic color, and fine film grain. It should look like a vintage studio print, not a candid snapshot.", "264-playmobil": "Render the image as Playmobil toy photography of a plastic playset. Convert the subject into a classic figure with a cylindrical head, black dot eyes, a curved smile, a molded hairpiece, rigid limbs, and C-shaped clip hands, with no realistic skin or fingers. Photograph the full figure in a bright molded diorama from a slightly elevated three-quarter angle. Use warm softbox light, crisp plastic highlights, visible seam lines, and shallow depth of field.", "265-vinyl-figure": "Render the image as a boxed designer vinyl collectible with Funko Pop-like proportions. Give the subject an enormous rounded-square head, solid black button eyes, a tiny simplified body, stubby limbs, and a matte soft-vinyl surface. Show the whole standing toy at eye level beside a blank window box with a clear plastic window and an insert tray. Use clean pale studio light and short shadows, with no realistic anatomy or printed lettering.", "266-kintsugi": "Render the image as a handmade kintsugi ceramic plate seen from above on dark linen. Paint the subject into the glaze as a simplified ceramic portrait, then break the plate and repair it with branching raised gold lacquer seams. Show joined fragments, fine crazing, pooled glossy glaze, and a handmade rim under directional side light. The portrait must belong to the fired surface, not sit on it like a photograph.", "267-patchwork-quilt": "Render the image as a handmade patchwork quilt portrait made entirely from fabric scraps. Build the subject from flat irregular applique of gingham, florals, denim, and plain cloth, with raised seams, visible running stitches, and meandering quilting lines. Show the quilt draped across a bed so soft folds bend the picture. It must be sewn textile, not a photograph printed onto cloth.", "268-tin-toy": "Render the image as a vintage wind-up tin toy on a worn collector's shelf. Translate the subject into a narrow lithographed pressed-tin figure with folded tabs, crimped seams, rivets, chipped enamel, and a large winding key projecting from the back. Use slightly misregistered printed graphics, oxidized edges, and scratched bare metal under warm raking light. It must read as antique mechanical tin, not vinyl, plush, or a living person.", "269-travel-poster": "Render the image as a 1930s WPA national park travel poster printed by silkscreen. Reduce the subject and landscape to bold flat shapes: angular mountains, towering pine silhouettes, and a strong diagonal trail. Limit the palette to deep forest green, muted teal, ochre, terracotta, and cream, with subtle ink-on-paper grain. The poster itself fills the view, with no title, lettering, or photographed frame.", "270-coloring-book": "Render the image as a children's coloring-book page seen from above and only about half colored. Draw thick clean rounded black outlines, simple shapes, and large empty white areas, with no hatching in the printed lines. Fill part of the drawing with rough wax-crayon scribbles that sometimes cross the lines, and leave the rest untouched paper. Scatter a few real crayons along the page edges.", "271-naturalist-plate": "Render the image as a 19th-century naturalist illustration plate on warm ivory rag paper. Render the subject with fine copperplate engraving, crosshatching, stippling, and restrained hand-applied watercolor, arranged like a specimen study with generous margins and detached detail vignettes. Add thin blank leader lines that imply labels without any letters or numbers. Keep muted scientific color and period draftsmanship, not a photograph.", "272-pysanka": "Render the image as a Ukrainian pysanka, a wax-resist decorated egg resting on embroidered linen. Wrap the shell in fine yellow and cream lines over black and vermilion fields, with geometric bands, triangular teeth, eight-pointed rosettes, and stylized flowers. Place the subject only as a flat folk-art portrait inside an oval medallion, simplified to fit the curve. Show handmade wax-line irregularity and eggshell texture, not gems or metalwork.", "273-mignola": "Render the image as Mike Mignola Hellboy-style comic art in a single gothic panel. Reduce forms to massive interlocking pools of solid black, blocky angular shapes, and extremely economical line detail. Use muted stone gray and desaturated olive with one warm accent, set among fractured arches and heavy shadow. No gradients, photographic modeling, or painterly rendering.", "274-chris-ware": "Render the image as a Chris Ware graphic-novel page. Rebuild the subject as a tiny simplified person with a round geometric head, small features, a blocky torso, and minimal limbs, repeated across many small sequential panels. Use meticulously precise architectural linework, flat muted pastels, rigid diagrammatic subdivisions, and generous cream gutters. No photorealism, gradients, or lettering anywhere on the page.", "275-charles-burns": "Render the image as Charles Burns Black Hole-style comic art. Draw with heavy glossy black brush ink on white paper, eerie smoothly swollen contours, and sculptural high-contrast shapes, with no gray wash, color, or gradients. Set a still, uncanny 1970s suburban mood through patterned interiors, odd elongated shadows, and a tightly cropped frozen pose. Everything must be brush and pen, stark and pristine.", "276-crumb": "Render the image as Robert Crumb-style underground comix. Use obsessively dense pen crosshatching, scratchy ink, thick cartoon outlines, rubbery exaggerated anatomy, heavy legs, and huge clunky shoes on slightly off-white newsprint. Stage a lively observed street of homely caricature and busy hatched detail, entirely in black and white. No color, photorealism, or smooth digital shading.", "277-mad-magazine": "Render the image as a 1970s MAD Magazine caricature in the manner of Mort Drucker. Give the subject a very large expressive head over a comically small body, inside a riotously crowded parody scene of tangled figures. Use loose confident pen-and-ink contours, irregular gestural strokes, and transparent watercolor washes pooling on warm paper. Keep the anatomy elastic and the jokes purely visual, with no lettering.", "278-archie": "Render the image as a classic Archie Comics panel of wholesome 1960s comics. Use smooth assured black brush contours, clean appealing faces, simply stylized anatomy, and bright absolutely flat color with minimal graphic shadows. Stage a friendly soda-fountain diner of cyan, cherry red, a checkerboard floor, and curved vinyl booths in open readable spacing. One thin-bordered panel only, with no gradients or speech balloons.", "279-ec-horror": "Render the image as a 1950s EC horror comic in the Tales from the Crypt tradition. Use a menacing low Dutch angle, ferocious ink hatching, thick jagged contours, and huge pools of black, with a creepy gaunt host peeking in from a corner. Color it in lurid acid yellow, blood red, poisonous green, and violet, printed with coarse Ben-Day dots on pulpy paper. No photorealism and no modern smooth rendering.", "280-alex-ross": "Render the image as Alex Ross fully painted gouache comic art. Render the subject as a photorealistic superhero with a flowing cape, idealized sculptural anatomy, and a monumental heroic pose on a city rooftop. Use opaque velvety pigment, broad brush passages, deep cool shadows, and brilliant dawn backlight against a distant skyline. It must be painted draftsmanship, not a photograph, inked outlines, or halftone dots.", "281-sienkiewicz": "Render the image as Bill Sienkiewicz mixed-media comic art. Build a chaotic collage of ink splatters, paint smears, torn blank paper, scratchy charcoal, and opaque gouache around an expressionistically distorted figure. Use extreme asymmetrical cropping, broken contours, psychological intensity, and daring empty space in black, bone, rust, and one hot accent. No typography, printed scraps, or photorealism.", "282-90s-superhero": "Render the image as a 1990s Jim Lee-style superhero comic splash page. Use hyper-detailed ink crosshatching, angular heroic anatomy, extreme foreshortening, pouches, armor, and a dramatic wind-blown silhouette in a dynamic midair pose. Color it like early computer comic coloring, with saturated separations, hard dramatic shadows, glossy rim light, and radiating speed lines. No photography and no sound-effect lettering.", "283-webtoon": "Render the image as Korean slice-of-life romance webtoon art. Use clean delicate digital line art, soft gradient cel shading, glossy stylized features, and luminous eyes in a spacious airy composition. Favor pastel cream, peach, and muted mint, with translucent window light and simplified furnishings. Keep illustrated manhwa proportions and a vertical reading path, with no photographic realism or dialogue balloons.", "284-scott-pilgrim": "Render the image as a Scott Pilgrim comic in the style of Bryan Lee O'Malley. Draw simple manga-influenced Western cartooning with large round eyes, oversized rounded heads, short simplified bodies, and chunky confident black brush inks. Use economical flat color, big inked shadow shapes, and angular video-game hit sparks that contain no letters or numbers. Keep it funny indie-comic drawing rather than photographic realism.", "285-hilda": "Render the image as a Hilda illustration in the style of Luke Pearson. Use flat graphic shapes, clean confident outlines, and radically simplified cartoon anatomy in a muted teal and burnt-orange palette. Stage an expansive Scandinavian folklore landscape where the subject stays small beside a tiny wood figure, an enormous gentle creature, stylized pines, and large negative space. No photorealism, photographic lighting, or 3D rendering.", "286-hugo-pratt": "Render the image as a Hugo Pratt Corto Maltese adventure comic drawing. Use fluid black brush ink, extremely economical line, sudden solid black shapes, and loose transparent watercolor washes on broad unpainted paper. Favor an asymmetrical off-center crop, elongated proportions, a windswept harbor mood, and watery blue-green with a few rust accents. Allow dry-brush breaks and unfinished paper rather than a filtered photograph.", "287-tezuka": "Render the image as Osamu Tezuka classic 1950s to 1960s manga. Use round simple features, enormous sparkling eyes, rounded rubber-hose anatomy, and bold black contours, in black, white, and gray screentone only. Keep generous white space, playful motion lines, and a small friendly robot companion with rocket boots beside the subject. No actual color, speech balloons, or sound-effect lettering.", "288-stick-figure": "Render the image as a minimalist xkcd-like stick-figure webcomic. Draw sparse black stick lines on solid white: single-line limbs, a simple round head, and almost no detail, with huge blank margins and no panel border. Keep the situation slightly absurd and wordless, using very few pen strokes and no shading, labels, or symbols. The canvas stays opaque pure white out to every edge.", "289-single-panel-gag": "Render the image as a Far Side-style single-panel gag cartoon. Use stubby rounded figures, deliberately awkward anatomy, heavy loose ink outlines, and flat muted colors in dull sage, beige, and brown. Stage a deadpan absurdist situation, such as animals calmly doing a human job, inside a simple rectangular border with no caption. The joke must live entirely in the picture, never in lettering or photographic rendering.", "290-persepolis": "Render the image as Marjane Satrapi Persepolis graphic-novel art. Use only pure black and pure white: bold flat shapes, simple emblematic faces, decorative patterns, and strong carved silhouettes in completely flat graphic space. Translate every color into shape and pattern, with expressive contours and no gray wash or gradients. No photographic anatomy, speech balloons, or lettering.", "291-richard-scarry": "Render the image as a Richard Scarry Busytown illustration. Show a bustling cutaway of a cheerful town full of busy anthropomorphic animals, drawn with delicate lively ink outlines and transparent watercolor. Use whimsical naive architecture, many tiny activities to discover, bright sunny color, and almost no heavy shadow on warm white paper. Keep the figures small, simplified, and wordless, with no photorealism.", "292-sendak": "Render the image as a Maurice Sendak picture-book illustration. Use exquisite fine pen crosshatching and delicate watercolor, richly textured furry monsters, and intensely observed leaves and twisting roots. Set a dreamy moonlit mood that is tender and a little strange, with eccentric creature anatomy and layered theatrical depth. Restrain the washes to midnight blue, moss green, umber, and pale lunar cream.", "293-silverstein": "Render the image as a Shel Silverstein pen drawing. Use loose wobbly black ink lines, whimsical minimal cartoon anatomy, and immense clean white negative space, with the drawing occupying only a modest part of the page. Stretch one gesture into an absurd poetic exaggeration, such as an impossibly long reach. No color washes, shaded realism, or lettering.", "294-gorey": "Render the image as an Edward Gorey illustration in meticulous fine black pen crosshatching. Use Edwardian gloom, stiff theatrical staging, eccentric long proportions, patterned walls, and heavy curtains, with the figure small inside elaborate architecture. Include a long fur coat and a strange little creature half-hidden in the hatching, held in sinister calm. No color, photographic realism, or lettering.", "295-searle": "Render the image as a Ronald Searle caricature. Use wildly spiky scratchy pen line, nervous tangled contours, eccentric elongated anatomy, and explosive but selective watercolor splashes on white paper. Tilt the space into skewed furniture and chaotic overlapping animals, with ink spatters and line that outruns the forms. Keep the humor in the line, using ochre, rust, dirty teal, and dusty rose.", "296-leyendecker": "Render the image as a Joseph Christian Leyendecker magazine-cover painting with no typography. Use glossy sculpted oil and gouache, confident angular planar strokes, bold creamy highlights, and a powerful graphic silhouette. Follow 1920s fashion-illustration proportions: a sculptural head, long elegant neck, and tapering articulated fingers against a restrained geometric ground. Paint in antique gold, copper, cream, emerald, and russet, never as a photograph.", "297-eric-carle": "Render the image as an Eric Carle tissue-paper collage. Build the subject from bold simplified cutouts of hand-painted tissue, with brilliant color, translucent overlaps, visible brush marks, and speckled paint inside each shape. Include a small hungry caterpillar as the defining storybook creature on the white ground. Leave generous white gaps between forms so the image reads as paper collage, not a filtered photograph.", "298-oliver-jeffers": "Render the image as an Oliver Jeffers picture-book illustration. Reduce the subject to a small naive figure with improbably thin stick legs, set low in an enormous almost empty page. Use a gentle muted palette, irregular pencil lines, awkward tender proportions, and quiet watercolor and gouache texture on warm off-white paper. Let the empty space carry a whimsical lonely charm, with no polished realism or lettering.", "299-victo-ngai": "Render the image as a Victo Ngai editorial illustration. Weave the subject into an intricate dreamlike swirl of flowing patterns, where vapor becomes waves and tiny nested cities share one flattened ornamental space. Use elongated graceful anatomy, delicate hatching, granular print texture, and jewel tones of lapis, malachite, ruby, aubergine, and ochre. Every shape should feel meticulously orchestrated, with no realistic lens effects or lettering.", "300-jon-klassen": "Render the image as a Jon Klassen picture-book illustration. Use a muted earthy palette, textured digital gouache, blunt simple shapes, and minimal deadpan faces whose tiny pupils glance sideways. Keep the body language stiff and restrained, the humor quiet, and the background nearly featureless in moss, taupe, russet, and bone. No lush detail, glossy features, dramatic light, or lettering.", "301-goya": "Render the image as one of Francisco Goya's Black Paintings. Paint in raw expressive oil with dark murky browns and blacks, broken scumbling, broad smears, and scraped paint, so forms emerge from a nearly black void. Crowd the subject with grotesque onlookers and coarse distorted faces, using only a few sickly bone-white highlights. It must look like late Goya, never a filtered photograph or a polished portrait.", "302-el-greco": "Render the image as an El Greco Mannerist oil painting. Stretch the subject into radically elongated flickering anatomy: a small narrow head, a serpentine pose, long expressive fingers, and flame-like folds. Set the figure rising above a distant Toledo under a stormy green-gray sky, in acid yellow, sharp green, icy white, and uncanny violet. Use cold supernatural light and agitated vertical brushwork, not naturalistic photography.", "303-gauguin": "Render the image as a Paul Gauguin Tahitian-period oil painting. Build a calm decorative tableau of broad flat color areas enclosed by dark outlines, shallow space, and simplified weighty anatomy. Use warm ochre flesh tones, pink sand, tropical flowers, emerald leaves, and turquoise water in large interlocking fields. Keep the surface matte and still, an authentic Synthetist painting rather than a realistic portrait.", "304-degas": "Render the image as an Edgar Degas pastel on textured paper. Use soft chalky strokes, dry layered hatching, smudged pigment, and restless broken contours, with the paper grain showing through. Crop the subject candidly and off-center from an unusual high viewpoint inside a cafe-concert interior under yellow-green gaslight. Favor smoky plum, dusty teal, ochre, and violet shadows, never smooth oil or a photographic filter.", "305-rousseau": "Render the image as a Henri Rousseau naive jungle painting. Fill a flattened dreamlike thicket with dense stylized leaves and precisely delineated tropical fronds beneath a full moon, and let a tiger peek out. Keep the subject small among the enormous plants, drawn with flat precise oil, awkward anatomy, and clear silhouettes. Use deep greens, moonlit blue, and earthy warm accents, not a photograph with a filter.", "306-grant-wood": "Render the image as a Grant Wood American Regionalist painting. Model every form smoothly, with clean hard contours and the stiff, frontal ceremony of American Gothic. Place a white farmhouse behind gently rounded farmland hills, painted in ocher, olive, and cream under clear country daylight. The surface should feel sculpted and evenly finished, like traditional oil, not a photograph.", "307-pollock": "Render the image as a Jackson Pollock drip painting. Pour looping filaments, thick woven skeins, and flung spatters of enamel across the whole field, leaving glimpses of bare canvas. Let the subject survive only as faint accidental curves inside an almost nonfigurative tangle. There is no focal center and no perspective, and the paint itself generates the image rather than decorating a finished picture.", "308-murakami": "Render the image as Takashi Murakami superflat art. Build it from glossy anime-pop shapes, outlines of one even weight, and unshaded candy color, packed with smiling rainbow flowers. Turn the subject into a cute, radically simplified character with huge sparkling eyes and an oversized head on a small body. Keep the depth perfectly flat, with cropped motifs and sudden scale jumps instead of shading or atmosphere.", "309-pre-raphaelite": "Render the image as a Pre-Raphaelite oil in the manner of John William Waterhouse. Use jewel-like glazes, melancholy romance, and botanical precision, with dense wildflowers mirrored in a still woodland pond. Clothe the subject in richly folded medieval costume and draw with idealized, poetic care, as on a nineteenth-century narrative canvas. The surface should glow with fine layered paint, never with photographic light or a digital filter.", "310-beardsley": "Render the image as an Aubrey Beardsley ink illustration. Draw decadent Art Nouveau with whiplash contours, peacock-feather ornament, and bold flat black shapes against untouched white. Keep the picture strictly black and white, with elongated proportions, theatrical asymmetry, and no gray shading at all. Thin exact lines should trade off with heavy solid masses across a flattened, decorative space.", "311-fauvism": "Render the image as an early Fauvist oil in the spirit of Matisse and Derain. Lay thick loose strokes and bristly impasto in impossible color: green and violet shadows, acid yellows, and a blazing orange-and-cobalt harbor. Let the drawing warp inside a tilted, flattened space. Build the whole image from expressive paint, not from observed light or a color filter.", "312-dexters-lab": "Render the image as a Dexter's Laboratory cartoon. Use angular geometric construction, a huge head on a tiny body, blocky rectangular hands, and black outlines of uneven weight. Fill a secret laboratory with skewed consoles, pipes, and oversized machines in cyan, navy, lavender, and hot orange. Color stays flat and bright, with hard cel shadows only and no gradients or rendered volume.", "313-courage": "Render the image as a Courage the Cowardly Dog cartoon. Stage an eerie, nearly empty Kansas farmhouse with crooked boards and a tiny window onto a barren desert, in dusty mauve and dull ocher. Distort the drawn forms with wobbly ink, let a small pink dog peek in fearfully, and drop in one shiny primitive CGI shape with an unsettling face. Leave wide empty areas and sour, dim light for deadpan horror-comedy.", "314-phineas-ferb": "Render the image as a Phineas and Ferb cartoon. Design characters from triangles and rectangles, with clean angular outlines, long narrow limbs, and bold flat color under sparse cel shadows. Overlook a sunny suburban backyard swallowed by one ridiculous oversized invention. Keep the energy bright and summery, with saturated lawn green and a huge clear sky, and no realistic lighting.", "315-gumball": "Render the image as a frame from The Amazing World of Gumball. Drop a flat 2D cartoon figure, thickly outlined, with giant simple eyes and a huge rounded head on a tiny body, into a photoreal setting. Park a glossy odd 3D character and a fuzzy felt puppet beside it so the media collide. The drawn figure stays unshaded and absurd against real light, texture, and depth of field.", "316-animaniacs": "Render the image as a 1990s Animaniacs cartoon cel. Stretch the pose with manic squash-and-stretch, rubbery expressions, elastic limbs, and a long curved muzzle. Place the action on a studio backlot beneath a round water tower on tall cross-braced legs, with painted palms and a saturated cyan sky. Use snappy thick-to-thin ink and flat cel color, gleefully exaggerated like vintage Warner television animation.", "317-johnny-bravo": "Render the image as a Johnny Bravo cartoon. Exaggerate the design until it is extremely top-heavy: massive shoulders, a sculpted pompadour, tiny hips and legs, dark sunglasses, and chunky angular black outlines. Strut the figure along a beach boardwalk of graphic turquoise water, peach sand, and flat palm shapes. Keep bold unshaded cel color and hard 1990s television sun, with no realistic light or texture.", "318-magic-school-bus": "Render the image as a 1990s Magic School Bus cartoon. Stage a whimsical science field trip inside a gigantic everyday object, crowded with colorful molecule models, bubbles, and diagram-like wonders. Use bouncy simplified drawing, thin slightly wobbly ink, and a bright painted background, and let the outfit bloom into a wild science pattern. The figure stays small against the huge explanatory setting, with no photographic realism.", "319-care-bears": "Render the image as a 1980s Care Bears cartoon. Float the scene on pastel cloud banks under soft rainbows and a gentle airbrushed glow, with plush bears close by, each marked by a simple belly badge. Use round friendly outlines, rosy cheeks, and tender shapes in pink, lavender, baby blue, and butter yellow. It should feel like sweet vintage animation paint, dreamy and soft, not a photograph.", "320-smurfs": "Render the image as a Peyo Smurfs comic cartoon. Use Franco-Belgian clear line, even contours, and gentle flat color in a soft forest of mushroom houses. Gather tiny blue figures in white caps around the subject, keeping them small, simple, and friendly. The picture should feel like a clean printed European children's comic, bright and legible, not painterly or photographic.", "321-inspector-gadget": "Render the image as a 1980s Inspector Gadget cartoon. Use stiff limited animation, flat cel color, and simple dark outlines on a sunny city street. Dress the figure in a trench coat and hat, and let a gadget limb comically extend some ordinary object out of the costume. The drawing stays blocky, bright, and unmistakably early-eighties Saturday-morning animation.", "322-demon-slayer": "Render the image as a Demon Slayer anime frame. Draw crisp contemporary anime with vivid saturated color, sharp cel shadows, and a dramatic sword stance. Swirl ukiyo-e water patterns around the figure like woodblock waves, and allow a checkered haori layer in the costume. The effect should fuse printed Japanese pattern with modern action animation, bold and theatrical.", "323-spy-family": "Render the image as a Spy x Family anime frame. Use clean modern linework, light cel shading, and warm color inside a cozy European apartment of tidy mid-century furniture. Hold an elegant, composed pose in soft interior light, as if in a gentle spy story. A small pink-haired child may peek from the edge, while the drawing stays polished and charming rather than gritty.", "324-chainsaw-man": "Render the image as a Chainsaw Man manga frame brought to anime color. Use gritty near-realistic line, deadpan expressions, and cinematic cropping inside a grimy city. Cut a single blood-red accent through a muted, dirty palette of grays and browns. The mood stays blunt and uneasy, with harsh contrast and unglamorous detail instead of glossy action sparkle.", "325-lain": "Render the image as a Serial Experiments Lain anime frame. Drain the color to a dim, eerie range, with humming power lines and the cold glow of a CRT in a sparse bedroom. Scatter tiny red static specks through flat shadow and large empty areas. The feeling should be uncanny and isolated, like late-1990s wired horror rather than bright television anime.", "326-maruko": "Render the image as a Chibi Maruko-chan anime drawing. Use very simple flat shapes, childlike proportions, short vertical blush strokes, and a gentle pastel palette. Set a modest 1970s Japanese house, its plain rooms and everyday clutter described with a few economical lines. The result should feel naive, warm, and almost diagram-simple, not like polished modern anime.", "327-gundam": "Render the image as a 1980s Gundam mecha anime frame. Show a giant robot in crisp cel shading, its pilot visible in a detailed cockpit against a vast space colony. Use panel lines, hard anime shadows, and the slightly painted look of vintage television cels. The machine dominates the scale while the pilot stays clearly readable inside the mechanical clutter.", "328-madoka": "Render the image as a Madoka Magica witch labyrinth in the manner of Gekidan Inu Curry. Collage cut paper, cookies, scissors, candy, and other craft scraps into a creepy-cute world of mismatched texture and abrupt scale. The subject should look pasted into that handmade set rather than drawn smoothly through it. Keep the mood sweet and threatening, like a stop-motion nursery nightmare.", "329-bubblegum-crisis": "Render the image as a late-1980s anime OVA in the manner of Bubblegum Crisis. Armor the subject in a sleek cyberpunk hardsuit with oversized shoulder plating, airbrushed highlights, and neon reflections from a night city. Add a light veil of VHS grain and the glossy hand-painted look of period cels. Magenta, cyan, and electric violet should burn against deep black, with no modern plastic 3D sheen.", "330-blade-runner": "Render the image as a 1982 Blade Runner film still. Soak the scene in smoky neon noir, steady rain, giant glowing billboards, and stripes of light like Venetian blinds, in amber and teal. Let steam and haze swallow the distance while the subject sits in cinematic shadow. It should look like grimy live-action photography, atmospheric and practical, not like an illustration.", "331-mad-max": "Render the image as a Mad Max: Fury Road film still. Set a blazing orange desert under a teal sky, with a spiked war rig tearing through a roaring dust storm. Use harsh midday sun, high-contrast color, grit on metal, and kinetic camera energy. The image must feel like gritty live-action photography, not a painted poster or a cartoon.", "332-amelie": "Render the image as an Amélie film still. Grade saturated greens and reds over warm golden light, with the whimsical storybook feeling of Montmartre. Soften the background, heighten everyday color, and keep the framing playful and precise. It should look like carefully art-directed live action from a 2001 French film, charming rather than naturalistic.", "333-wong-kar-wai": "Render the image as a Wong Kar-wai film still in the manner of In the Mood for Love. Use step-printed motion blur, saturated reds and greens, and a rainy Hong Kong alley lit by neon and warm tungsten. Let smoke, wet reflections, and smeared slow movement carry a melancholy mood. It should feel like lush 35mm cinematography with intimate, drifting focus.", "334-caligari": "Render the image as a Cabinet of Dr. Caligari film still. Build a painted stage of jagged, impossible architecture, with sharp shadows painted onto the walls and floor. Photograph it in black and white tinted a sickly green, with stark theatrical makeup and angular costume shapes. Space should feel crooked and stage-bound, like 1920s German Expressionist cinema, not a real place.", "335-spaghetti-western": "Render the image as a 1960s spaghetti-western film still. Use harsh sun, a dusty frontier town, sun-bleached timber, and a tight squinting close view against the glare. Add grit, sweat, telephoto compression, and a weathered poncho silhouette in high-contrast color. The mood stays tense and sun-blasted, like a photographed showdown rather than an illustration.", "336-harryhausen": "Render the image as a Ray Harryhausen stop-motion fantasy frame. Confront the subject with a jerky articulated skeleton warrior among sunlit ancient Greek ruins, the creature clearly a physical puppet. Use bright Technicolor and the slight stutter of a miniature composited onto a live-action plate. Scale and contact should feel handmade, charming, and a little uncanny.", "337-struzan": "Render the image as a 1980s painted movie poster in the manner of Drew Struzan. Montage the subject with a few heroic moments in airbrush and colored pencil, edged with soft glow. Use warm rim light, rich smoke, and a dense layered arrangement that still reads as one painted sheet. The finish should feel luminous and hand-rendered, not like a photograph or a digital collage.", "338-atari-2600": "Render the image as an Atari 2600 game screen. Reduce everything to extremely chunky blocks, a handful of flat colors, and simple horizontal bands. The subject becomes a barely readable stack of rectangles on a striped playfield. Keep hard edges and almost no detail, like the earliest home-console graphics, with no anti-aliasing or modern pixel polish.", "339-c64": "Render the image as a Commodore 64 game picture. Use that machine's 16-color palette, wide chunky pixels, and visible dither, framed by a solid blue border. Shapes stay blocky yet busier than the earliest consoles, built from bright primaries, cyan, and brown. It should look like a period home-computer illustration, not sleek modern pixel art.", "340-zx-spectrum": "Render the image as a ZX Spectrum game graphic. Draw jagged one-bit detail, then flood it with bright cyan, magenta, and yellow on black. Let attribute clash smear each color past the shapes it belongs to, in coarse blocks that ignore edges. The result should look like 1980s British microcomputer art, harsh and clashing, not smooth modern pixels.", "341-ff7": "Render the image as a 1997 Final Fantasy VII scene. Place a low-poly, blocky character in a detailed pre-rendered industrial city of pipes, girders, and haze. Use early PlayStation CGI: coarse textures, simple smooth shading, and a backdrop sharper than the figure. The gap between chunky geometry and a moody painted set should be obvious.", "342-botw": "Render the image as a Zelda: Breath of the Wild scene. Use soft painterly cel-shaded 3D, vast green hills, and stylized grass bending in the wind under a bright open sky. A simple paraglider may drift in the distance, drawn with clean toon edges and gentle light. The world should feel airy, legible, and hand-painted rather than realistically textured.", "343-ori": "Render the image as Ori and the Blind Forest game art. Paint a luminous forest in deep blues and teals, with bioluminescent plants and drifting motes of light. Let the subject read as a small glowing presence against layered, atmospheric scenery. Light should bloom softly through darkness, and the finish stays painterly and emotional rather than hard-edged or flat.", "344-limbo": "Render the image as Limbo game art. Turn the subject into a pure black silhouette in a foggy gray forest, with film grain and faint glow along the mist. Only small bright eyes should break the darkness. Keep the world monochrome, shallow, and eerie, like a side-view puzzle scene rather than a detailed illustration.", "345-stardew": "Render the image as Stardew Valley-like farming-game pixel art. Build a cozy, slightly chunky scene of wooden rooms, warm lamplight, and a soft limited palette. Show simple crops just outside and a friendly, readable character sprite. The mood stays gentle and domestic, like a modern indie farm game drawn in clear readable pixels.", "346-overwatch": "Render the image as an Overwatch-like hero character render. Use stylized Blizzard 3D, saturated color, clean readable materials, and a confident pose in exaggerated sci-fi gear. Set a bright Mediterranean-style map behind the figure, sunny and colorful. The finish should feel like a polished game poster, friendly and immediately legible rather than realistic.", "347-kirby-yarn": "Render the image as a world from Kirby's Epic Yarn. Build every form from yarn outlines, visible stitches, buttons, and zippers against felt-fabric scenery. The subject becomes a soft stuffed contour, crafted rather than drawn, inside a textile diorama. Light stays gentle and tactile, as if the set were a photographed craft playset instead of a digital cartoon.", "348-kings-quest": "Render the image as a 1980s Sierra adventure screen in the manner of King's Quest. Use a 16-color EGA palette, chunky pixels, and a small walking sprite before a bright fairytale castle. Build scenery from large flat color regions with simple dither. It should look like an early PC graphic adventure, not high-resolution modern pixel art.", "349-night-in-the-woods": "Render the image as Night in the Woods game art. Simplify the subject into a flat, slightly animal-like cartoon shape with minimal features and solid color. Set a small rust-belt town at dusk, in autumn orange, brown, and muted teal, with glowing windows and quiet streets. The mood stays cozy and melancholy, like an indie story game rather than glossy animation.", "350-blacksad": "Render the image as a Blacksad comic panel. Turn the subject into an elegant anthropomorphic animal in lush watercolor noir, under realistic light, with a smoky 1950s jazz bar behind. Use painterly color and deep shadow, with tailored period clothes on an animal body. The finish should feel like a European graphic novel, not a cartoon and not a photograph.", "351-franquin": "Render the image as an André Franquin comic in the manner of Gaston Lagaffe. Draw wildly dynamic rubbery lines, springy bodies, and flat 1960s Belgian color. Cram a messy office with slapstick clutter and an absurd contraption caught mid-malfunction, motion flying outward. The line should feel spontaneous and elastic, funny and loose, rather than tidy clear-line comic art.", "352-garfield": "Render the image as a Garfield newspaper comic panel. Use simple thick outlines, flat color, and a nearly empty kitchen table, with a fat orange cat planted smugly in front. Keep the drawing economical and deadpan, like one syndicated strip panel rather than a full illustration. Shapes stay bold and readable, with minimal background and no painterly texture.", "353-bone": "Render the image as a Jeff Smith Bone comic drawing. Use the rounded, bouncy cartooning of Pogo and Carl Barks, with fluffy silhouettes and rich black ink on white. Open the scene into a gentle fantasy valley and elastic, expressive characters. Stay strictly black and white, with confident brush line and no gray wash standing in for color.", "354-saga": "Render the image as a Saga comic panel in the manner of Fiona Staples. Use soft painterly digital color, close intimate expressions, and a lived-in space-opera interior. Nearby figures may carry the book's fantasy anatomy, such as horns or small wings, while the focus stays emotional. Light feels contemporary and natural inside an imaginative science-fiction world, without hard comic inks.", "355-watchmen": "Render the image as a Watchmen panel drawn in the manner of Dave Gibbons. Use precise clean ink, flat unmodeled color, and a secondary palette of purple, green, and orange. A plain yellow smiley may sit in the design as a graphic emblem, inside a rigid, grid-like layout. The image should feel like a carefully ruled 1980s American comic panel, cool and diagrammatic.", "356-mafalda": "Render the image as a Mafalda strip by Quino. Use simple round-headed caricatures with childlike proportions, thick clean ink, and black and white only. Set a spare Argentine family kitchen with a small globe on the table, drawn in a few blunt humorous lines. It should feel like a newspaper gag panel, friendly and almost empty of background, not a rendered scene.", "357-little-nemo": "Render the image as a Winsor McCay Little Nemo in Slumberland scene. Build an Art Nouveau dream of giant mushrooms, a bed that walks, and soaring ornamental architecture. Use delicate early-1900s newspaper color, fine line, and gentle impossible scale. The page should feel intricately drawn and softly unreal, like a vintage Sunday comic, not a modern cartoon.", "358-beatrix-potter": "Render the image as a Beatrix Potter watercolor. Use delicate natural-history detail, soft muted greens, and the damp light of the English countryside. Small animals in fitted little jackets may join the scene, with careful fur, leaves, and gentle outlines. The surface should feel like a quiet children's-book watercolor, precise and tender rather than bold or graphic.", "359-rackham": "Render the image as an Arthur Rackham fairy-tale illustration. Lay fine ink over sepia-muted watercolor, and crowd the edges with gnarled living trees, goblins, and fairies. Twisted roots and knotted bark should seem awake, under dim enchanted light. The mood stays eerie and intricate, like an early-1900s storybook plate rather than a bright cartoon.", "360-kay-nielsen": "Render the image as a Kay Nielsen fairy-tale illustration. Draw elegant elongated figures in intricate patterned robes, staged in a snowy fantasy landscape. Use midnight blue, black, and metallic gold, with flat decorative silhouettes and repeating ornament. The space should feel ceremonial and designed, like a 1910s storybook plate, not a naturalistic painting.", "361-ungerer": "Render the image as a Tomi Ungerer picture book. Use a sharp witty ink line and bold flat washes, with mischievous, slightly wicked humor. A few graphic storybook robbers may enter the scene as flat shapes rather than realistic people. Keep the design spare and poster-like, with large fields of unmodulated color and a dry joke in the staging.", "362-charley-harper": "Render the image as a Charley Harper mid-century illustration. Reduce the subject and nearby wildlife, such as cardinals or owls, to flat geometric shapes, exact edges, and clever symmetry. Use an earthy limited palette with no gradients, texture, or realistic shading. The result should feel witty, minimal, and perfectly planned.", "363-tenniel": "Render the image as a John Tenniel engraving for Alice's Adventures in Wonderland. Use fine Victorian line, dense cross-hatching, and black and white only, staged like the Mad Hatter's tea party. The March Hare and a crowded tea table may appear as crisp storybook elements around the subject. The drawing stays precise, slightly grotesque, and printed, never painted or photographic.", "364-golden-books": "Render the image as a 1950s Little Golden Book illustration. Paint cheerful gouache with soft rounded shapes, rosy cheeks, and warm nostalgic color in an idealized suburban home. Edges stay gentle, light is even and friendly, and every form feels simple and cuddly. It should look like a mid-century children's book plate, cozy and printed, not a photograph or a vector graphic.", "365-froud": "Render the image as a Brian Froud faerie illustration. Work in detailed pencil and watercolor, building mossy earth, twisted roots, gnarled faeries, and troll-like creatures. Pack the surface with bark, lichen, wrinkles, and damp stone under a mystical half-light. The subject belongs in that tangled undergrowth, drawn with obsessive natural detail rather than clean cartoon shapes.", "366-elvgren": "Render the image as a 1950s Gil Elvgren pin-up illustration. Paint glossy oil with smooth blending, bright clean color, and a playful surprised look caught mid-mishap. Use polished glamour lighting and a simple vivid background, with the crisp cheerful finish of mid-century calendar art. It should feel like a staged studio painting, highly finished and lighthearted, not a photograph.", "367-isometric-infographic": "Render the image as a polished isometric vector infographic. Use clean flat geometric shapes, crisp edges, and parallel lines from a high thirty-degree view, with no perspective convergence, gradients, or painterly texture. Keep a pastel interface palette of mint, lilac, pale peach, sky blue, and cream, plus restrained dark contours and directional arrows that explain a process through objects alone. Airy spacing should make the diagram self-explanatory.", "368-vogue-editorial": "Render the image as a high-fashion magazine editorial photograph. Use dramatic studio lighting, a low camera angle, and close three-quarter framing against a seamless deep slate-blue backdrop with deep negative space. Hold a sculptural, twisted pose under one hard key light that throws graphic shadows. Render sharp medium-format photography with tactile materials and lacquerlike highlights, never an illustration or a cover layout.", "369-cartier-bresson": "Render the image as a black-and-white 1950s street photograph in the decisive-moment manner of Henri Cartier-Bresson. Shoot it like 35mm silver gelatin: geometric composition, deep focus, slightly gritty grain, and a rich grayscale with no color. Organize the street on strong diagonals so a spontaneous gesture locks into balanced geometry. The figure belongs to the scene rather than posing for the camera.", "370-pinhole": "Render the image as a primitive pinhole-camera long exposure printed on sepia paper. Keep infinite soft focus at every depth, with nothing tack sharp, heavy dark vignetting in all four corners, and diffuse luminous highlights. Use warm brown monochrome, visible matte paper fibers, subtly uneven exposure, and motion as translucent ghosts and smeared trails. The softness must come from the pinhole, not a crisp photo with a sepia overlay.", "371-fisheye": "Render the image as an extreme fisheye photograph with a 1990s skate-video vibe. Use wildly bulging optical distortion, a bent horizon, saturated punchy color, harsh direct daylight, and a little analog video grit. Put the camera inches from the nearest form so it balloons in the foreground while the surroundings taper and bend outward. Tilt the frame and let real ultra-wide perspective govern every edge.", "372-night-vision": "Render the image as raw night-vision camera footage. Render the scene in bright phosphor-green monochrome and near-black, with hard frontal infrared light, glowing eye-shine, coarse sensor noise, and blooming highlights. Crush the background, keep low-resolution video softness, and frame it unevenly from a high corner. It must feel captured by a real infrared device, not a conventional photograph painted green, and it should show no on-screen graphics.", "373-x-ray": "Render the image as a medical X-ray radiograph on deep blue-black film. Show a translucent skeleton in clinical side projection, bones luminous pale icy blue and white, with overlapping structures and soft-tissue attenuation. Analog film grain and density should govern the image, and solid objects appear only as bright radiopaque shapes or faint silhouettes. No opaque face, color portrait, or visible-light photograph remains.", "374-photobooth": "Render the image as a physical vintage 1950s photobooth strip. Show one long narrow strip of exactly four stacked black-and-white frames on a plain dark surface, with curled paper edges, worn silver-gelatin stock, and analog grain. Each frame is a tight head-and-shoulders exposure under harsh booth flash, with a different spontaneous expression and a plain curtain. Keep true monochrome and one continuous strip, not a digital grid.", "375-drone-overhead": "Render the image as a high-resolution drone photograph aimed straight down at ninety degrees. Build a wide top-down scene with no horizon and no eye-level view, using bold geometric ground patterns, vivid photographic color, and long crisp late-afternoon shadows. Keep the subject foreshortened from directly above, readable but small inside the larger layout. Real sunlight, natural materials, and aerial optics should dominate.", "376-wood-relief": "Render the image as a hand-carved walnut relief panel resting on a woodcarver's bench. Describe every form in unpainted walnut: chisel facets, gouge marks, deep undercut shadows, polished highlights, and dark grain. View the panel at an oblique three-quarter angle so thickness and projections read clearly, with a few shavings and tools at the edge. It must be sculpture, not a photograph printed on wood.", "377-papier-mache": "Render the image as a handmade papier-mâché sculpture on a messy art-class table. Give it an oversized lumpy head, a tiny squat body, stubby arms, and an off-kilter pose built from overlapping newspaper strips, torn fibers, and dried paste. Cover the form in bright thick matte acrylic with naive brushstrokes, and leave no readable print. Soft daylight should reveal coarse childlike craft, never a living subject with a filter.", "378-glass-figurine": "Render the image as a miniature lampworked glass figurine on a mirrored plinth. Form everything from fused translucent colored glass, with elongated elegant proportions, trapped air bubbles, rounded fire-polished joins, and colored refraction. Set it against a dark ground with luminous rim light, caustics, and a clear reflection below. It should read as a three-dimensional glass collectible, not plastic and not a living subject under a glass effect.", "379-pancake-art": "Render the image as pancake art cooking on a black griddle, seen straight overhead. Shape the picture from batter alone, using pale cream, honey gold, caramel, and chestnut browning for graphic outlines and detail. Keep caricature proportions, porous cooked surface, browned edges, and tiny bubbles, and let a metal spatula enter at one edge. No paint, icing, or printed portrait.", "380-cardboard": "Render the image as a playful corrugated-cardboard diorama. Build the scene and figure from cut and folded kraft board: angular block bodies, layered cutouts, curled paper strips, and marker details. Expose fluting on every cut edge, along with tabs, scored bends, tape, and uneven glue, in brown and cream with a few color accents. Use a wide stage-like view, a pale background, and hard daylight shadows.", "381-wire-sculpture": "Render the image as a minimalist sculpture bent from one continuous black wire, hanging just in front of a solid white wall. Reduce the subject to an open linear form with empty space inside every shape, elongated proportions, cylindrical thickness, and continuous handcrafted bends. One hard light should cast a razor-sharp offset shadow of that same line across abundant white ground. It is sculpture, not a drawing.", "382-bento": "Render the image as Japanese character bento, kyaraben, photographed straight down in a wooden lunch box. Build an oversized round face and a short stubby body entirely from rice, nori cutouts, glossy seaweed, and other real ingredients, with visible grains and moist texture. Tuck a few vegetable garnishes at the edges under bright lunch-table light. No photographed sitter and no drawn overlay should appear, only edible assembly.", "383-jigsaw": "Render the image as a partially assembled jigsaw of a brushy painting, photographed on a wooden table. Show thick cardboard pieces, interlocking tabs, small bevels, and cast shadows, with the puzzle about two-thirds complete, a ragged unfinished edge, and loose pieces scattered around. The printed picture should look like expressive oil, not a photograph. An open box may repeat the image, with no lettering.", "384-swiss-poster": "Render the image as a mid-century Swiss International Typographic poster that contains no type. Use a strict modular grid, severe asymmetry, a hard photographic cutout, coarse analog halftone, and flat vermilion, black, and white, with huge negative space. Crop the subject into a tall offset block crossed by simple geometric fields and a few hairline rules. Crisp ink on uncoated paper should feel like printed design, not a filtered photograph.", "385-matchbox-label": "Render the image as a vintage Japanese matchbox label, the flat printed sheet itself. Use only two inks on aged warm cream paper: bold, extremely simple flat silhouettes, naive proportions, slight misregistration, and fibrous rubbed edges. Enclose the emblem in a decorative geometric double border with small repeated ornaments, purely pictorial. No photorealism, gradients, or dimensional rendering.", "386-xerox-zine": "Render the image as a handmade photocopied punk zine collage. Fragment the subject across overlapping torn scraps in harsh black and paper white, with one vivid pink highlighter accent. Use jagged scissor cuts, glue seams, enlarged copier grain, roller streaks, crushed shadows, toner dropout, and wonky registration. Ransom-note energy should come from mismatched geometric paper shapes, never from letters.", "387-gig-poster": "Render the image as a screen-printed psychedelic rock poster with no lettering. Limit the inks to midnight purple, burnt orange, and acid yellow on ivory paper. Draw thick flowing contours, chunky interlocking negative space, flat separations, broad halftone dots, slight misregistration, and visible mesh. Stretch the figure into lanky sinuous proportions amid wavy optical bands, with no photographic skin or lighting.", "388-seed-packet": "Render the image as a vintage seed-packet illustration, the flat printed front filling the frame. Use botanical chromolithography on subtly embossed aged ivory paper, inside an ornamental border of flowers and geometry only. Draw with antique stippling, engraved hatchwork, and soft layered color in fern green, cherry red, russet, and muted blue. Keep idealized forms and natural-history detail, with no title ribbons or labels.", "389-minhwa": "Render the image as a Korean minhwa folk painting on textured mulberry paper. Use opaque mineral pigments, fine ink contours, stacked flat planes, and playful jumps in scale. A gnarled pine, a magpie, and a comically wide-eyed striped tiger may share the scene with stylized rocks and ornamental needles. Favor vermilion, malachite green, azurite blue, ochre, and ink black, with naive lively forms rather than realism.", "390-wycinanki": "Render the image as Polish wycinanki, a hand-cut folk paper picture. Build a symmetrical stack of brilliant opaque paper on pure white, with a strong vertical mirror axis, angular scissor cuts, and perforated negative space. Set a stylized central figure between mirrored roosters, fan tails, tulips, and round rosettes. Use crimson, pink, emerald, royal blue, golden yellow, and black, with subtle layered edges and no painted or photographic rendering.", "391-talavera": "Render the image as Mexican Talavera pottery on a round scalloped plate seen face-on. Paint cobalt blue, golden yellow, and rich green mineral glazes on creamy white tin glaze, with floral bands following the rim and a central medallion. Flatten the subject into confident cobalt contours and botanical ornament. Glossy highlights, glaze pooling, and slightly irregular brushwork should reveal curved handmade ceramic.", "392-kalighat": "Render the image as a Kalighat pat painting from Bengal. Use flowing watercolor and opaque pigment on plain warm paper, with sweeping black brush outlines and soft shading inside the forms. Draw one large simplified figure in a sculptural pose, using sinuous contours, an almond-shaped eye, tapered fingers, and rounded limbs. Limit the color to ochre, burnt sienna, ink black, and muted indigo, and leave the ground empty.", "393-otomi": "Render the image as Mexican Otomi, or Tenango, embroidery on white cotton. Fill the cloth with flat folkloric silhouettes in dense satin stitch, each shape one bright thread color, separated by white cloth. Surround the subject with fantastical birds, deer, rabbits, curling stems, and large flowers. Visible weave and individual lustrous stitches, seen straight down, must read as real threadwork rather than paint.", "394-sylvanian": "Render the image as a Sylvanian Families-like flocked miniature in a dollhouse interior. Turn the subject into a small animal toy with fuzzy flocking, an oversized head, a stout body, short limbs, bead eyes, and tiny clothes, among thimble-scale wooden furniture. Use a low close view, cream and sage color, warm window light, and gentle macro focus. Everything must read as a physical animal toy, not a human portrait.", "395-fashion-doll": "Render the image as a glossy plastic fashion doll sealed in a pink retail box. Use elongated toy proportions, a molded vinyl face, rooted synthetic hair, visible joints, and a miniature sewn outfit, shown full-length behind a clear window. Photograph the whole box from a slightly elevated three-quarter angle, with a molded accessory tray and no writing. Pink surroundings and crisp highlights should make it a packaged toy.", "396-polly-pocket": "Render the image as a 1990s Polly Pocket-like micro playset. Show an open palm-sized scalloped clamshell in bright pink and teal plastic, hinge and all, from a near-overhead angle with space around it. Mold architectural detail into the interior, with retro painted color, light scuffs, and parting lines. Keep the figure truly tiny and stubby, with a dotlike painted face, so the toy world stays the focus.", "397-peg-doll": "Render the image as a hand-painted wooden peg doll on a Montessori shelf. Use one turned cylinder, rounded shoulders, and a large spherical head, with features and clothing painted in matte craft color over pale wood grain. Show the whole doll in a birch cubby from a low three-quarter view. Quiet natural wood, a few painted accents, and gentle side light should keep it a simple toy, not a realistic figure.", "398-plush": "Render the image as a handmade soft plush toy. Build it from minky fabric, an embroidered face, looped yarn hair, visible seams, and squashy abbreviated limbs, with a small knitted garment. Lay the doll on a cushion in an intimate overhead view so pile, stitching, and yarn read under soft diffuse light. Stuffed-animal proportions and a cozy muted palette should replace any realistic anatomy.", "399-isometric-room": "Render the image as an isometric 3D diorama of a cube-shaped cutaway room. Show the full floor slab and two walls from an elevated orthographic angle, isolated on a pale ground, with the figure kept small inside the miniature interior. Use matte clay-like materials, soft bevels, and a pastel mint, peach, lavender, and cream palette. Soft global illumination and ambient occlusion should feel like a polished architectural render.", "400-puffy-3d": "Render the image as an inflated puffy 3D render in which every form is glossy balloon vinyl. Use swelling rounded volumes, heat-welded seams, pearlescent highlights, an oversized head, a tiny torso, and inflated limbs caught mid-bounce. Float a simple aqua-to-lilac-to-pink gradient behind it, with a soft shadow and broad softbox reflections. No realistic anatomy, skin, or matte material.", "401-cloud-formation": "Render the image as a photograph of a real sky in which cumulus clouds form the silhouette. Keep genuine cloud volumes, irregular wisps, and airy gaps, with no solid body. Shoot upward at golden hour so edges glow silver and peach, shadows stay soft, and plenty of blue sky remains. A tiny distant airplane may give scale, and the result must look meteorological, not a person under a cloud filter.", "402-constellation": "Render the image as an antique celestial chart set in a deep navy sky with a faint Milky Way. Build the subject only from bright stars joined by thin golden lines, leaving navy empty inside the figure. Add orbital arcs, compass rosettes, and an engraved circular frame, with no labels or numerals. Keep a restrained navy, gold, and copper palette and the fine touch of astronomical engraving.", "403-watermelon-carving": "Render the image as intricate Thai fruit carving, photographed as food craft. Carve the subject in shallow relief into one watermelon, using striped green rind, pale inner rind, and red flesh, ringed by layered lotus petals and leaves. Show precise knife cuts, moist translucent edges, and undercut rind, with no paint. A close oblique view, warm light, and blurred banquet linen should keep the melon the object.", "404-coffee-bean-mosaic": "Render the image as a mosaic made entirely of whole roasted coffee beans on coarse burlap. Use only natural roast colors, from tawny gold through reddish brown to espresso black, and let bean direction and density build coarse simplified shapes. Photograph it from above in macro, with hard raking light so every bean casts a small shadow. No paint, printed portrait, or other materials.", "405-moss-art": "Render the image as living moss wall art on weathered pale concrete. Grow the portrait from mixed mosses and lichens in many greens, using rusty and ochre species for warmer notes, with irregular edges and bare wall left visible. Show fine strands, tiny leaves, and dew under raking overcast light. It should feel like dimensional urban eco art, not a photograph with a moss filter.", "406-shadow-sculpture": "Render the image as a gallery shadow installation. A messy pile of ordinary junk, lit by one small hard projector, should look meaningless from the camera, while its cast shadow becomes a razor-clear silhouette of the subject. Keep the pile low, the wall broad, and projector, objects, and shadow in a readable line. The portrait exists only as black shadow, not as a projected picture.", "407-holo-card": "Render the image as an ultra-rare holographic trading card, photographed at a tilted angle on a dark glossy surface. Give it rounded corners, a metallic border, embossed detail, and rainbow foil with star sparkles and bright specular streaks. Illustrate the subject in collectible style: exaggerated proportions, large eyes, graphic contours, and jewel-like cel shading. A prismatic glint must cross both picture and frame, with no lettering anywhere.", "408-times-square": "Render the image as a night photograph of a giant LED billboard on a busy neon square. Look sharply up from the street so the wraparound screen fills a tower, and show the subject only on that display. Individual diodes, a black pixel grid, panel seams, and light spill must read up close, with tiny crowds and taxis on the wet street below. Surrounding screens stay abstract light, with no readable words.", "409-arm-tattoo": "Render the image as a fresh American traditional tattoo on a forearm, shot in a studio. Use heavy black outlines, simplified shapes, flat red, yellow, cream, and black, and solid shadow blocks in a compact curving design. Photograph it in tight oblique macro, with ointment sheen, pores, slight redness, and skin curvature bending the ink. The picture exists only as pigment in skin, not as a person behind the arm.", "410-da-vinci-notebook": "Render the image as a page from a Leonardo da Vinci notebook. Cover aged parchment with sepia ink studies of the same subject from several angles, plus silverpoint guidelines, searching contours, and fine cross-hatching. Add one anatomical detail study and a small flying-machine sketch, with a few illegible mirror-writing squiggles that are not real letters. Mixed scales, empty margins, and worn edges should feel like a working sheet.", "411-mars-rover": "Render the image as a NASA Mars rover photograph. Use a low, slightly tilted wide view over rusty regolith, rocks, and a pale salmon-gray sky, with wheel tracks in the foreground, mild sensor grain, and harsh small-sun shadows. Keep the subject small in the empty middle distance and the color plain and scientific. No cinematic grading, telemetry, or mission markings.", "412-museum-diorama": "Render the image as a natural-history museum diorama seen through glass. Present the subject as a life-size display figure with waxy skin, fixed glassy eyes, and a stiff arranged pose, in front of a curved hand-painted habitat. Include the case frame, warm interior spotlights, cool gallery reflections, and faint silhouettes on the glass. The result should feel deliberately artificial, not like a candid photograph.", "413-gingerbread": "Render the image as an edible gingerbread scene under powdered sugar. Build the subject as a thick cookie figure with piped icing features and a simplified charming silhouette, beside a gingerbread house trimmed with royal icing, gumdrops, and candy canes. Show biscuit pores, browned edges, raised icing, and translucent candy in warm display light. Everything is confection, with no realistic skin.", "414-knitting-chart": "Render the image as a knitting colorwork chart next to the unfinished piece it charts. Render the subject as a strict grid of colored squares, each cell holding a small symbol such as a dot, slash, or diamond, so anatomy stays blocky. Beside the paper, show that same motif in stranded knitting on live needles, with balls of yarn around it. Even craft-room light should separate flat paper from soft stitches, with no letters or numbers.", "415-playing-card": "Render the image as a vintage European court playing card. Use a double-headed figure, perfectly mirrored top to bottom and joined at the waist, on aged ivory stock inside an ornate border. Draw flat black contours, ceremonial robes, and graphic anatomy in only red, blue, and yellow, with slight misregistration and no shading. Replace indices with small pictorial emblems, and include no letters or numerals.", "416-cameo": "Render the image as a shell cameo brooch in an open velvet box. Carve a crisp side-profile bust entirely in ivory relief on a peach ground, with classical proportions, deep undercuts, and tiny tool marks. Set it in a pierced gold filigree oval against deep wine velvet. Intimate raking light should catch luminous shell and fine wire, with no multicolor portrait and no lettering.", "417-coin": "Render the image as a minted gold coin in extreme macro. Model the subject as a shallow profile relief on the field, ringed by a laurel wreath and a plain rim, with the reeded edge visible. Show struck metal, satin fields, light wear, and hairline scratches under raking light, with deep gold shadow and amber highlights. Use numismatic proportions rather than a living face, and include no letters or numbers.", "418-chess-piece": "Render the image as a hand-carved boxwood chess queen standing on a board mid-game. Turn a tall spindle and a ringed base, and reduce the subject to a compact bust finial with a small coronet, glossy varnish, and visible grain. Surround it with opposing dark pieces and shoot from board height with very shallow focus. Warm side light on honey wood should make it read as one playable piece.", "419-ship-in-bottle": "Render the image as a miniature scene sealed in a clear horizontal bottle with a cork. Lay the whole bottle diagonally on a weathered nautical desk, and build the interior from matchsticks, wire, thread, and visible tweezer-scale joints. Let refraction and long reflections bend the contents, and keep the figure doll-like and fully inside the glass. A coil of rope and an unmarked compass may sit nearby.", "420-terrarium": "Render the image as a living miniature world inside a rounded glass terrarium. Fill the glass with moss hills, tiny ferns, stones, visible soil, and drifting mist, and keep the subject small within that landscape. Shoot through the curved front near moss level, with condensation and bright droplets and one clearer patch. Cool diffuse light should make the biosphere the main subject.", "421-underwater": "Render the image as an authentic underwater photograph on a sandy sea floor. Use blue-green water, rising bubbles, suspended particles, a few curious fish, and sun shafts that throw caustics. Frame it wide from a low side angle, with refraction and natural submerged color, and no dry-land background. The water should decide softness, lighting, and depth.", "422-fireworks": "Render the image as a long-exposure photograph of fireworks in a night sky. Form the subject only from sparks, embers, and broken trails, with dark gaps instead of solid skin, above small crowd silhouettes. Add extra colored bursts, smoke lit by the blasts, bloom, and falling spark paths. Explosive, imperfect physics should matter more than literal anatomy.", "423-drone-show": "Render the image as a night photograph of a drone light show over a skyline and a still lake. Build the subject from hundreds of separate glowing points with black sky between them, a volumetric cloud of drones rather than solid surfaces or neon tubes. Use ordered spacing, copper and gold lights against cool blue night, and a clear reflection below. No firework trails or smoke.", "424-crop-circle": "Render the image as an aerial photograph of a giant crop circle in wheat. Form the subject only from standing and flattened stalks, in straw gold, ochre, and brown shadow, inside a broad circular border. Use simplified geometric shapes, spirals, and chevrons, with tractor lines crossing the field and real stalk texture. Shoot from high above at a slight angle in late-afternoon sun, with no paint and no solid body.", "425-maya-stela": "Render the image as an ancient Maya limestone stela standing in a jungle clearing. Carve the subject as a richly adorned ruler in strict profile, with stylized proportions, deep relief, dense ornament, and eroded edges. Add lichen, trailing vines, and faint mineral pigment. Frame the figure with square panels of abstract geometric marks that suggest inscription blocks but contain no real glyphs.", "426-gargoyle": "Render the image as a Gothic stone gargoyle on a cathedral ledge. Compress the subject into a crouching grotesque with an exaggerated face, heavy hands, bent limbs, and small folded wings. Use weathered limestone, chips, moss, and black rain streaks, with water dripping from a spout and a steep view down to a tiny city. It should be craggy architectural carving, not a living portrait.", "427-royal-icing-cookie": "Render the image as a decorated sugar cookie on a metal baking tray, seen from above. Translate the subject into royal icing: flooded color, raised piped outlines, wet-on-wet swirls, fine lines, and a little edible glitter, with the golden baked edge showing. A couple of tiny companion cookies may sit close by. Warm bakery light should reveal satiny relief and small handmade flaws, not a photographic face.", "428-early-cgi": "Render the image as a frame of primitive 1995-era early 3D CGI. Use faceted low-poly geometry, blocky sculpted masses, simple repeating bitmap textures, and overly glossy Phong materials with hard specular highlights. Limbs become elongated and primitive, hands oversized, and shadows turn into harsh ray-traced edges without global illumination. The result should feel awkwardly synthetic and technically limited, not like a modern physically based render.", "429-wireframe": "Render the image as early-1980s vector wireframe 3D graphics. Draw only luminous monochrome green edges on deep black, with hidden-line removal, unfilled faces, and coarse straight-segment geometry. Add a faint CRT phosphor bloom and leave broad black negative space. It must read as a sparse primitive computer model, not a shaded render or a neon outline traced over a photograph.", "430-vectrex": "Render the image as an early-1980s Vectrex vector arcade screen. Use a very small count of intensely bright white-blue electron-beam strokes on a pitch-black curved CRT, with tiny bright joints, soft phosphor trails, and local transparent color overlays. Keep exaggerated, extremely simple arcade-vector proportions and conspicuous empty black space. This is pure 2D line graphics, not a polygonal 3D wireframe or a neon illustration.", "431-tamagotchi": "Render the image as the tiny monochrome sprite on a 1990s egg-shaped virtual-pet toy. Show the warm amber plastic shell, three round buttons, and a short ball-chain in a close oblique product view, the oval toy filling the frame. On the green-gray LCD, reduce the subject to a coarse single-color pixel pet with a comically large head and a tiny squat body, hard square pixels, and no antialiasing.", "432-pinball-backglass": "Render the image as flamboyant 1970s pinball-machine backglass art. Paint bold inked contours, flat spot colors, and selective airbrush highlights on glossy reverse-painted glass, lit from behind by warm bulbs with hotspots and a reflective sheen. Use heroic low-angle glamour proportions, sweeping diagonals, and radiating coral, cobalt, gold, and ivory starbursts, stars, and zigzag rays. A thin chrome cabinet edge may frame the glowing panel.", "433-arcade-side-art": "Render the image as 1980s arcade-cabinet side art. Paint a flamboyant hand-airbrushed science-fiction scene across the large side panel, with only slim chrome edges and colored arcade-light reflections at the border. Use chunky heroic proportions, candy-colored lacquer, razor stencil highlights, and broad sprayed gradients. Planetary rings, starbursts, crystalline mountains, and a huge orange planet fill a deep cobalt and violet sky.", "434-anaglyph-3d": "Render the image as a red-cyan anaglyph 3D horror comic with a 1950s monster-movie mood. Draw vigorous brush blacks and gray halftone screens on pulpy cream paper, then offset red and cyan outlines so the doubled channels create real stereoscopic disparity. Push the foreground toward the viewer with extreme foreshortening and a stronger channel offset than a distant looming rubber-suit monster. The picture is the doubled anaglyph itself, not someone wearing 3D glasses.", "435-stereoscope": "Render the image as a Victorian stereoscope card, a gently bowed buff mount holding two nearly identical sepia albumen prints with arched tops. Match both views except for a subtle horizontal parallax shift, with soft period-lens grain, long-exposure stillness, and a silver-brown tonal range. Set the curved card on dark wood beside an antique wooden-and-brass stereoscope viewer with a binocular hood and sliding rail.", "436-glitch": "Render the image as datamoshed glitch art. Tear the picture into rectangular temporal-compression mosaics, horizontal pixel-sorting streaks, displaced JPEG macroblocks, and severe RGB channel splits. Let forms melt into checkerboard gaps, torn scanlines, quantization banding, and chroma noise, with lime, magenta, and cyan bleeding against deep black. Keep one fragment readable while the rest collapses into corrupted video artifacts.", "437-deep-dream": "Render the image as a 2015 DeepDream neural-network hallucination. Cover every surface with fractal dog faces, repeated eyes at many scales, and crunchy hyper-patterned paisley swirls. Use iridescent acid teal, electric blue, coral, gold, and violet, with stretched forms and impossible organic architectures. The whole frame should feel like an early convolutional dream, dense and psychedelic rather than a lightly filtered photograph.", "438-spectrogram": "Render the image as a scientific audio spectrogram in which the frequency heatmap itself forms the subject. Use only a magma colormap, from near-black and deep purple through magenta and orange to yellow-ivory peaks, on a flat plotting canvas. Place a large rectangular plot with thin blank axes, a faint graticule, and a small unlabeled color scale. Forms arise from glowing harmonic bands and hot clusters, not from drawn outlines.", "439-emoji-mosaic": "Render the image as a photomosaic built entirely from thousands of tiny colorful emoji icons on a tight regular grid. At a distance the icon colors assemble the subject; up close every region is distinct miniature pictograms such as faces, hearts, animals, fruit, and stars. Keep the tiles crisp and flat, with no continuous photograph hiding underneath. The likeness must come only from icon choice, color, and spacing.", "440-spreadsheet-art": "Render the image as spreadsheet cell art, a flat screenshot whose picture is made only by background fills of tiny perfectly square cells. Show crisp thin gray gridlines, blank row and column header strips, an empty corner selector, and faint scrollbars. Use stepped blocky edges, a limited flat palette, and uncolored worksheet around the motif. No gradients, pasted photographs, or shading that crosses a cell boundary.", "441-dot-matrix": "Render the image as a 1980s dot-matrix banner printout on pale continuous tractor-feed paper. Build the subject only from coarse impact-printer dots and ASCII-like dot densities, with faded ribbon ink, uneven horizontal passes, missing dots, and pale ribbon bands. Include perforated sprocket strips and faint paper folds, viewed straight on so the sheet itself is the object. Monochrome dot density replaces color, and it should look designed for the printer rather than filtered from a photograph.", "442-receipt": "Render the image as a black thermal-receipt printout. Show a long narrow ivory receipt curling into a loose arch across a counter, seen from a steep overhead angle, with a torn end and blank margins. Print the subject as a bold simplified silhouette of coarse error-diffusion dither, white gaps, thermal streaking, and locally faded heat marks. Colors become black-dot densities only, and the narrow curling paper forces a compressed vertical layout.", "443-topographic": "Render the image as an unlabeled topographic hiking map in which elevation contours themselves form the subject. Use dense continuous brown contour lines, winding blue rivers, dashed trails, pale sage woodland, subtle hillshading, and a faint square grid on cartographic paper. Every feature must arise from nested ridgelines and terrain conventions, not from an outlined drawing with map lines laid on top. Keep muted russet, ochre, sage, and river-blue inks in a flat orthographic view.", "444-subway-map": "Render the image as a schematic transit map that itself forms the subject. Use only solid colored routes running horizontally, vertically, and at 45 degrees, with evenly spaced station dots and hollow interchange circles on white. Give the figure the radically distorted geometry the network requires, and let feeder routes run out into blank space. No shading, texture, filled surfaces, or illustration underneath the diagram.", "445-circuit-board": "Render the image as a fabricated printed circuit board whose copper traces, solder pads, and components form the subject. Use a rich green solder mask, silver solder, vias, and tiny unmarked parts, with one small LED glowing as an accent. Shoot it as an extreme oblique macro of a real board, tactile and sharply resolved at the center, with shallow focus across the peripheral circuitry. The likeness is circuitry, not paint or skin.", "446-rubber-stamp": "Render the image as handmade rubber-stamp printmaking. Show a hand-carved stamp face-up on kraft paper beside several uneven red and blue impressions and an open ink pad, photographed overhead in soft daylight. Reduce the motif to a bold simplified carving with chunky contours, printed with patchy ink, broken edges, and uneven pressure. Color becomes monochrome carved shape rather than full-color rendering.", "447-spirograph": "Render the image as a genuine Spirograph gel-pen drawing. Construct every part from overlapping hypotrochoid and epitrochoid curves in red-orange, gold, cyan, magenta, and green, with airy white gaps and no solid fills. Show toothed plastic gears and metal pins lying on the white sheet in a slight overhead view. The subject should emerge only from interlocking floral wheels and thin mathematical pen lines, never from conventional contours.", "448-frottage": "Render the image as surreal frottage, dry crayon and graphite rubbings on thin ivory paper. Build the subject from textured impressions of leaves, coin rims, and wood grain, with irregular pressure, branching veins, growth rings, and rough uncertain edges. Use graphite gray, burnt sienna, and muted ochre, leaving much of the thin paper untouched so wrinkles and fibers stay visible. It should feel like a flattened rubbing, not a painted portrait or a photograph.", "449-soviet-metro-mosaic": "Render the image as a Moscow metro ceiling mosaic in heroic socialist-realist style. Build the scene entirely from irregular opaque smalti tesserae, with a luminous gilded ground, white sculpted stucco framing, and ornate chandeliers at the edges. Use monumental idealized anatomy, a foreshortened upward view into the vault, and a rich cobalt, ochre, earth-red, gold, and cream palette with visible grout and glinting glass. The figure exists only as mosaic set into the ceiling.", "450-heraldry": "Render the image as a medieval heraldic coat of arms painted on aged vellum. Use flat bold tinctures of gules, azure, sable, gold or, and argent, with precise dark ink outlines, opaque tempera, and restrained gold leaf. Arrange a central shield with one bold charge, a tournament helm, torse, curling acanthus mantling, a crest, and a completely blank banner scroll. Any supporter should be an elongated, stiff, hieratic profile figure, not a realistic body pasted onto the emblem.", "451-sarcophagus": "Render the image as an ancient Egyptian painted mummy-sarcophagus lid. Show a rigid frontal wooden effigy under warm museum spotlights against a nearly black ground, with idealized almond eyes, symmetrical immobilized anatomy, a broad winged pectoral, and incised geometric borders. Use worn gold leaf, lapis blue, and carnelian red, with cracks and paint losses. Decorative bands may echo hieroglyph spacing but stay abstract and nonlinguistic, so the result remains a carved coffin rather than a living subject.", "452-terracotta-warrior": "Render the image as a life-size terracotta warrior statue standing in a dusty archaeological pit. Fire the whole subject as weathered earthenware with clay seams, chips, dust, and scant pigment remnants, in stiff upright Qin sculptural proportions among receding ranks of similar statues. Use muted clay tones and diffuse excavation daylight between earthen trenches. Armor, features, and any held objects are sculpted clay, not a living subject in costume.", "453-alebrije": "Render the image as an Oaxacan alebrije, an elaborately carved and hand-painted wooden folk sculpture. Transform the subject into a fantastical creature hybrid with wings, a long curling tail, small animal feet, and a stylized face, shown from an elevated three-quarter angle. Cover every surface in meticulous dots, scallops, florals, and geometry in electric turquoise, magenta, orange, cobalt, lime, and gold. Leave visible wood grain, chisel facets, and slight paint irregularities against a plain deep-blue wall.", "454-wayang-kulit": "Render the image as an Indonesian wayang kulit leather shadow puppet, seen from the puppeteer's side. Use a strict profile, an impossibly narrow elongated torso, a pointed nose, a delicately downcast eye, and extremely long thin jointed arms with rivets and control rods. Pierce the painted leather so warm pinpoints shine through gold leaf, oxblood, and black ornament against a glowing ivory screen, with a faint silhouette on the cloth. Anatomy stays flat puppet construction, not flesh.", "455-kokeshi": "Render the image as a Japanese kokeshi doll, a small lacquered wooden object on tatami. Give it a completely limbless lathe-turned cylindrical body and an oversized round head, painted with simple closed smiling eyes, a tiny red mouth, and spare floral motifs. Show honey-colored wood grain under thin lacquer, circular lathe rings, and restrained brushwork, with soft shoji light and woven straw behind. No arms, hands, legs, or feet; the body stays one uninterrupted cylinder.", "456-parade-balloon": "Render the image as a giant inflatable parade balloon floating between skyscrapers. Build it from shiny sewn fabric panels with welded seams, soft creases, vinyl highlights, and comically exaggerated rounded proportions: a huge simplified head and short puffy limbs. View it from street level looking steeply upward, with taut tether ropes running to small handlers and crowds along the sidewalks. It must read as hollow air-filled fabric in crisp daylight, never a real giant figure.", "457-porcelain-figurine": "Render the image as an eighteenth-century Meissen-style hard-paste porcelain figurine inside a dark walnut cabinet. Model a full-length rococo figure on an ornate pierced scrollwork base, with a tiny head, a graceful curved pose, delicate fingers, painted gilt flourishes, and a luminous ivory glaze. Use exquisite enamel brushwork, pastel ornament, sharp specular highlights, and a little worn patina under restrained museum lighting. The subject is fired glazed ceramic, not a living sitter.", "458-scarecrow": "Render the image as a handmade scarecrow in a pumpkin field at autumn sunset. Build it from straw tufts, a stuffed burlap head with a stitched smile, oversized weathered clothing leaking straw at the cuffs, and a tied cloth body lashed to a crooked wooden post. Emphasize burlap weave, yarn, dry straw, cracked wood, and lumpy folk construction, seen low and wide above pumpkin rows. It is an agricultural effigy, never someone wearing a scarecrow costume.", "459-hand-shadows": "Render the image as hand-shadow puppetry on a plain plaster wall. Place exactly two real hands large in the lower foreground, contorted between a small warm lamp and the wall so they cast one enlarged simplified silhouette. Keep the finger shapes visible and a little optically ambiguous, with an amber light pool, deep brown-black shadow, grainy plaster, and a soft penumbra. The wall image is only cast shadow, never a projection, painting, or paper cutout.", "460-soap-bubble": "Render the image as an extreme macro photograph of one giant iridescent soap bubble. Fill the frame with the spherical film, flowing rainbow interference bands of magenta, cyan, gold, and violet, and a razor-thin glistening rim. Reflect the subject stretched, curved, and rippled inside the membrane, merged with the film rather than inset as a sharp picture. Soft circular park bokeh sits behind, with shallow macro focus and transmitted daylight.", "461-hilma-af-klint": "Render the image as a Hilma af Klint esoteric abstract painting. Dominate the canvas with monumental symmetrical spirals, petal forms, concentric circles, and spiritual diagrams in pastel pink, ochre, and blue. Integrate the subject gently as a small hieratic emblem whose shapes echo that geometry, not as a conventional portrait parked on an abstract background. Use flat opaque and translucent washes on subtly aged canvas, mystical and deliberately non-naturalistic.", "462-klee": "Render the image as a Paul Klee painting. Use playful childlike wandering lines, uneven grids of soft colored squares, tiny arrows and moons, and translucent watercolor on coarse burlap. Reduce the subject to a small elongated, tenderly awkward figure inside a flattened miniature world of scratchy marks and bleeding pigment. Keep a muted palette of peach, dusty blue, moss, ochre, and faded rose, with open areas of rough fabric showing through.", "463-miro": "Render the image as a Joan Miró surrealist painting. Float biomorphic shapes, stars, eyes, and squiggles across a spacious pale ground, tied by confident black lines and bold pools of red, blue, and yellow. Translate the subject into a weightless abstract figure of looping tendrils, spidery limbs, and symbolic dots, with vastly distorted scale. The surface should be flat hand-painted pigment, airy and elastic, never a realistic face or a photographic filter.", "464-lucian-freud": "Render the image as a Lucian Freud oil portrait. Build heavy impasto flesh from thick Cremnitz white and dense loaded-brush marks, with unflinching intimate realism, awkward foreshortening, and the weight of a body slumped in a worn studio chair. Use a scuffed bare floor, stained walls, earthy grays and ochres, and cold natural window light. Every area must be sculptural oil paint, never a photograph with a painterly filter.", "465-durer-woodcut": "Render the image as an Albrecht Dürer Northern Renaissance woodcut. Carve meticulous dense black linework, precise parallel and cross-hatching, angular contours, and curling drapery on ivory paper, so every tone comes from ink and unprinted paper. Set the subject in a dramatic wide landscape of swirling clouds, a winding river, rocky hills, trees, and a small walled town. No grayscale wash and no photographic rendering.", "466-floral-portrait": "Render the image as a contemporary heroic oil portrait in the manner of Kehinde Wiley. Paint the subject with hyperreal flesh and clothing, posed with the regal authority of an aristocratic portrait, against a dense flattened field of ornamental blossoms, emerald leaves, and gold stems. Let several vines pass impossibly in front of the figure. Use rich oil glazing and sculptural light, with no realistic depth in the floral ground.", "467-squigglevision": "Render the image as a Squigglevision cartoon still, in the nervous hand-drawn language of Home Movies and Doctor Katz. Use constantly jittering, slightly doubled redrawn contours, thin inconsistent pen strokes, crude awkward shapes, and simple flat colors. Stage a deadpan side-on conversation at a sparse kitchen table, with a minimal background and muted everyday color. Contours should look redrawn every frame, not fuzzy, smeared, or polished into vector curves.", "468-rockos-modern-life": "Render the image as a Rocko's Modern Life cartoon from 1990s Nickelodeon. Use aggressively warped perspective, thick-and-thin angular ink, acidic lime, chartreuse, salmon, and turquoise, and a crooked suburban interior full of small grotesque details. Give the subject lanky rubbery limbs, a strong ridiculous silhouette, and flat cel color against a painted, buckling background. It should feel zany and off-balance, not like clean modern vector art.", "469-ed-edd-eddy": "Render the image as an Ed, Edd n Eddy animation frame. Draw boiling wobbly ink outlines with irregular double strokes, flat unshaded cel colors, crude elastic anatomy, an enormous expressive mouth, and blocky bent limbs. Set the scene in a skewed suburban cul-de-sac of flat pastel houses under a solid pale cyan sky with two simple cream clouds. Keep the drawing graphic and fully opaque, with no realistic shading or polished vector finish.", "470-tekkonkinkreet": "Render the image as a Studio 4°C Tekkonkinkreet animation frame. Fill the view with a chaotic hand-drawn city of ramshackle rooftops, water tanks, tangled wires, patched sheet metal, and weathered concrete, bent by a huge warped fisheye. Use gritty pencil texture and warm dusty ochres, rust reds, and muted turquoise. Place the subject in a daring airborne leap across the upper foreground, with exaggerated foreshortened limbs and eccentric hand-drawn anatomy, not glossy 3D or generic modern anime.", "471-amano": "Render the image as Yoshitaka Amano fantasy concept art. Draw extraordinarily fine calligraphic ink contours and wispy dry-brush trails on luminous ivory paper, with translucent watercolor, pale lavender, celadon, and faint rose washes, plus delicate gold touches. Elongate the subject into a graceful narrow figure in an elaborate ornamental costume with drifting ribbons, surrounded by large unpainted areas. It should feel like a haunting RPG design sketch, never a photo or a polished digital portrait.", "472-fantasia": "Render the image as a hand-painted frame from Fantasia (1940). Visualize symphonic movement through dancing mushrooms in sweeping arcs, glowing fairies, and abstract bands of magical pink and cyan light against a velvety dark violet forest. Use rich jewel-tone Technicolor, soft multiplane painted backgrounds, and crisp traditional cel shapes with rounded 1940s animated construction. No musical notation, photographic texture, or contemporary anime finish.", "473-svankmajer": "Render the image as a Jan Švankmajer surreal stop-motion frame. Assemble a cramped Czech cabinet of curiosities from tangible puppets and animated objects: wax, wood, coarse fiber, splinters, patinated metal, loose teeth, and old toy parts under raking tungsten light. Use irregular puppet anatomy, odd scale, hard eerie shadows, and a faded umber and sickly-cream palette. It must feel like a physical object set, unsettling and tactile, not glossy CGI.", "474-cartoon-saloon": "Render the image as Cartoon Saloon animation in the tradition of The Secret of Kells and Wolfwalkers. Use flat Celtic-inspired geometry, visible pencil construction lines, decorative knotwork, swirling stylized forest branches, and interlocking patterned shapes. Give the subject an elongated simplified silhouette and an angular profile, small inside a large circular canopy of green, teal, ochre, and russet on pale parchment. Light stays graphic, with no volumetric shading, photo texture, or lens effects.", "475-game-and-watch": "Render the image as a 1980s LCD handheld in the Game & Watch manner. Show a worn cream-and-burgundy dual-screen clamshell gripped in a hand, with small rubber buttons and a visible hinge. On the gray-green panels, lay fixed opaque black segmented sprites over a permanently printed colorful background, including a few faint dormant segments. The on-screen subject becomes a tiny squat arcade mascot frozen in a static pose, never full-color pixels or a modern display.", "476-papers-please": "Render the image as Papers, Please game art. Draw a grim low-resolution pixel scene of a cramped border-checkpoint booth, using crisp square pixels, hard stepped edges, and a restricted palette of slate gray, dirty olive, desaturated brown, and soot. View it from inside the dark booth toward a small service window, brutalist walls, and a cold gray sky. Bodies stay coarse and stiff, with no antialiasing, gradients, or painterly texture.", "477-fez": "Render the image as Fez game art. Build a bright pixel-art world of orthographic cuboid floating islands that feel able to rotate in three dimensions, covered in vivid pastel mint, peach, lavender, and sky-blue tile patterns. Keep the subject a tiny simplified sprite on a small ledge amid much larger ruins, white clouds, and stepped foliage. Use one crisp pixel grid and flat game lighting, not smooth painting or a glossy modern 3D render.", "478-crash-bandicoot": "Render the image as a late-1990s PlayStation 1 platformer frame in the vein of Crash Bandicoot. Use a conspicuous low polygon count, faceted geometry, crunchy low-resolution textures with affine warping, simple vertex shading, and a short draw distance. Give the subject an oversized head, a huge grin, big angular hands, and skinny bent limbs on a saturated jungle path lined with wooden crates. It must look like 1997 console hardware, not modern polished animation.", "479-hewlett": "Render the image as a Jamie Hewlett illustration in the punk language of Tank Girl and Gorillaz. Use scratchy angular black ink, flat bold color, sharp cheekbones, sunken half-lidded eyes, wiry fingers, and selective heavy black shadows. Crop tight and off-kilter against grimy walls, nonverbal graffiti, and flaking paint, in acid yellow, burnt orange, turquoise, coral, and dense black. No soft airbrushing, gradients, or clean cartoon polish.", "480-dave-mckean": "Render the image as a Dave McKean mixed-media assemblage in the vein of The Sandman covers. Combine photographic fragments, painted textures, torn edges, scratched emulsion, and sculptural objects inside battered wooden shadow boxes, with layered transparencies and incompatible scales. Use soot black, umber, tarnished bronze, deep blue-green, and bruised purple, raked by dim light into velvety recesses. The subject should feel fractured and collaged, not a straight photograph with a filter on top.", "481-sempe": "Render the image as a Jean-Jacques Sempé illustration. Reduce the subject to a tiny economical cartoon figure, almost comically small inside a vast, delicately observed city of façades, rooftops, and airy open space. Use a tremulous fine pen line and very soft translucent watercolor on white paper, with a restrained pale gray, faded blue, and cream palette and lots of unpainted paper. The humor is the disproportion between one quiet small moment and the immense surroundings.", "482-heath-robinson": "Render the image as a Heath Robinson contraption illustration. Surround the subject with an absurdly elaborate machine of knotted strings, pulleys, pivoting levers, spindly timber, counterweights, and funnels, drawn in fine vintage British comic ink with a little muted wash on ivory paper. Show the whole precarious apparatus in one flattened side-on view, with precise small hardware and ridiculous but readable mechanical connections. Keep the figure an elongated economical pen character, never photorealistic.", "483-autochrome": "Render the image as a 1910s Autochrome color glass-plate photograph. Render color as a coarse irregular mosaic of dyed potato-starch grains, with soft muted rose and sage, low contrast, gentle halation, and deep shadows that still hold color. Suggest a long exposure through a faint tremble in foliage while the pose stays still, plus subtle plate-edge flaws. It should look like a historical garden capture, not a modern photo with a vintage preset or an illustration.", "484-paparazzi": "Render the image as a chaotic tabloid paparazzi snapshot. Use a tight off-kilter telephoto crop, harsh direct on-camera flash, hot specular highlights, hard-edged shadows, and motion smear on moving edges while the flash freezes the subject. Add crunchy high-ISO grain, abrupt flash color, and a murky night background compressed close behind. It should feel like an imperfect candid grabbed across a pavement, not posed glamour or a painterly portrait.", "485-film-negative": "Render the image as a physical color-film negative, a 35mm strip lying on a glowing lightbox beside a black inspection loupe. Show the orange-brown base, sprocket perforations, and several frames, with the main frame magnified. Truly invert color and luminance so highlights go dark and hues flip toward their complements, with visible analog grain. The loupe enlarges the negative and must not turn it positive.", "486-lace-doily": "Render the image as a handmade crocheted lace doily whose openwork itself forms the subject. Lay one complete round doily of fine ecru cotton on dark walnut, seen nearly overhead, with a scalloped radial picot border. Build the motif from chain loops, mesh holes, and tiny irregular stitches so the wood shows through the openings, with only restrained dyed-thread color. It is cotton and negative space, not print, solid-cloth embroidery, or a photograph set into lace.", "487-wax-figure": "Render the image as a wax-museum exhibit. Present an uncannily lifelike wax figure under a warm theatrical spotlight, behind a burgundy velvet rope on brass stanchions, while visitors in the foreground raise phones to photograph it. Wax surfaces look slightly translucent and too smooth, eyes are unblinking glass, and the pose is faintly stiff, even if hair and clothing are real materials. The gallery falls into cool dark shadow so the display reads clearly, not as an ordinary portrait.", "488-tin-foil": "Render the image as a handmade crumpled aluminum-foil sculpture. Translate the subject into folded, pinched, crushed kitchen foil, with coiled strips, ribbed texture, and chunky simplified forms, lit by a narrow raking key and a strong rim light against seamless charcoal. Thousands of real creases should throw jagged silver highlights and deep shadowed folds, and a restrained translucent tint may hint at local color without hiding the aluminum. It is a tactile foil object, not polished chrome or a metallic skin filter.", "489-painted-mug": "Render the image as a portrait painted in underglaze on a handmade ceramic mug. Use simplified expressive proportions, flowing brushstrokes, stippled texture, and irregular pools of pigment under a thick glossy transparent glaze, seen in a close three-quarter view of the curved vessel, uneven rim, handle, and bright glaze highlights. It should read as a decorated ceramic object on a quiet receding counter, not as a printed photograph or a decal.", "490-rubber-duck": "Render the image as a yellow rubber-duck bath toy. Translate the subject into plump molded-duck anatomy, with a rounded body, tiny wings, an orange bill, and beady eyes, and only small painted details on soft rubber. Float it diagonally among foam, turquoise water, and white porcelain in an overhead oblique view, showing mold seams, clinging droplets, cool bathroom light, and bold toy color rather than realistic anatomy.", "491-nutcracker": "Render the image as a traditional German carved wooden nutcracker. Give it a tall cylindrical hat, an oversized blocky head, a big square jaw with a visible lever, a rigid body, turned wooden limbs, and broad feet on a pedestal, finished in glossy red, gold, and evergreen lacquer over visible wood grain. Use a low viewpoint so the full figure stands in warm festive light against cold blue twilight, with gentle handmade imperfections.", "492-gashapon": "Render the image as a Japanese gashapon capsule toy. Sculpt a glossy injection-molded PVC figure with a dramatically oversized head and very short rounded limbs, resting in an opened clear capsule shot from an extreme low macro angle, with the vending machine's round window, knob, and chute receding behind. Show mold lines, plastic refraction, and pastel arcade light so it looks mass-produced and chibi, not like a realistic body shrunk down.", "493-william-morris": "Render the image as a William Morris Arts and Crafts wallpaper. Build an edge-to-edge repeating arabesque of acanthus, sinuous stems, birds, and flowers, and weave the subject in as a small flattened silhouette that follows the curling stems, with no central portrait. Use a vegetable-dye woodblock palette of indigo, madder red, sage, and cream, with carved line, slight misregistration, and paper grain, kept flat and unshaded.", "494-nianhua": "Render the image as a festive Chinese nianhua woodblock print. Use bold black carved outlines and flat vermilion, gold-yellow, and emerald ink, with chubby folk proportions, powerful silhouettes, and a buoyant asymmetrical grouping of the subject, a very large fish, lush peonies, and auspicious curls. Leave subtle printing irregularities so it reads as a hand-printed New Year picture rather than a filtered portrait.", "495-huichol": "Render the image as a Huichol yarn painting, every contour and color field built from densely packed yarn pressed into beeswax, with raised fiber and no brushstrokes. Place the subject frontally in a symmetrical radial vision of deer, peyote blossoms, a sun, concentric circles, and swirling lines. Fill every gap with electric cobalt, magenta, orange, turquoise, grass green, and luminous yellow, separated by dark contours, flat and symbolic edge to edge.", "496-palekh": "Render the image as a Russian Palekh lacquer miniature on a black box lid. Paint minute tempera and luminous gold-leaf line over glossy jet black, with flame-shaped foliage, an ornate gold border, slender trees, a small pavilion, and patterned hills. Elongate the subject into a graceful figure small on the lid, so intricate fairy-tale landscape and black negative space dominate in finely controlled jewel color.", "497-papel-picado": "Render the image as Mexican papel picado. Render the subject only as holes and connected shapes cut into translucent tissue, hung with other banners on strings across a sunlit plaza of warm stucco and blue sky. Use fuchsia, orange-red, turquoise, and yellow sheets, scalloped borders, floral filigree, and visible fibers, and let strong sun glow through every opening and cast lace shadows.", "498-rorschach": "Render the image as a Rorschach inkblot card. Fold black and dark vermilion ink into a perfectly bilateral blot on unmarked ivory paper, every splash, lobe, and feathered edge mirrored, and hide the subject in the positive and negative shapes as pareidolia rather than clean drawing. Emphasize pressed dendrites, capillary feathering, dry fibrous islands, pooled ink, and a faint center fold on a generous ivory ground, clinical and handmade.", "499-kaleidoscope": "Render the image as a view straight into an optical kaleidoscope. Break the subject into mirrored triangular slices and assemble a rigorous twelve-fold radial mandala of concentric bands, star polygons, and a central rosette, with no single intact portrait left. Use luminous ruby, emerald, sapphire, amethyst, amber, and gold, dark jewel depths, prismatic facets, internal reflections, and razor-sharp seams, filling the frame with layered optical depth and no outer tube.", "500-droste": "Render the image as a Droste-effect illustration. Nest the whole scene inside itself at least six times, each copy smaller and turned a little clockwise, so the repeats form one logarithmic spiral into a tiny center, with floors and arches bending along the recursion. Paint it as surreal gouache and lithograph in celadon, petrol teal, terracotta, and warm ivory, with matte pigment, delicate drawn detail, and impossible architecture rather than a stack of plain rectangles.", "501-loriot": "Render the image as a Loriot cartoon by Vicco von Bülow. Draw little figures with enormous bulbous potato noses, tiny dot eyes, and stiff dignified posture, in immaculate fine pen and sparse pale watercolor on mostly untouched white paper. Set a sparse full-body side view in a formal German living room with an ornate armchair and only a few furnishings, in muted ocher, pale blue-gray, and beige, the humor ceremonious and understated.", "502-wilhelm-busch": "Render the image as a Wilhelm Busch picture story in the manner of Max und Moritz. Stack three small, widely spaced wordless vignettes on a mostly blank cream page, drawn with loose scratchy dip-pen line and the lightest pale hand color. Use tiny stocky figures with large round heads, button noses, pinprick eyes, and short bendy limbs in awkward elastic poses, with a spare 1860s interior scratched in and the humor carried by the mishap.", "503-janosch": "Render the image as a Janosch children's picture-book drawing. Use sparse naive colored pencil and watercolor, crooked contours, flattened perspective, and bright transparent yellow and green washes, leaving about half the sheet as bare cream paper. Reduce the page to a few affectionate scribbles: a tiny crooked house, a ribbon of river, a friendly bear, and a yellow duck with black tiger stripes and little red wheels, wonky and barely rendered.", "504-sendung-mit-der-maus": "Render the image as a frame from the German educational cartoon Die Sendung mit der Maus. Use extremely simple flat shapes, even solid color, and gentle clean dark contours on pure white, with no texture, gradient, or shadow. Simplify the subject into a friendly waist-up shape beside the show's orange mouse, with large brown ears, and a much smaller blue elephant, the sizes cartoonishly disproportionate and the mood warm and uncluttered.", "505-kollwitz": "Render the image as a Käthe Kollwitz charcoal drawing and lithograph. Build heavy searching black strokes, velvety rubbed tone, scraped highlights, broken crayon, and granular paper, strictly in black, gray, and paper white. Crop tightly, bow the posture, and let enlarged sculptural hands and hunched shoulders carry the weight while the surroundings dissolve into dark tone, compassionate, somber, and unmistakably handmade.", "506-otto-dix": "Render the image as an Otto Dix New Objectivity portrait from 1920s Berlin. Paint in oil and tempera with razor-sharp cold outlines, glazed glossy color, and unforgiving detail, subtly caricaturing a long neck, an angular oversized face, a pointed chin, thin fingers, and a tense asymmetrical expression. Seat the figure erect in a severe geometric interior of burgundy, bottle green, and icy blue-gray under cool crystalline light, hard and psychologically still rather than photographic.", "507-grosz": "Render the image as a George Grosz satire of 1920s Berlin. Draw scratchy dip-pen contours and loose acidic watercolor on off-white paper, washes slipping past the lines, and caricature the crowd with angular noses, tiny eyes, balloon cheeks, stick wrists, and heavy bellies. Pack them into a high oblique café with a radically tilted floor and overlapping tables, in acid yellow, rancid pink, vermilion, dirty turquoise, and bilious green, mordant and grotesque.", "508-kirchner": "Render the image as an Ernst Ludwig Kirchner painting of Die Brücke. Use harsh slashing strokes, jagged forms, heavily elongated bodies, and pointed masklike faces on a flattened Berlin street at night, façades tilting and diagonals rushing to a tight vanishing point. Clash hot pink with toxic emerald, sulfur yellow, and violet-black, thin scumbled paint and a few impasto accents on rough canvas, the light non-naturalistic and the energy tense.", "509-mr-men": "Render the image as a Mr. Men illustration by Roger Hargreaves. Turn the subject into one round blob whose body is also its head, with spindly black stick arms and legs, comically large friendly eyes, a thick slightly wobbly outline, and a single flat color on plain white. Show the whole character in a jaunty off-center side-step, one stick foot raised, amid enormous negative space, like a cheerful children's-book drawing rather than a detailed cartoon.", "510-miffy": "Render the image as a Dick Bruna illustration in the manner of Miffy. Use thick black outlines of nearly uniform width, flat opaque primary colors, and radical economy: a frontal symmetrical pose, a large round face, tiny dot eyes, a small x-shaped mouth, and mitten-like hands, with no shading or perspective. Set a solid primary-blue ground and a vivid red band along the bottom, quiet, iconic, and almost abstract.", "511-babar": "Render the image as a Babar picture-book illustration by Jean de Brunhoff. Draw delicate early-1930s ink and gentle faded watercolor on airy cream paper, in sage, dusty terracotta, pale blue-gray, and diluted ocher. Set a small full-length profile in a wide quaint town of pale shutters and cobbles, with breathing room and a dignified elephant in a green suit nearby. Keep the perspective naive, the washes translucent, and the light undramatic.", "512-sanrio": "Render the image as a Sanrio-style kawaii mascot. Shrink the subject into a huge rounded head about two-thirds of the height, a tiny stub body, nubby limbs, widely spaced dot eyes, a very small mouth, and one tiny accent bow. Use soft cocoa outlines and flat pastel fills with no shadows or gradients, and arrange matching front, side, and rear views on a pale blush ground with clear space between them, ready for an enamel pin.", "513-boondocks": "Render the image as a frame from The Boondocks television animation. Use sharp ink outlines, anime-influenced angular eyes, stylized adult proportions, and flat separated cel shading over a painted suburb of houses, lawns, and overhead wires. Stage an asymmetric medium shot from a slightly low angle, clump complex shapes into clean inked masses, and block the shadows crisply in muted cool daylight. It should be native 2D animation, not a photograph with a cartoon filter.", "514-castlevania": "Render the image as a frame from the Netflix Castlevania animation. Draw dramatic gothic 2D anime with sharp angular faces, elegant elongated anatomy, fine linework, and hard graphic cel shadows, set in a candlelit castle hall where pointed arches dwarf the figure. Let blood-red glow and deep midnight-blue shadow dominate, with only small gold highlights, and keep the silhouettes blade-like and hand-drawn rather than photographic or generic 3D.", "515-puss-in-boots": "Render the image as painterly 3D animation in the manner of Puss in Boots: The Last Wish. Sculpt a caricatured form with an exaggerated head, elastic limbs, and a held action pose, covered in chunky visible brush texture, graphic hard light, and a few rough motion streaks as if the frame rate had stepped. Stage extreme foreshortening in a fairytale forest of diagonal roots, teal shadow, and fiery orange sunset, vibrant and tactile rather than photoreal or smooth CGI.", "516-kubo": "Render the image as a Laika stop-motion frame in the manner of Kubo and the Two Strings. Translate the subject into a handcrafted puppet of painted resin, fiber, and fabric inside a physical miniature, backed by a layered cut-paper sky, a circular paper moon, and a few origami forms. Photograph cool moonlight, a small warm glow, deep gaps between paper layers, and shallow focus, leaving torn edges, wood grain, seams, and handmade irregularity tangible.", "517-guilty-gear": "Render the image as a Guilty Gear Strive fighting-game key frame. Render cel-shaded 3D that still looks hand-drawn, with hard graphic shadows, angular ink outlines, and extreme foreshortening, freezing an airborne twist from a low Dutch angle so a huge limb thrusts toward the lens. Burst the move into speed slashes and jagged impact shapes against angular stone and a crimson-black sky, dramatic and flashy rather than painterly.", "518-hotline-miami": "Render the image as Hotline Miami pixel art. Lock a strict overhead floor plan in chunky low-resolution pixels with stepped edges, flooding a small 1980s apartment with neon hot pink, electric cyan, and purple-black shadow. Let psychedelic color bands and VHS-like scanline glitches ripple past one small top-down sprite and a stylized animal mask, the architecture dominating. It should feel like unsettling retro game art, not isometric 3D or a photo filter.", "519-obra-dinn": "Render the image as a Return of the Obra Dinn screenshot. Restrict everything to warm amber pixels on absolute black, with no gray, gradient, or antialiasing, and describe form through chunky ordered Bayer dither, hard stippled silhouettes, and stark low-poly geometry. Set the view in a cramped sailing-ship cabin of beams, ropes, and a hanging lantern, like a memory frozen mid-instant, clearly dithered game rendering rather than an engraving or a sepia photograph.", "520-tunic": "Render the image as Tunic game art. Build a cute isometric low-poly diorama with toy proportions, a large simplified head, and a tiny compact body, seen from high above a mossy stair, russet autumn trees, rounded ruins, and a turquoise stream. Use luminous golden afternoon light, soft tilt-shift focus, gentle ambient occlusion, and a restrained palette of jade, terracotta, ocher, and cream. Keep it calm, exploratory, and clearly stylized adventure-game art.", "521-instagram-2012": "Render the image as a 2012 Instagram snapshot with a heavy early retro filter. Lift and fade the blacks, push a strong warm yellow cast, crush the corners in a soft dark vignette, and soften detail like an early smartphone camera, including a thin white border on every side. Frame an informal close candid from a slightly high diagonal angle, with casual imperfect cropping and washed, brownish shadows. The filter should transform the whole picture, not add a mild tint.", "522-napkin-sketch": "Render the image as a quick ballpoint sketch on a crumpled paper napkin. Show the napkin from overhead on a dark table, with uneven edges, embossed fiber, creases, and a faint brown ring stain, and draw the subject in sparse blue ink: variable pressure, searching overlaps, open gaps, and a few hatches, plus minimal rusty accents. Leave it lively and unfinished, a sixty-second sketch whose paper and pen marks carry the picture.", "523-post-it-mosaic": "Render the image as a mosaic of blank sticky notes on an office wall. Build the picture only from square paper notes, with chunky stepped contours and simplified proportions, photographed slightly obliquely so edges, tiny shadows, and curled corners read as real paper. Use broad cyan, coral, lime, and pink in ordinary daylight, desks below establishing scale, the notes themselves the only medium.", "524-cuckoo-clock": "Render the image as a small automaton on a Black Forest cuckoo clock. Carve the full chalet in walnut and honey-brown wood, with grain, gouge marks, oak leaves, a deer on the roof, pinecone weights, and a dial of ornamental marks, and pop the subject from the upper door as a folk figure with an oversized head and compact carved body. Light it from a low three-quarter angle so raking warmth picks out deep relief on a plain plaster wall.", "525-dubuffet": "Render the image as a Jean Dubuffet art brut painting. Scratch a crude childlike full-body figure into thick gritty impasto, with an enlarged asymmetric head, spindly bent limbs, splayed feet, and compressed perspective, woven with L'Hourloupe cells of white, red, and blue inside wandering black outlines. Cover earthy mud browns and ocher with paint crusts, scratches, embedded grit, and clumsy marks, raw, flattened, and irreverent rather than naturalistic.", "526-sorolla": "Render the image as a Joaquín Sorolla luminist oil painting. Open a wide Mediterranean beach in dazzling afternoon sun, fluttering white linen and turquoise water taking most of the light, and keep the subject small within that airy diagonal. Describe form with loaded impasto, broken color, and broad bravura strokes that let sunlight dissolve contours, highlights in blinding cream and pale gold, shadows in lavender and violet warmed by reflection.", "527-gerhard-richter": "Render the image as a Gerhard Richter photo-painting. Start from a restrained, slightly photographic oil image in cool grays and off-white, tightly cropped and off-center like a fragment of memory, then drag wet paint horizontally with a squeegee. Leave continuous lateral smears, sliding doubled contours, whisper-thin films, canvas tooth, and a few sharp residues so the blur is physical paint. Keep the mood ambiguous, melancholic, and ghostly.", "528-thunderbirds": "Render the image as a frame of 1960s Thunderbirds Supermarionation. Stage a wooden marionette with an oversized carved head, a small rigid body, a hinged jaw, glossy glass eyes, and visible control strings, inside a miniature futuristic set of molded furniture, colored lamp panels, and a round window onto a slender rocket. Use saturated teal, scarlet, canary yellow, and cream under hard studio spotlights, with theatrical shadows, television grain, and obviously physical scale-model seams.", "529-pee-wee-playhouse": "Render the image as a Pee-wee's Playhouse-style 1980s children's television set. Build a dense practical world of talking furniture, a grinning globe, a peeking hand puppet, and oversized crooked props in painted foam and felt, clashing turquoise, red, yellow, fuchsia, and lime across Memphis squiggles and checkerboards. Shoot a wide view from an eccentric low diagonal under bright flat multi-camera light, with analog broadcast softness, so the subject belongs to that manic handmade set.", "530-teletubbies": "Render the image as a Teletubbies-like preschool television landscape. Turn the subject into a plush costume with an oversized round head, stubby limbs, a broad belly, and a rectangular tummy screen, standing small on vast unnaturally green hills beneath a smiling baby-faced sun, with giant rabbits in the grass. Use a low viewpoint, brilliant midday sun, candy color, and tactile faux fur so the proportions feel stuffed and surreal rather than ordinary.", "531-frieren": "Render the image as a soft fantasy anime illustration in the manner of Frieren. Use delicate hand-drawn lines, restrained cel shading, and a watercolor background of pale luminous color and quiet negative space, the subject in side profile low in a meadow of blue flowers, distant hills dissolving in pearly light. Keep the proportions slender, the brushwork loose, and the mood contemplative and faintly melancholy, a finished 2D frame rather than a photograph or a 3D render.", "532-godzilla-1954": "Render the image as a monochrome still from a 1954 Japanese kaiju film. Composite a gigantic rubber-suit monster, craggy hide and jagged dorsal plates, over a miniature city at night, with collapsing models, practical smoke, and searchlight beams, the subject large in a lower corner from an extreme low angle. Use harsh backlighting, silver-gelatin tones, coarse film grain, and optical softness, strictly black and white and obviously practical, never modern CGI.", "533-barbie-dreamhouse": "Render the image as a still from the 2023 Barbie movie dreamhouse. Place the subject in a hyper-pink plastic set of toy-like furniture, a painted blue-sky backdrop, and theatrical clouds, framed full-length from a slightly low frontal angle. Use bright pristine studio light, candy-pink curves, molded gloss, and lilac and peach accents so everything feels immaculate, glossy, and joyfully artificial, a practical set scaled like a toy rather than lived-in daylight.", "534-dune": "Render the image as a cinematic still in the manner of the 2021 film Dune. Reduce the world to vast muted beige dunes, severe brutalist monoliths, and a tiny ornithopter in dusty haze, and set the subject small in profile within the narrow shade of an immense concrete mass. Use monumental hard shadow, minimal forms, and a subdued palette of ocher, bone, and gray under distant diffuse sun, somber, austere, and precisely designed.", "535-splatoon": "Render the image as glossy Splatoon-style 3D game art. Rebuild the subject with squid-kid proportions, an oversized head, big expressive eyes, a compact body, chunky shoes, and tentacles rising from the head, caught off balance in an urban skatepark from an extreme high fisheye angle. Splash thick neon yellow and purple ink across curved ramps and rails, with rubbery highlights and punchy cel-informed shading on clean sculpted assets.", "536-silent-hill": "Render the image as a late-1990s Silent Hill gameplay frame. Swallow a deserted low-poly street in dense gray fog, with grimy pixel textures, visible polygon edges, affine texture warping, dither, and grain, seen by a fixed camera with a short draw distance. Keep the geometry blocky and the palette a dirty gray and rusty brown under heavy unnatural shadow, coarse original-console dread rather than a photograph with noise added.", "537-earthbound": "Render the image as EarthBound SNES pixel art. Draw hard low-resolution pixels and quirky simplified sprites on an oblique suburban map of slanting sidewalks and toy-like buildings, in mint, lilac, peach, and buttery yellow. Open part of the street into a vivid psychedelic concentric battle swirl and let the scale turn comical, as if an ordinary object had become an enemy. Keep every form crisp 16-bit pixel art, with no smooth painting.", "538-beano": "Render the image as a 1950s to 1970s British children's comic in the Beano and Dandy tradition. Draw one chaotic slapstick scene in thick chunky black brush outlines, grotesque proportions, rubbery limbs, and flat primary color on white paper, crowded with diagonal action, kicking furniture, speed lines, and jagged wordless burst shapes. Keep it vintage, gag-driven, and thoroughly cartooned, with no realistic proportion and no glossy finish.", "539-valerian": "Render the image as a Jean-Claude Mézières bande dessinée in the manner of Valérian. Fill a wide view with a busy alien market of bizarre endearing creatures, ramshackle retro-futuristic gadgets, and eccentric stalls, drawn in precise lively pen and rich flat turquoise, rusty orange, plum, and ocher, with only small patches of hatching. Layer foreground figures, a middle-ground subject, and deep organic-mechanical towers, imaginative and hand-drawn rather than cinematic concept art.", "540-blankets": "Render the image as an intimate graphic-novel drawing in the manner of Craig Thompson's Blankets. Use flowing brush ink in black and white only, variable-width contours, and velvety pools of solid black against luminous blank paper, with decorative snowfall curling through so the line echoes the subject. Hold a close, tender, asymmetric profile, quiet and inward, with elongated curves rather than realistic modeling, clearly a hand-inked page and not a grayscale photograph.", "541-fashion-plate": "Render the image as a Victorian hand-colored fashion plate. Etch fine outlines and restrained cross-hatching on ivory paper, then lay translucent pastel washes, elongating the figure to full-length fashion-plate proportions in elaborate period dress with a bustle, posed in a lightly engraved garden. Leave much of the paper unprinted and describe folds with meticulous delicate line, an engraving with hand-applied color, flat and graphic rather than photographic.", "542-ladybird-books": "Render the image as a 1960s Ladybird children's book illustration. Paint crisp opaque gouache in warm natural color, a wide slightly raised view of a cozy British interior opening onto a high street, with cream walls, polished wood, and clear window daylight. Keep proportions understated and idealized, details tidy and of the period, and the setting informative, every surface brush-painted with mid-century nonfiction clarity rather than lifestyle photography.", "543-electron-microscope": "Render the image as a scanning electron microscope micrograph. Present the subject as a tiny sculpture on the flattened tip of a sewing needle rising diagonally through a black field, rendered as grayscale relief with charging highlights, granular microtexture, machining striations, and deep occlusion shadows. Use the instrument's strange depth of field so it reads as a scientific specimen, not an optical macro shot, and a small blank white scale bar may sit in a corner.", "544-macrame": "Render the image as a handmade macramé wall hanging. Form the whole image from knotted cotton cord, square knots, spirals, loops, and openwork, hung from a wooden dowel with long fringe, straight-on against warm plaster. Let undyed cotton dominate, accented by muted plant-dyed terracotta and ocher, and light the thick fiber so imperfect knots cast small shadows. The cord, not print or paint, should decide the simplified folk-art shapes.", "545-hard-candy": "Render the image as a design suspended in a translucent hard-candy lollipop. Build it from colored sugar glass, with refraction, tiny bubbles, glossy highlights, and edges warped by the thick round disc on a pale stick, swirled with pink, amber, turquoise, and ruby and a few caught sprinkles. Shoot a tilted macro in the foreground so light passes through and warm caustics run along the rim. It must look edible and handmade, not printed onto candy.", "546-truck-art": "Render the image as South Asian truck art. Show a cargo truck's rear from a low wide three-quarter view, tailgate dominant, covered in hand-painted enamel scrolls, paired peacocks, roses, scalloped borders, mirror chips, chains, and dangling ornaments. Set the subject into one panel as flat vernacular portraiture with bold black outlines and saturated vermilion, emerald, cobalt, hot pink, and yellow, sunlight catching worn metal while ornament fills every remaining gap.", "547-retablo": "Render the image as a Mexican retablo ex-voto painted on aged tin. Tell a naive story of a mishap narrowly averted, in flattened perspective, stiff gestures, simple faces, and skewed scale, with a small luminous apparition, pale gold halo, and thin miraculous ray in an upper corner. Leave a broad blank cream band along the bottom, and keep nail holes, oxidation, scratches, and thin matte paint in faded ocher, brick red, dusty blue, and cream.", "548-view-master": "Render the image as a 1970s product photograph of a View-Master-style stereo toy. Set a chunky glossy red plastic viewer and a round ivory reel of tiny slides on worn pale aqua, shot from a diagonal overhead angle, the subject appearing only inside the small transparencies, one window warmly backlit. Show film grain, dust, amber color, cardboard fiber, and scuffed plastic under direct light and crisp shadows, the viewer and reel otherwise blank.", "549-1920s-magazine-ad": "Render the image as a 1920s magazine-advertisement illustration. Paint soft opaque gouache and fine ink on aged cream paper, with lithographic halftone dots and restrained flat color, inside a border where Art Nouveau curves become stepped Art Deco corners. Elongate the silhouette into a poised full-figure vignette anchored by an elegant foreground object, in muted amber, dusty rose, sage, and black, graphic and deliberately open.", "550-1920s-home-movie": "Render the image as a single frame from a 1920s hand-cranked amateur home movie. Render it in sepia-toned black-and-white monochrome with gentle soft focus, silver grain, uneven flickering exposure, a heavy vignette, dust, and irregular vertical emulsion scratches. Keep the framing casually crooked and eye-level, with slight motion smear that suggests sped-up cranking, and show the rounded corners of the film gate. It should look like an archival moving-picture frame, not a modern photograph.", "551-1930s-streamline-ad": "Render the image as a 1930s streamline moderne advertisement painted in airbrush and crisp commercial gouache. Use sleek aerodynamic curves, sweeping speed lines, and diagonal arcs through pastel mint, powder blue, and peach gradients. Turn metal into idealized painted ribbons of white and blue, with smooth airbrush shading, crisp edges, and a delicate old printed-paper texture. Exaggerate fashion-illustration proportions and asymmetric balance so it reads as period advertising art, not a photograph or a 3D render.", "552-1930s-newsreel": "Render the image as an authentic 1930s cinema newsreel frame in harsh high-contrast black and white. Use a low, crowded documentary angle, strong chiaroscuro, direct flashbulb overexposure, and deep crushed shadows. Add heavy coarse film grain, irregular vertical scratches, dust, emulsion wear, slight movement blur, and rough reportage optics. The capture should feel spontaneous, irregularly framed, and archival rather than posed or illustrated.", "553-1940s-homefront-poster": "Render the image as a 1940s home-front propaganda poster painted in flat bold opaque gouache. Use economical hard-edged shapes, a strong graphic silhouette, simplified painted anatomy, and confident heroic proportions seen from a low monumental angle. Let red, white, and navy dominate the flat background shapes, with a screenprinted-paper feel and no photographic rendering or modern gradients. The design should read as period poster painting, not a photograph.", "554-1940s-pulp-cover": "Render the image as a 1940s pulp magazine cover painted in lurid saturated oils. Use energetic brushwork, melodramatic exaggerated anatomy, glowing highlights, deep colored shadows, and a steep diagonal of adventure. Set emerald and ultramarine shadows against fiery scarlet and brilliant amber light, and leave the upper field as empty richly painted space. It must look like painted adventure illustration, never a photograph with a filter.", "555-1950s-appliance-ad": "Render the image as a 1950s atomic-age appliance advertisement in cheerful commercial gouache. Use elongated domestic-advertising proportions, brisk economical brush shapes, and decorative flat pastel planes of mint, pale pink, buttercream, and robin's-egg blue. Make the product oversized and gleaming, with painted chrome highlights, floating atomic starbursts, and boomerang shapes in playful asymmetry. It should feel like optimistic period advertising art, not a photograph or a product render.", "556-1950s-tv-commercial": "Render the image as a black-and-white frame from a 1950s live television commercial, captured as a kinescope. Use a soft low-resolution silver-gray range, film grain, slight interlaced ghosting, blown studio highlights, and subdued shadow. Curve the picture with CRT raster distortion and clearly rounded dark screen edges. Stage it as awkward earnest live-TV product demonstration under fixed studio lighting, not a crisp modern portrait.", "557-1950s-photojournalism": "Render the image as 1950s picture-magazine photojournalism shot on a medium-format Rolleiflex. Use classic waist-level framing, a candid unstaged moment, crisp silver-gelatin tonality, deep blacks, and luminous natural window light. Keep restrained medium-format grain and believable documentary detail, with no beauty retouching and no color at all. It should feel like an actual period negative rather than a filtered contemporary photograph.", "558-1960s-mod-photoshoot": "Render the image as a mid-1960s Swinging London mod fashion photograph. Fill the frame with a striking angular pose against a bold black-and-white op-art field of concentric circles. Use graphic styling, heavy mod eyeliner, crisp high-key studio light, saturated print color, and subtle magazine grain. It should read at once as a 1965 London fashion editorial, not a contemporary photo with a vintage filter.", "559-1960s-super-8": "Render the image as one frame from a 1960s Super 8 family home movie. Shoot it handheld, slightly tilted, and imperfectly composed, with soft focus, slight motion smear, and dense organic film grain. Use warm saturated yet faded orange, yellow, and olive, plus gentle halation, uneven exposure, and a reddish light leak. Include a dark film margin and a plausible sprocket-hole edge, so it looks like modest amateur footage rather than polished cinema.", "560-1960s-airline-ad": "Render the image as a 1960s jet-age airline travel advertisement painted in bright optimistic gouache. Use a sweeping diagonal inside a sleek cabin, oval windows, curved architecture, and brilliant clouds against an azure sky. Draw elongated fashion-illustration proportions with confident flat color shapes, angular brush marks, simplified features, and vintage commercial paper texture. Keep ivory, coral, golden yellow, and azure, with no photographic base or camera effects.", "561-1970s-tv-commercial": "Render the image as a frame from a 1970s television commercial photographed on period color film. Use a low intimate viewpoint, as if caught midway through a slow optical zoom, with diffusion-filter halation and gently compressed perspective. Warm the palette toward orange and brown, add analog grain and low broadcast-era detail, and stage a cozy room of shag and amber lamp light. It must not look like a modern photograph with a retro filter.", "562-1970s-catalog-photo": "Render the image as a 1970s mail-order catalog photograph, posed like a stiff product plate. Use a straight-on full view in a wood-paneled room, flat direct flash, and minimal sculptural shadow. Colors are slightly faded printed inks in muted ocher, olive, tan, and brown, with cheap printed-paper quality. Keep the pose rehearsed and unglamorous, ordinary catalog production rather than editorial lighting or cinematic glow.", "563-1970s-disco-ad": "Render the image as a dazzling 1970s disco-era airbrushed poster painting. Spray pigment into smooth gradients, lacquered chrome highlight bands, fragmented mirror-ball facets, and sharp white starburst glints. Use elongated fashion anatomy, graphic sweeping arcs, and a low dynamic angle against midnight space lit in fuchsia, cyan, violet, and electric orange. Specular highlights must be hand-painted, never photographic.", "564-1980s-glamour-shots": "Render the image as an extravagant 1980s shopping-mall glamour-shots studio portrait. Use a tight head-and-shoulders crop, a deliberate head tilt, teased voluminous hair, and a big shoulder-pad silhouette. Apply heavy optical soft-focus, pearly highlight bloom, frontal fill, a hair light, and a gauzy lavender-and-rose muslin backdrop. Keep the cheesy earnest mall-portrait glamour, rosy makeup, and softened film texture of a compressed portrait lens.", "565-1980s-tv-commercial": "Render the image as a frame from an exuberant 1980s television commercial recorded onto VHS. Stage a bright synthwave studio with a luminous cyan-and-magenta perspective grid, geometric light panels, chrome sparkle, and four-point analog star glints. Use a low wide camera, theatrical colored rim light, oversaturated camcorder color, slight horizontal tracking noise, chroma smear, and softened interlaced videotape. The energy should belong to period TV advertising, not a contemporary portrait with a filter.", "566-1980s-camcorder": "Render the image as a paused frame from ordinary 1980s home camcorder VHS footage. Use an awkward handheld elevated angle, slightly tilted wide framing, fuzzy low resolution, analog edge halos, and strong red color bleed. Add muted brown-green shadows, blown highlights, white-balance errors, interlacing, ragged tracking, and only a tiny red dot with pale corner brackets. It should look like poorly recorded home video, not a sharp photograph with a VHS overlay.", "567-1990s-fashion-ad": "Render the image as a 1990s minimalist fashion-magazine advertisement in strictly monochrome silver-gelatin photography. Use conspicuous organic 35mm grain, deep blacks, an austere pale-gray studio backdrop, and understated moody directional light. Keep the pose raw and casually elegant, with spare negative space, unretouched texture, and imperfect analog focus. It should feel like an actual 1990s print campaign, not contemporary lifestyle photography with a filter.", "568-1990s-kids-commercial": "Render the image as a bombastic frame from a 1990s children's television commercial. Use an extreme fisheye close-up, barrel distortion, a crazy Dutch angle, and comically exaggerated near-and-far scale. Surround the live action with splashy flat graphics in slime green and purple: giant splats, zigzags, stars, and speed strokes. Keep cheap broadcast softness, composited graphic edges, and manic frontal studio light, like paused early-1990s kids' TV rather than a polished movie shot.", "569-1990s-geocities": "Render the image as a flat 1990s amateur personal homepage graphic, seen straight on with no browser chrome. Tile a black-and-navy starfield, freeze cheap animated-GIF sparkles at mismatched phases, and add garish cyan and magenta rules plus yellow-and-black under-construction bars. Inset one small off-center picture as crudely dithered, pixelated 256-color web imagery among blank chunky beveled buttons. Jagged edges, mismatched scales, and busy table-layout clutter should feel genuinely late-1990s and unpolished.", "570-2000s-silhouette-ad": "Render the image as a mid-2000s music-player silhouette advertisement, printed as a flat opaque poster. Fill the field edge to edge with one bright neon color, and reduce the subject to a solid jet-black full-body dancing silhouette. Add thin white earbud cables and a small blank white player shape, using only those three flat colors. No texture, gradients, or shadows; the graphic should stay crisp, simplified, and poster-bold.", "571-2000s-digicam": "Render the image as a candid snapshot from a cheap early-2000s compact digital camera. Use harsh on-camera flash, hard black shadows, blown chalk-white highlights, cold blue-white balance, and obvious red-eye. Soften detail like a low-megapixel sensor, with JPEG blocks, chroma noise, slight motion blur, and skewed amateur framing. A tiny corner patch of fragmented amber dashes may suggest a timestamp, but nothing in it should be readable.", "572-2000s-webcam": "Render the image as a mid-2000s low-quality webcam selfie, as if captured by a tiny USB camera and enlarged. Use a steep high angle, an awkward close crop, foreshortened features, washed-out color, and murky crushed shadows. Add blotchy sensor noise, chunky JPEG squares, color bleeding, and a soft smeared lens under dim mixed room light. It should look like a cheap personal webcam frame, not a sharp or professionally lit photograph.", "573-alma-thomas": "Render the image as an Alma Thomas abstract painting. Build the entire subject from rhythmic vertical rows of irregular mosaic-like brush dabs and radiant concentric bands, with joyful color and abundant white canvas gaps. No continuous realistic outlines or photographic shading; the subject should emerge loosely and partly dissolve into the color rhythms. Keep irregular brush pressure and visible ground between every separated mark.", "574-thiebaud": "Render the image as a Wayne Thiebaud oil painting. Use thick buttery impasto, palette-knife buildup, and tactile paint, with orderly rows of cakes and pastries, bright halo outlines in electric blue, red, and orange, and strong cast shadows. Simplify figures into solid painted forms, smaller than the still-life display, under pale luminous surroundings. The surface should show real impasto ridges, not a filtered photograph or a 3D render.", "575-petrykivka": "Render the image as Ukrainian Petrykivka decorative painting on a deep pure black ground. Surround a flat ornamental profile with lush flowing flowers, curling foliage, and berry clusters painted in fine comb-like strokes, tapered petals, and fingertip dots. Use bright red, yellow, and green with cream highlights, rhythmic stems, and crisp black breathing spaces. Keep it entirely flat folk ornament, with no modeled realism, gradients, or camera effects.", "576-tom-and-jerry": "Render the image as a single frame from a lavish 1940s MGM theatrical cartoon in the Tom and Jerry tradition. Paint a lush gouache interior behind hand-inked, hand-painted cels, with clear silhouettes, extreme squash and stretch, and rubbery slapstick motion. A gray cat and a tiny brown mouse may race through the scene and send objects into the air. Use warm painted depth, opaque flat color, and exuberant comic timing, never photography or 3D.", "577-popeye-fleischer": "Render the image as a 1930s Fleischer Studios cartoon frame in the Popeye tradition, strictly black and white. Use hand-inked rubber-hose anatomy, bulbous forearms, a tiny waist, bent rubbery legs, squash and stretch, and simple pie-cut eyes. Place flat animation cels in front of a sculptural miniature tabletop set with deep theatrical shadows. Add silvery grayscale and aged film grain so it reads as vintage animation, never a photograph.", "578-berserk": "Render the image as a Berserk-style dark fantasy manga panel in black ink on white paper. Use obsessive fine crosshatching, stark masses of solid black, etched highlights, and monumental threatening architecture. Frame from a tight low angle with dense foreground detail swallowed by brooding shadow, and include a huge blade as a diagonal mass. No color; it must read as dramatic hand-drawn manga, not a photograph.", "579-yotsuba": "Render the image as a Yotsuba&!-style slice-of-life manga panel. Give most of the frame to a spacious, precisely observed everyday Japanese setting, drawn with clean fine pen lines and delicate black-and-white screentone. Keep faces simple and cheerful against accurate mundane architecture, large sunlit whites, and a light breezy rhythm. The result should feel like an airy printed comic panel, not a dense heroic close-up or a photograph.", "580-holy-mountain": "Render the image as a still from a 1970s surreal cult film in the manner of Alejandro Jodorowsky. Build a rigorously symmetrical ritual tableau with bold primary-colored costumes, alchemical glass vessels, and monumental geometric staging. Use saturated analog 35mm color, subtle grain, hard dramatic studio light, and a wide frontal view of a practical set. The subject should sit small on the central axis within enormous theatrical architecture.", "581-space-odyssey": "Render the image as a 1968 science-fiction film still in the manner of 2001: A Space Odyssey. Compose a pristine white retro-futurist interior with bilateral symmetry, a centered vanishing point, luminous curved panels, and sculptural late-1960s furniture. Keep the architecture dominant, the light cool and diffused, and the color restrained, with large-format cinema texture and subtle analog grain. It should feel like a meticulously built practical set.", "582-twin-peaks": "Render the image as an early-1990s mystery television still in the manner of Twin Peaks. Set a theatrical room of floor-to-ceiling crimson velvet and a black-and-cream zigzag chevron floor, with dreamlike stillness and eerie stage light. Use saturated red, deep shadow, amber light, and subtle analog film grain. Keep the staging quiet, asymmetrical, and uncanny, like a practical set rather than a stylized illustration.", "583-gris": "Render the image as hand-painted art from the game Gris. Use delicate watercolor gradients, translucent pigment blooms, soft pastel reds and blues, and immense pale negative space. Place the subject small within minimalist geometric architecture of slender arches and broken circles, and let flowing cloth become long ribbons. Keep fragile linework, dusty coral, powder blue, and indigo on textured ivory paper, melancholic and fully illustrative, never photoreal or 3D.", "584-donkey-kong-country": "Render the image as a 1994 pre-rendered 3D console game scene in the manner of Donkey Kong Country. Use a chunky pixel raster, a limited palette, and early CGI sprites with baked highlights, glossy toy-like materials, and dithered shadows. Stage a side-on jungle platform with lush parallax layers of oversized leaves and banana-yellow greens. Keep exaggerated sprite proportions, hard specular spots, and crunchy low-resolution edges, not smooth modern 3D.", "585-rayman": "Render the image as Rayman-style hand-painted platform-game art. Rebuild the subject as a bouncy limbless cartoon: an oversized expressive head, a compact torso, and completely detached floating hands and feet, with no connecting arms or legs. Set that silhouette in a vibrant fantasy forest of lush saturated foliage, twisting plants, and glowing golden lums. Use elastic proportions, painterly shapes, and luminous turquoise and warm orange, never a photograph.", "586-ghost-world": "Render the image as a Daniel Clowes alternative-comic panel in the manner of Ghost World. Use clean deadpan ink line, flat pale blue-green spot color on off-white paper, and rigid awkward geometry. Place the subject in a bland suburban diner booth with spare banal detail, unromantic even light, and an ironic detached mood. Almost no modeling; it should look like a printed comic panel, not photo realism.", "587-love-and-rockets": "Render the image as a Hernandez brothers comic drawing in the manner of Love and Rockets, in pure black brush ink on white paper. Use bold confident contours, heavy balanced black shapes, expressive realistically drawn characters, and sparse deliberate hatching. Set a punk-era Los Angeles backyard with chain-link, stucco, rough plants, and utility wires, seen from a low natural viewpoint. No color and no photographic tracing.", "588-james-jean": "Render the image as a James Jean surreal illustration. Use intricate flowing ornamental linework, obsessive botanical detail, and delicate muted color in jade, shell pink, dusty ivory, and softened gold. Let flowers, waves, and ribbon-like currents spill through the composition and merge with the subject in an asymmetrical dreamlike spiral. Elongate forms gently, shift scale, and paint with fine ink and translucent washes rather than photographic space.", "589-syd-mead": "Render the image as Syd Mead retro-futurist concept art, painted as sleek industrial-design gouache. Dominate the frame with a gleaming elongated vehicle, a streamlined structure, and sweeping architecture under an optimistic golden-pink sunset. Use a wide low viewpoint, precise perspective, glossy painted reflections, airbrushed tonal transitions, and confident opaque planes. Figures stay economical and painterly, integrated into the design presentation, never photographed.", "590-paper-doll": "Render the image as a vintage mid-century paper-doll sheet printed on slightly yellowed cardboard, viewed perfectly flat. Draw one standing doll among separate cut-out garments and tiny accessories, each with white rectangular fold tabs and dotted scissor outlines. Use soft muted lithographic inks, stipple shading, fine outlines, and slight registration slip, with no cast shadows or environment. Elongated fashion-illustration proportions and playful spacing should feel like an old craft sheet.", "591-redscale": "Render the image as an authentic redscale film photograph, as if color negative stock were loaded backwards and exposed through its base. Push shadows to deep crimson and burgundy, midtones to burnt orange, and highlights to glowing golden yellow, with almost no blue. Add dense irregular grain, sunset halation, soft imperfect focus, and candid analog framing. The red-orange-yellow cast must cover the whole frame, not a mild warm tint.", "592-solarization": "Render the image as a Man Ray solarized black-and-white photograph, a 1930s silver-gelatin darkroom experiment. Apply Sabattier partial tone reversal so glowing silver Mackie lines trace the contours while some highlights sink to graphite and some shadows turn pale. Keep regions of positive and negative in the same print, with fine silver grain and a stark studio lamp. It must read as true solarization, not ordinary monochrome or digital edge tracing.", "593-kirlian": "Render the image as a 1970s Kirlian aura photograph on deep black film. Let electrophotographic corona discharge define the picture: intricate violet and cobalt filaments, branching sparks, and fuzzy electric halos around the silhouette and fingertips. Keep the interior mostly near-black, with irregular exposure bloom, silver-halide grain, and enormous black negative space. The mood is a mystical parapsychology lab contact print, not a conventional portrait with a glow filter.", "594-shibori": "Render the image as handmade Japanese shibori indigo cloth hanging to dry. Build the entire subject from resist-dyed pattern only: deep indigo and natural white, itajime folded geometry, nui-shibori stitched channels, concentric ties, and bleeding dye boundaries. Show puckered woven grain, fold creases, and a slightly billowing cloth clipped to a line. Nothing should look painted or photographic; every shape is dyed or undyed textile.", "595-marquetry": "Render the image as a polished wood marquetry panel assembled entirely from thin veneers. Construct every shape from walnut, maple, cherry, and ebony, with visible grain, fine seams, and curved cut edges, and no painted or rendered shading. Use flattened geometric perspective, stylized elongated proportions, and a narrow solid-wood border under gentle raking light. Color and tone come only from wood species and a satin-polished finish.", "596-enamel-pin": "Render the image as macro photography of a hard-enamel lapel pin on blue denim. Reduce the subject to a compact die-cut design with an oversized head, a tiny torso, thick polished gold outlines, and flat glossy enamel cells flush with the metal. Photograph it close and slightly angled above a pocket seam, with shallow depth of field, coarse twill, and a few simple neighboring pins. It must look manufactured and small, not like a life-size illustration.", "597-paint-by-numbers": "Render the image as a half-finished paint-by-numbers kit seen from directly overhead on a worktable. Segment the picture into hard-edged flat acrylic shapes; paint about half the cells and leave the rest as bare outlined regions with tiny unreadable guide marks. Set a few open paint pots and a fine used brush beside the board. Show real paint thickness, matte board, and hobby-kit simplification rather than photographic portraiture.", "598-fisher-price": "Render the image as 1970s Fisher-Price Little People toy photography. Turn the subject into a chunky wooden peg figure with a round painted head, a thick turned body, and no realistic limbs, posed inside a bright primary-color plastic playset. Use elevated three-quarter product lighting, glossy molded plastic, mild scuffs, and visible wood grain. Proportions must belong entirely to a vintage preschool toy, not a realistic person.", "599-fruit-crate-label": "Render the image as a 1930s California fruit-crate label lithograph inside an elaborate ornamental border of plants and geometry. Use vivid layered color plates in vermilion, golden yellow, orchard green, and cobalt, with an idealized sunlit landscape of orchard rows and distant mountains. Draw bold ink outlines, print grain, slight misregistration, and sweeping idealized advertising figures. The result should be period commercial lithography, not a filtered photograph.", "600-voronoi": "Render the image as pure generative art built from one irregular Voronoi tessellation. Fill the frame with contiguous convex polygons, mostly five- and six-sided, separated by thin consistent dark borders, each cell a single flat color sampled from the subject. Use smaller denser cells for fine detail and larger cells for broad areas, with no gaps, gradients, texture, or painterly shading. The look is crisp vector geometry, not low-poly triangles or mosaic tile.", "601-francis-bacon": "Render the image as a Francis Bacon oil painting. Smear a large distorted figure across a flat orange ground, mouth open, the body dragged with a rag, and box it in sparse black cage lines. Use raw pinks, bone white, and torn smears of color, with nearby objects half dissolved into the paint. The surface should feel violently brushed and wiped, an actual painting rather than a photograph or a filter.", "602-op-art": "Render the image as a Bridget Riley op-art painting. Turn the whole field into a precise black-and-white pattern of hard-edged bending stripes, and build the subject from those same stripes so the silhouette stays legible. Use no gray, no color, no soft shading, and no photographic base. The optical rhythm of the stripes must determine every edge, proportion, and spatial interval.", "603-de-chirico": "Render the image as a Giorgio de Chirico metaphysical painting. Set a silent arcade piazza under an empty ochre sky, with long hard shadows, deep receding perspective, and a tiny distant train. Place the subject large in the foreground, still as a mannequin, beside a sharply tilted plane. Paint it in uncanny oil with stylized anatomy and empty angular architecture, not as a photograph with a filter.", "604-yoshitomo-nara": "Render the image as a Yoshitomo Nara acrylic painting. Give the subject an enormous childlike head, a tiny body, and glaring widely spaced eyes against a flat pale ground, drawn with deliberate naivete. Keep the figure huge in the frame, reduced to simple flat shapes, with a tender but defiant mood. Use a subtly painted acrylic surface and almost no setting, an original painting rather than a photograph.", "605-jacob-lawrence": "Render the image as a Jacob Lawrence tempera painting. Use flat unmixed color in brown, yellow, green, and black, sharp diagonals, rhythmic radically simplified people, and angular limbs and faces. Build emphatic graphic negative space and a matte hand-painted tempera surface, with objects reduced to bold shapes. The picture must read as tempera from the start, with no photographic realism.", "606-remedios-varo": "Render the image as a Remedios Varo egg-tempera painting. Place a tall, precise, elongated figure in a narrow alchemical study, filling most of the height, surrounded by floating brass instruments. Paint with meticulous fine brush detail, layered glazes, and a softly luminous palette of warm ochre and moss. Turn ordinary objects into strange apparatus, and keep the space dreamlike, intricate, and free of photographic realism.", "607-ivan-bilibin": "Render the image as an Ivan Bilibin Russian fairy-tale illustration. Use crisp articulate black ink contours and flat watercolor washes, with decorative folk drawing of a pine forest and a carved wooden hut. Frame the scene inside an ornamental border of plants and geometry only. Keep stylized outlined forms, patterned timber, and restrained natural pigment on paper, with no photographic modeling.", "608-eyvind-earle": "Render the image as an Eyvind Earle storybook painting. Reduce the subject to a large foreground figure of graceful elongated graphic shapes, never a tiny detail lost in the landscape. Behind it, stack stylized horizontal bands of forest in gold, teal, and black, with intricate rhythmic tree forms and medieval elegance. Keep flat decorative shapes and exquisite pattern, a fully graphic painting rather than a photograph.", "609-saul-leiter": "Render the image as a Saul Leiter color photograph. See the subject large and off-center through a rain-wet window, in soft focus, with condensation and droplets partly veiling the view. Let abstract blocks of red and yellow in the glass dominate, while a 1950s city street melts across the pane. Use subdued vintage color film and painterly layering made by real reflections, not a modern portrait with a filter.", "610-nc-wyeth": "Render the image as an N.C. Wyeth adventure oil illustration, not a dry Andrew Wyeth tempera. Paint a heroic large figure on a windy rocky coast, with theatrical golden light, sculptural drapery, deep rich shadows, and broad confident brushwork. Use monumental storytelling anatomy, a low viewpoint, and an empty luminous sky above sea and stone. The surface should be vigorous book illustration in oil, never photorealism or a photo filter.", "611-kurosawa": "Render the image as an Akira Kurosawa black-and-white film still. Stage wind and slanting rain over open mud, with deep focus and compressed telephoto planes so the subject, the rain, and the distant landscape all stay sharp. Use severe luminous blacks and whites, fine silver grain, and a high-contrast storm sky. Diagonal weather and unposed cinematic gravity should drive the frame, with no color at all.", "612-suspiria": "Render the image as a 1977 Suspiria film still. Drown a baroque hallway of ornate wallpaper and elaborate doorframes in unnatural red, cobalt, and acid-yellow theatrical gels. Use saturated dye-transfer color, vintage 35mm grain, and an extreme off-center close view with geometric depth receding behind the subject. The light should feel designed and physical, not a filter laid over a photograph.", "613-jacques-tati": "Render the image as a Jacques Tati film still. Place the subject large in the foreground of an immense modernist glass lobby, a gray-beige grid of mullions, polished floors, and reflections held in deep focus. Keep mid-century color restrained and daylight cool and diffuse, with one strong chromatic accent against subdued tones. Framing stays deadpan, asymmetrical, and gently absurd.", "614-del-toro": "Render the image as a Guillermo del Toro dark fairy-tale film still. Set a damp subterranean chamber of carved stone, moss, and worn gold, with a pale intricate creature half-revealed in the shadow behind the subject. Use amber practical lantern light, wet gleam, and tactile practical-creature effects rather than digital gloss. Framing is dense, low, and asymmetrically intimate.", "615-color-wuxia": "Render the image as a Zhang Yimou color wuxia film still. Build the world from saturated yellow silk and falling golden ginkgo leaves, with gravity-defying movement, sweeping fabric, and a strong diagonal silhouette. Use luminous golden backlight, rhythmic negative space, and painterly atmospheric planes. Materials should feel tangible and cinematic, the palette almost a single operatic gold.", "616-fairly-oddparents": "Render the image as a Fairly OddParents cartoon frame. Use ultra-thick black angular outlines, flat candy colors in pink, mint, lavender, and aqua, and hard-edged cel fills with no gradients or shading. Heads become big and geometric, with wedge chins, oversized oval eyes, tiny noses, and small torsos on bent rubbery arms. Stage a wonky fairy-world diner of floating booths and star-shaped lights.", "617-ducktales": "Render the image as a 1987 DuckTales adventure-cartoon cel. Draw bold black outlines, rounded appealing proportions, oversized eyes, and a strong curved line of action, but keep the subject itself rather than turning it into a duck. Set a hand-painted treasure room of gold, jewels, and teal arches behind the flat cel. Warm gold light and exuberant low-angle staging should read as Saturday-morning television.", "618-regular-show": "Render the image as a Regular Show cartoon from early-2010s television. Use extremely simple shapes, slack deadpan posture, thin casual outlines, and flat color with almost no shading or texture. Place the subject large in an ordinary park, drawn with the same spare, slightly awkward limited animation. The mood stays bored and graphic, never polished illustration or anime.", "619-venture-bros": "Render the image as a Venture Bros cartoon frame. Use slightly awkward comic realism, thick uneven outlines, and muted rusty color with hard cel shading. Set a retro science compound of clunky machinery, corroded metal, and mid-century futurism gone to seed. Staging is deadpan and a little stiff, like early-2000s cable adventure animation rather than slick superhero art.", "620-scavengers-reign": "Render the image as a Scavengers Reign painted-animation frame. Surround the subject with soft alien plants and strange gentle creatures whose shapes feel grown rather than designed. Use thin delicate line, muted earth color, pale bioluminescent glows, and a gouache background that stays atmospheric behind a clearer foreground figure. The mood is ecological, slow, and eerie rather than heroic cartoon action.", "621-blue-eye-samurai": "Render the image as a Blue Eye Samurai painterly 3D frame. Shade volumes like ink wash, with graphic blocks of snow, dark timber, and an Edo street. Keep edges crisp and surfaces tactile, closer to a printed illustration than to smooth cartoon plastic. Cool gray light and falling snow bind the planes, while the subject stays large and close in the frame.", "622-jet-set-radio": "Render the image as Jet Set Radio cel-shaded 3D. Use thick black toon outlines, flat poster color, hard two-tone shading, and an exaggerated foreshortened street pose. Set a dense Tokyo-inspired city of concrete, rails, and walls covered in abstract painted shapes. It should feel like early-2000s game graphics, loud and graphic, not a photograph or a soft illustration.", "623-portal": "Render the image as a Portal (2007) in-game screenshot. Simplify the subject into a stylized real-time character with slightly plastic surfaces and clean game lighting, not a photograph. Place the subject in a sterile white modular test chamber with one glowing orange oval portal, one glowing blue oval portal, and a weighted storage cube. Surfaces stay smooth, clinical, and fluorescent.", "624-psychonauts": "Render the image as Psychonauts stylized 3D. Enlarge the head, stretch the limbs, and cover surfaces in visible painted texture instead of realistic materials. Set a whimsical mental world of giant teeth and a colorful brain-like landscape, bright and slightly queasy. Proportions stay squashy and storybook, lit like a mid-2000s platform game rather than a photograph.", "625-leonora-carrington": "Render the image as a Leonora Carrington surrealist painting. Set the subject among hybrid dream animals and intricate folklore creatures under moonlight, with ordinary objects turned ceremonial. Use muted greens, bone white, and smoky ochre, painted in fine obsessive detail on a dry, slightly chalky surface. Scale and symbolism should feel alchemical and uncanny, never loose or cartooned.", "626-chuck-close": "Render the image as a Chuck Close portrait painting. Let the head fill the canvas and rebuild it from a visible grid of handmade marks, so the likeness reassembles only from a distance. Each cell is a distinct dab, fingerprint, or color note, photographic and mosaic at once. The surface stays systematic, large, and obsessively crafted rather than smoothly blended.", "627-nihonga": "Render the image as a Nihonga painting on silk. Lay soft mineral pigments in thin veils, with a quiet touch of gold and delicate seasonal flowers. Light is indoor and hushed, edges dissolve, and the ground breathes like silk rather than paper. It must not look like a woodblock print or an ink sketch; the surface stays matte, powdered, and contemplative.", "628-edmund-dulac": "Render the image as an Edmund Dulac fairy-tale watercolor. Use powdery soft washes, hazy jewel colors, and a moonlit garden that melts at the edges. Keep the subject large and half-length, modeled with delicate stipple and luminous glazes rather than a hard outline. It should feel like a 1910s gift-book plate, not a flat miniature or a pen-and-ink drawing.", "629-mo-willems": "Render the image as a Mo Willems picture-book drawing. Leave lots of empty white space and describe the subject with one thick, slightly uneven black line and a single flat color. The expression goes deadpan, the shapes extremely simple, and the subject huge on the page. It should feel like a confident kindergarten marker sketch, spare, with no scenery competing.", "630-dav-pilkey": "Render the image as a Dav Pilkey children's-book drawing in the Captain Underpants vein. Use energetic uneven marker, goofy action lines, and bright childish color that spills on purpose. Proportions turn rubbery and exaggerated, with speed marks and a slapstick pose. The page should look like a kid's notebook cartoon, crude, loud, and joyful, not a tidy illustration.", "631-philippe-druillet": "Render the image as a Philippe Druillet comic painting. Crowd the background with baroque science-fiction architecture, heavy black structure, and dense metallic ornament while the subject stays large in front. Use acid color against near-black voids, obsessive line, and monumental scale that makes machinery feel like a temple. The surface is painted and airless, more metal-hued illustration than clean superhero comics.", "632-enki-bilal": "Render the image as an Enki Bilal painted comic panel. Use a cold blue-gray palette, scratched pigment, and a muddy future city dissolving into haze. Keep the subject large and close, modeled as if the paint were rubbed and abraded, with hard ink contours cutting through the pastel grime. Light stays flat and exhausted, the mood political and science-fictional rather than sleek or cartooned.", "633-jacques-tardi": "Render the image as a Jacques Tardi comic panel. Use murky ink and wash, heavy blacks, and a rainy Paris street of wet cobbles, ironwork, and dripping roofs. Draw with nervous precise line in a slightly grotesque clear-line tradition, the subject large against the weather. Color stays stained olive and tobacco, never bright, digital, or clean.", "634-strange-planet": "Render the image as a Strange Planet comic panel. Reduce every figure and object to a few rounded capsule shapes, with a face of only two solid black dot eyes and a tiny line mouth, and no nose. Use flat pastel pink and lavender, no shading and no texture, and turn props into plain geometric solids. It should feel like extreme graphic simplicity, not an ordinary cartoon.", "635-owl-house": "Render the image as a frame from The Owl House. Use bold pointed shapes, thick lively outlines, flat expressive color, and warm magic light, with silhouettes built from triangles and crescents. Set a cozy cursed room of witchy clutter, crooked furniture, and small glowing charms. The design stays graphic and slightly spooky, modern television fantasy rather than soft pastel or anime.", "636-aqua-teen": "Render the image as an Aqua Teen Hunger Force cartoon. Draw with extremely crude wobbly lines, flat grimy colors, and almost no shading, like early Flash animation left unfinished. Set a dingy room of stained surfaces and ugly simple props, and keep the subject itself, deadpan and large, rather than replacing it with a food mascot. The humor is cheap, grotesque, and deliberately ugly.", "637-my-little-pony": "Render the image as a My Little Pony: Friendship Is Magic cartoon. Use clean vector shapes, huge shiny eyes with catchlights, smooth outlines, and a soft pastel world of candy color and gentle gradients. Keep the subject itself, drawn in that style, rather than turning it into a pony. The finish should look like bright modern television animation, cute, polished, and evenly lit.", "638-she-ra": "Render the image as a 2018 She-Ra and the Princesses of Power cartoon. Use sharp graphic shapes, bold flat color, thick tapered outlines, and a heroic stance against a wide sunset sky. Design is angular and fashion-forward, with clean cel fills and a strong silhouette. It should feel like modern television animation, powerful and poster-like, not the 1980s series and not anime.", "639-bee-and-puppycat": "Render the image as a Bee and PuppyCat cartoon. Use soft lazy pastels, simple rounded drawing, thin relaxed outlines, and minimal shading in a muted cotton-candy haze. Set a gentle, slightly messy apartment, with one small strange puppy-cat creature nearby. The mood is drowsy and cute, like a plush web cartoon rather than high-energy television animation.", "640-kipo": "Render the image as a Kipo and the Age of Wonderbeasts cartoon. Use bold shapes, thick confident outlines, and highly saturated color in a vibrant mutant wilderness. Keep the subject large in the foreground, with weird friendly creatures and oversized flora behind. The style is modern television adventure: flat, graphic, and exuberant, with clean shapes and sunny contrast.", "641-belleville": "Render the image as a frame from The Triplets of Belleville. Stretch proportions into elongated caricature, with small heads, exaggerated limbs, and rubbery narrow silhouettes. Use sepia, olive, and tobacco, a French music-hall mood, and slightly grimy painted backgrounds. Line stays hand-drawn and elastic, weary and retro, never cute modern television animation.", "642-fellini": "Render the image as a Federico Fellini film still. Stage a dreamlike circus night with theatrical spotlights, deep black-and-white photography, and strange joyful figures crowding the background. Leave a single saturated accent as the only color in an otherwise monochrome frame. Grain, baroque excess, and carnival unreality should feel like 1960s Italian cinema, not a tinted snapshot.", "643-the-lighthouse": "Render the image as a still from The Lighthouse. Shoot harsh black and white, tight and close, with wet cloth, sea grit, and a lantern's glare cutting the dark. Use coarse grain and crushed shadows like damaged orthochromatic stock, the subject large and windblown. The mood is grim, salty, and claustrophobic, never romantic or softly graded.", "644-midsommar": "Render the image as a Midsommar film still. Flood the frame with blinding high-noon daylight, flower crowns, white embroidered folk dress, and a meadow feast where blossoms cover every surface. Color is pastel, clean, and slightly overexposed, the prettiness uneasy. Framing stays symmetrical and cult-like, horror staged as a sunny Scandinavian folk painting.", "645-gregory-crewdson": "Render the image as a Gregory Crewdson photograph. Build a meticulously staged suburban interior at blue twilight, with cinematic mixed lighting, uncanny quiet, and every prop placed like a film set. Use large-format sharpness, cool window light against a warm practical lamp, and a frozen, slightly troubled stillness. The subject is large in the foreground, not a tiny figure lost in the room.", "646-martin-parr": "Render the image as a Martin Parr photograph. Use harsh direct flash, saturated slightly garish color, and a close British seaside cafe where the subject fills the frame. Plastic tableware, bright packaging, and patterned surfaces should feel blunt and unflattering. The picture is social and comic, a 35mm snapshot pushed too far, not a polite travel photograph.", "647-masaaki-yuasa": "Render the image as a Masaaki Yuasa anime frame. Draw the subject like liquid, off-model on purpose, with a loose swimming line and flat unexpected color. Proportions squash, smear, and change within the pose, and any held object may stretch along with the motion. The animation should feel hand-drawn, elastic, and emotionally extreme, never tidy model-sheet anime.", "648-hyper-light-drifter": "Render the image as Hyper Light Drifter pixel art. Render the subject large and detailed, not as a tiny sprite, in neon pink and cyan against a ruined pastel wilderness. Use tight clusters of color, hard pixel edges, and long clean silhouettes. The world feels lonely, geometric, and luminous, like a modern indie game rather than mushy 8-bit nostalgia.", "649-ludwig-bemelmans": "Render the image as a Ludwig Bemelmans Madeline-book gouache. Paint with loose watery brushwork, slightly naive perspective, and crooked charming Paris buildings along a park or old street. Favor yellows and soft blues, with the subject large and prominent rather than a tiny figure in the scenery. The surface should show visible gouache strokes, charming and imperfect, like a mid-century children's book.", "650-new-yorker-cartoon": "Render the image as a classic New Yorker single-panel cartoon. Use a confident economical pen line and a single gray wash, with generous white paper and the subject large in the foreground. The mood is dry and sophisticated, an observed social moment rather than a slapstick gag. It should look like a printed magazine drawing, elegant and spare.", "651-saul-steinberg": "Render the image as a Saul Steinberg ink drawing. Use spare conceptual line on lots of white paper, where one witty contour becomes a street, a building, or a diagram. Keep the subject recognizable but reduced to a few decisive strokes, large against empty space. The humor is intellectual and graphic, a doodle turned into architecture, with at most one flat poster-like color.", "652-charles-addams": "Render the image as a Charles Addams cartoon. Use fine pen line and gray wash, a macabre Victorian house, and deadpan humor with something strange lurking just behind the subject. The subject stays large and oddly calm amid the gloom. Black is velvety, the architecture gothic and overgrown, the joke quiet rather than gory or cartoon-bright.", "653-tarkovsky": "Render the image as an Andrei Tarkovsky film still. Set damp earth, fog, and standing water, with a slow spiritual quiet and a muted palette of brown, moss, and gray. The subject is large in the foreground, observed in long breathing depth rather than as a distant silhouette. Light falls naturally and unevenly, like weathered 35mm, patient and elemental, never stylish or glossy.", "654-ozu": "Render the image as a Yasujirō Ozu film still. Place the camera low and still, in a quiet domestic interior of tatami, shoji, and a low table. Color is faded and orderly, the subject large within a balanced, unhurried composition lit by ordinary daylight. The feeling is restrained Japanese family cinema, with no dramatic angles or gloss.", "655-john-carpenter": "Render the image as a John Carpenter night film still. Use anamorphic lens flare, a practical flashlight beam, and a dark suburban street held in tense quiet. Widescreen staging, deep blacks, and a synth-era chill should isolate the subject large in the foreground. It must feel like early-1980s Panavision horror, grainy and predatory, not a modern digital grade.", "656-hammer-horror": "Render the image as a Hammer Horror film still. Use lush gothic color, candlelight, studio fog, and a shadowy castle of velvet drapery and carved stone. Place the subject large against rich crimson and bottle green, with inky shadows and a slightly artificial studio sky. It should look like 1960s British Technicolor horror, theatrical and painted rather than documentary darkness.", "657-park-chan-wook": "Render the image as a Park Chan-wook film still. Use precise symmetrical framing, rich green and gold, and elegant stillness composed to the inch. Light is controlled and luxurious, with lacquered surfaces, careful color blocking, and the subject large and formal. The mood is revenge-opera cinema: beautiful, exact, and faintly cruel, not handheld realism.", "658-baz-luhrmann": "Render the image as a Baz Luhrmann film still. Drown the frame in theatrical red and gold, sequins, and hot stage light, with a heavy curtain behind the subject. Everything should feel exuberant, artificial, and deliberately too much, like a musical number breaking into real life. Use saturated theatrical color, glitter, and swooning camera energy rather than natural light.", "659-animagic": "Render the image as Rankin/Bass Animagic stop-motion. Rebuild the subject as a handmade felt doll with a visible seam and button eyes, posed on a miniature holiday set. Use stitched cloth, yarn texture, and a slightly jerky puppet presence, clearly not a Muppet and not smooth clay. Warm practical miniature light, fake snow, and tangible fabric should feel like 1960s television craft.", "660-infinity-train": "Render the image as an Infinity Train cartoon frame. Place the subject large in a bold surreal train car built from graphic shapes and strange friendly machinery. Use flat color, clean modern outlines, and impossible interior design that still reads as a vehicle. The style is crisp television fantasy, colorful and geometric, more cartoon than science-fiction concept art.", "661-thundercats": "Render the image as a 1985 ThunderCats cartoon cel. Use bold black outlines, saturated color, and a heroic low-angle stance on a fantastical cliff. Keep the subject itself rather than turning it into a cat warrior, drawn with hard cel shadows and gleaming fantasy metal. It should look like 1980s American television animation, graphic and larger than life.", "662-real-monsters": "Render the image as an Aaahh Real Monsters cartoon frame. Use ugly-cute underground shapes, scratchy color, and lumpy uneven outlines in a monster dump of trash and tunnels. Keep the subject itself, large and deadpan, drawn in that grotesque Klasky Csupo style rather than turned into a monster. The palette is sickly and dim, the humor gross and childish, like 1990s television animation.", "663-kentucky-route-zero": "Render the image as Kentucky Route Zero game art. Use flat magic-realist illustration, a rural night road, and a limited antique palette of faded ink, cream, and dusk blue. Shapes are simple and stage-like, lighting quiet and strange, with the subject large against the highway dark. It should feel like a theatrical adventure-game painting, still and literary, not 3D and not a cartoon.", "664-bioshock": "Render the image as a BioShock game scene, not a photograph. Set a ruined underwater art-deco hall, leaking brass, cracked marble, and green water light pouring through ocean windows. The subject is a large stylized game character among the wreckage, with slightly exaggerated real-time materials. Atmosphere is drowned opulence: late-2000s cinematic game graphics, murky, ornate, and theatrical.", "665-mirrors-edge": "Render the image as a Mirror's Edge game scene, not a photograph. Use a clean white city, sharp red accents, and bright graphic daylight, the subject a large stylized character poised in motion. Surfaces stay smooth and almost poster-flat, the architecture minimal and sunlit. It should feel like late-2000s parkour graphics: crisp, high-key, and designed, never grimy or photoreal.", "666-takehiko-inoue": "Render the image as a Takehiko Inoue ink drawing. Use precise sumi brushwork and fine dry line, with quiet historical stillness and large areas of untouched paper. Keep the subject large in a spare composition, described by controlled strokes rather than screentone or color. The feeling is literary manga illustration: calm, observational, and exact, on bare paper.", "667-jillian-tamaki": "Render the image as a Jillian Tamaki comic illustration. Use a loose contemporary line, soft muted color, and an ordinary intimate moment rather than spectacle. Forms stay observed and slightly awkward, the line sketchy at the edges, with watercolor-like fills that slip outside the drawing. The mood is modern, tender, and everyday, like an indie graphic novel page.", "668-tillie-walden": "Render the image as a Tillie Walden comic panel. Use soft flat color, quiet science-fiction architecture, and wide tender space around a subject that stays large in the foreground. Palette favors dusk pink, pale blue, and warm yellow windows, with simplified shapes and gentle gradients. The feeling is lonely and intimate, a spacious graphic novel rather than detailed manga or a hard science-fiction render.", "669-jeff-lemire": "Render the image as a Jeff Lemire comic panel. Use sparse scratchy ink and thin watercolor in a lonely rural kitchen, the drawing raw and a little unfinished. Color is pale and washy, blacks nervous, and the subject large against empty domestic quiet. It should feel like an indie graphic novel, bleak and tender, not polished superhero art.", "670-amy-sherald": "Render the image as an Amy Sherald portrait painting. Render skin in gray tones, the gaze direct and calm, against a plain flat field of color. Turn clothing into a bold unshaded graphic shape, with no cast shadows and no busy setting. The handling is smooth contemporary oil, poised and frontal, more emblem than psychology.", "671-alice-neel": "Render the image as an Alice Neel portrait painting. Use a nervous blue-green outline, an awkward seated pose, and patchy acidic color on a bare unfinished ground. Distort proportions slightly, leave the drawing visible, and only roughly indicate the chair or room. The result should feel psychological and unfinished, intimate rather than flattering or smooth.", "672-david-lachapelle": "Render the image as a David LaChapelle fine-art editorial photograph. Use hyper-saturated color, glossy baroque theatrical props, and jewel-bright artificial studio lighting with polished specular highlights. Tilt the camera and pack the frame with gilded ornament, fuchsia drapery, and exuberant staged objects. The result should feel like a meticulously constructed maximalist photographic tableau, sharp and theatrical rather than candid.", "673-fargo": "Render the image as a Coen brothers Fargo film still. Use a static straight-on shot at seated eye level, unglamorous practical lighting, and restrained browns and institutional greens with analog cinematic texture. Place the scene in a sparsely occupied wood-paneled roadside cafe, with a vinyl booth, a laminate table, and a huge window of flat featureless white snow. Keep the mood deadpan, banal, and awkwardly quiet.", "674-sofia-coppola": "Render the image as a Sofia Coppola film still. Use soft overexposed pastel light, delicate 35mm grain, and halation in blown-out window highlights. Set a languid unposed moment in a lonely pretty hotel room of powder pink, pale mint, white linens, and gauze curtains, with airy negative space and an off-center composition. The mood should feel intimate, wistful, and slightly sad rather than glamorous or commercial.", "675-universal-monsters": "Render the image as a 1931 Universal monster-horror film still in strict black-and-white silver-gelatin monochrome. Use a gothic castle laboratory of rough stone arches, antique glass vessels, and electrical equipment, lit by harsh theatrical spotlights that carve hard expressionist shadows. Let blacks swallow whole areas, with glowing gray grain and antique optical softness. A distorted hulking shadow looms behind the subject, who stays wholly human.", "676-primal": "Render the image as a frame from Genndy Tartakovsky's Primal. Build the subject from heavy sculptural painterly shapes with almost no outlines, ferociously simple graphic masses, and boldly exaggerated angular anatomy. Set a still, brutal prehistoric wilderness of charcoal tree trunks, jagged ferns, desaturated jade and blue-green layers, and a small pale sky. Use flat planes of warm ocher and hard-edged shadow in a restrained black-green, slate, and bone palette.", "677-apichatpong": "Render the image as an Apichatpong Weerasethakul film still. Hold an unshowy shot at eye level in humid jungle daylight, with layered motionless tropical foliage pressing close around a simple weathered setting. Use soft overcast light, deep leaf greens, damp earth, subdued contrast, and subtly organic film texture. The moment should feel ordinary, still, and faintly uncanny, without supernatural spectacle.", "678-kore-eda": "Render the image as a Hirokazu Kore-eda film still. Use gentle natural window light in a modest, slightly untidy home of pale wood, translucent curtains, and ordinary kitchen clutter. Frame an unforced intimate domestic moment with soft cool-white light, muted ivory, restrained highlights, and subtle film grain. The feeling should stay quiet and affectionate, observed from inside a family room rather than staged or glamorous.", "679-de-palma": "Render the image as a Brian De Palma split-diopter shot from a 1970s psychological thriller. Keep two planes razor-sharp in one continuous frame: the subject enormous and pin-sharp in the near foreground, and a distant doorway equally sharp down a long corridor, with the middle distance visibly soft. Use amber practical light in front, cooler blue-green depth, directional shadows, and tactile analog grain. Show no divider line and no collage seam.", "680-catdog": "Render the image as a 1990s CatDog cartoon frame in the Klasky Csupo house style, keeping the subject human rather than an animal hybrid. Use thick irregular ink outlines, lopsided geometric heads, ungainly elastic limbs, asymmetrical cartoon eyes, and wonky hand-drawn perspective. Fill a cluttered suburban yard with a leaning fence, patchy grass, and crooked house shapes in sour yellow-green, dirty lavender, and faded turquoise. Keep the cel color flat, ugly-cute, and free of gradients.", "681-teen-titans": "Render the image as a 2003 Teen Titans cartoon frame. Draw anime-influenced Western television animation with bold simplified silhouettes, angular almond eyes, an oversized head and hands, thick clean ink contours, and large flat cel-color shapes with sharply limited shadows. Place the subject on a city rooftop at dusk against a simplified skyline in lavender, indigo, and coral. It should look like a hand-drawn animation cel over a painted graphic background.", "682-x-men-92": "Render the image as a 1992 X-Men animated series cel. Use thick 1990s Marvel ink contours, angular faces with relatively small eyes, broad shoulders, and powerful heroic proportions. Apply saturated jewel-tone cel color, decisive black shadow shapes, and hard-edged two-tone shading. Stage a dramatic low-angle heroic stance against steep urban perspective and a bright blue sky, like a 1992 production cel rather than modern anime or 3D.", "683-batman-beyond": "Render the image as a Batman Beyond cartoon frame. Draw severe angular future-noir geometry, with wedge-shaped features, small angular eyes, long flat limbs, thin-to-bold black contours, and huge areas of pure black. Use hard cel shading in navy and black, pierced by electric magenta and cyan, on a night rooftop of minimal futuristic towers. Keep the subject human, with no bats, capes, or emblems, like a late-1990s animated production cel.", "684-aeon-flux": "Render the image as an Aeon Flux animation frame. Draw liquid 1990s experimental linework with sinuous unstable contours, radically elongated limbs and neck, a small stretched head, and strange compressed perspective. Place the subject in a sparse biomechanical room of ribbed walls, cablelike loops, and abstract vents. Use large flat areas of dusty ocher, desaturated teal, bone gray, and clay red so the distortion feels elegant, graphic, and unsettling rather than gory.", "685-leiji-matsumoto": "Render the image as a Leiji Matsumoto anime cel. Stretch the subject into an extraordinarily tall, extremely lanky silhouette with impossibly long limbs, a narrow torso, a delicate angular face, and enormous reflective eyes. Use sparse elegant ink contours and restrained vintage cel color. Set a full-length profile in a starship corridor of receding window ribs and an immense midnight-blue starfield, with a melancholy, wistful, quietly intimate mood.", "686-monogatari": "Render the image as a Monogatari anime frame. Use radical graphic composition, sharply delineated flat color blocks, and empty space treated as a strong geometric shape. Tilt the subject sharply and crop a large diagonal bust against huge vermilion and chalk-white planes, black angular shadows, and high-contrast flat cel shading. Precise anime linework and exaggerated perspective should dominate, with no soft background and no photographic modeling.", "687-littlebigplanet": "Render the image as a LittleBigPlanet craft-world scene. Turn the subject into a knitted character with an oversized rounded plush head, very short soft limbs, chunky yarn loops, visible seams, and button eyes. Build a handmade landscape of corrugated cardboard, cloth trees, dangling felt clouds, and stitched patchwork ground. Warm game-render light should reveal yarn fuzz, thread, buttons, and raw cardboard edges, reading as a stylized diorama rather than a photograph.", "688-outer-wilds": "Render the image as an Outer Wilds game scene. Use warm handmade low-poly art with faceted geometry and painterly textures, never a photograph. Place a timber campfire and rough wooden camp in the foreground beneath a huge alien planet that fills the sky with broad swirling cloud bands and a soft celestial glow. Contrast amber firelight with cool teal-blue night, angular pines, and a starry indigo sky so the mood feels cozy, curious, and cosmic.", "689-chicory": "Render the image as Chicory game art, a whimsical coloring-book world. Draw clean confident black outlines of nearly uniform thickness around simple enclosed organic shapes, and leave almost everything pure white. Fill only a few separated regions with soft flat color, with no shading or gradients. Use charming cartoon proportions, a big round head and a squat body, seen from a slightly overhead view among looping paths and simplified plants.", "690-krazy-kat": "Render the image as a George Herriman Krazy Kat comic drawing. Use poetic wiggly dip-pen ink, elastic eccentric cartoon anatomy, and spare Sunday-strip color in muted rust, ocher, and lavender on pale paper. Set a shifting Southwestern desert where the horizon changes direction and striped mesas sit at incompatible scales, with a lone brick in the scene. Strange contradictory perspective should feel integral, not like a filter laid over realism.", "691-edward-koren": "Render the image as an Edward Koren cartoon. Cover the subject, the furniture, and nearby objects in fuzzy hairy pen-and-ink, with innumerable nervous bristling strokes and spindly uncertain contours. Use a close low viewpoint, awkward friendly proportions, and lots of white gaps among the hairs. Stay mostly black and white, with only a faint restrained color wash, so the mood feels anxious, cozy, and absurd rather than smooth or polished.", "692-william-steig": "Render the image as a William Steig drawing. Use a wiggly, neurotic, very economical ink line and deliberately lumpy primitive shapes: a potato-like head, a bulbous nose, tiny uneven eyes, and knobby mitten hands. Float small watercolor patches that drift past their edges, and leave lots of untouched white paper with no filled background. The drawing should feel impulsive, awkward, and charming, never prettified or densely hatched.", "693-peter-arno": "Render the image as a Peter Arno 1930s society cartoon. Use confident thick brush-ink contours, economical caricature, and fluid gray washes on white paper, anchored by one dramatically dominant solid-black shape. Suggest an elegant nightclub with a few deft marks and abundant untouched paper. Give the subject an oversized head and sweeping expressive limbs in period cartoon proportions, and let assertive black brushwork carry the wit rather than realistic modeling.", "694-lyonel-feininger": "Render the image as a Lyonel Feininger painting. Build a crystalline city of overlapping luminous prismatic planes, soaring faceted buildings, and fractured shafts of light along severe diagonals. Break the subject into an elongated faceted figure of interlocking wedges, painted with the same translucent angular geometry as the street. Use cool ultramarine, smoky lilac, and pale gold, with fine uneven brush edges, so the whole image reads as painted geometry rather than a filtered photograph.", "695-pierre-bonnard": "Render the image as a Pierre Bonnard painting of an intimate patterned domestic room. See the scene obliquely, with a patterned tablecloth, fruit, wallpaper, and sunlit fabric dissolving into one another through vibrating small brush touches, softened boundaries, and no rigid perspective. Dapple radiant yellow and orange with violet shadows, rose, and verdigris, keeping the subject close, cropped, and absorbed into the oil-painted color tapestry.", "696-alex-katz": "Render the image as an Alex Katz painting. Crop a huge calm face so it occupies most of the frame, built from hard-edged flat shapes. Every color region must be completely uniform, as if cut from one sheet of opaque paper, with no gradients, shading, shadows, highlights, or texture. Reduce features to a few spare lines and solid silhouettes against one flat background color so the result reads as matte reductive hard-edge portrait painting.", "697-sonia-delaunay": "Render the image as a Sonia Delaunay painting. Cover the figure and the surrounding room with rhythmic Orphist circles, intersecting arcs, and blocks of pure saturated color. Build the subject from painted planes so it stays readable amid the abstraction, with flattened space and assertive warm and cool relationships. Use genuine pigment and visible brushwork rather than a photographic base, so the geometry feels interlocking, rhythmic, and decorative.", "698-daido-moriyama": "Render the image as a Daido Moriyama photograph. Shoot abrasive black-and-white street photography with extreme gritty grain, crushed blacks, blown highlights, imperfect focus, and harsh immediacy. Push the subject extremely close in a tilted, uncomfortable diagonal crop, leaving only a sliver of stark pavement and black architectural shadow behind. Embrace coarse analog grain and graphic black-and-white masses so the picture feels raw, candid, and abrasive rather than a clean portrait.", "699-dicorcia": "Render the image as a Philip-Lorca diCorcia photograph. Isolate the subject in a precisely placed flash on a daylight street while the background falls unexpectedly dark. Use a measured distance, rich controlled color, crisp flash on the figure, and deep cool shadows, with plausible daylight still visible past shaded storefronts. The ordinary instant should feel suspended, enigmatic, and cinematically staged, optically convincing rather than filtered.", "700-ernest-celestine": "Render the image as an Ernest & Celestine watercolor-animation frame. Use soft spontaneous pencil contours and translucent watercolor washes on warm white paper, with airy unfinished edges and economical hand-drawn detail. Set a modest French storybook room, lightly sketched, leaving pools of bare paper and delicate blooms around the drawing. Keep charming simplified proportions and a tender palette of honey, warm brown, and blue-gray, with no photographic base.", "701-corporate-memphis": "Render the image as 2010s Corporate Memphis flat vector art. Reduce the subject to an outline-free pill-shaped body, a tiny head with no nose and a small smile, and unequal rubber-hose limbs frozen in a playful unbalanced pose. Float coral, teal, and navy organic blobs plus simplified plant cutouts on an off-white ground. Every shape is one solid color, with no gradients, shading, texture, or depth.", "702-distracted-boyfriend": "Render the image as the Distracted Boyfriend stock-photo meme in one continuous street photograph. Make the subject large in the foreground, seen from a three-quarter rear angle while turning back with a guilty smile, as exactly two smaller adults walk ahead and one glances back in annoyance. Use bright daylight, a sunlit sidewalk, shallow depth of field, and clean commercial stock color. The candid staging should be instantly recognizable.", "703-woman-yelling-at-a-cat": "Render the image as the Woman Yelling at a Cat meme, divided by a hard vertical seam into two equal photographic panels. On the left, use a tight reality-show dinner shot of the subject leaning forward, mouth open, one finger pointing toward the right. On the right, seat a confused white cat before a plate of green salad under slightly mismatched candid light. Keep glossy television illumination and realistic texture, with no captions.", "704-this-is-fine": "Render the image as the This Is Fine webcomic. Draw a crude deadpan cartoon with thick uneven black marker outlines and very simple naive shapes, each filled with one flat uniform color. Show a straight-on side view of the subject seated calmly at a tiny table while solid orange and red flames surround the whole room. Use no gradients, shading, or texture, so the joke is absurd stillness inside the fire.", "705-drake-format": "Render the image as the Drake Hotline Bling two-panel meme. Stack exactly two horizontal panels with no captions, both showing the same subject large from the chest up. In the top panel the subject turns away in disgust and rejects what is offered; in the bottom panel the subject leans in, smiles, and points with approval. Use saturated music-video lighting, a warm orange-yellow backdrop, and magenta highlights, with exaggerated instantly readable gestures.", "706-expanding-brain": "Render the image as the Expanding Brain meme in flat internet illustration. Stack exactly four horizontal panels edge to edge, each filled by the same subject, with the head growing larger and brighter from top to bottom. Move from an ordinary simplified portrait to a brain-glow, then a cosmic head of violet and cyan stars, then a galaxy-filled cranium nearly filling the last strip. Use crisp outlines, flat shapes, and graphic neon glow, not a medical diagram.", "707-wojak": "Render the image as a crude Wojak meme drawing. Put one lopsided bust large on a plain white field, drawn with uneven black mouse lines, dot eyes, and a tiny wobbly mouth. Keep flat unshaded color, almost no interior detail, and no gradients, background, or scene. The result should stay awkward and unfinished, like a low-effort paint-program doodle rather than polished illustration or anime.", "708-always-has-been": "Render the image as the Always Has Been astronaut meme in clean digital illustration. Cast the subject as a large foreground astronaut in a white suit, seen from a low three-quarter angle, pointing back at a distinctly smaller second astronaut, with Earth hanging in star-filled space behind them. Use strong foreshortening, crisp contours, and confident digital shading, with cool blue Earth light and a warm sunlit edge. Keep it graphic and readable, with no captions and no weapons.", "709-spider-man-pointing": "Render the image as the Spider-Man pointing meme in flat retro comic illustration. Two matching figures face each other in a narrow brick alley and point at one another, large enough to fill the frame. Both wear red-and-blue web-pattern hoods, sleeves, and leggings. Use bold black outlines, flat color, and clean graphic shapes.", "710-surprised-pikachu": "Render the image as the Surprised Pikachu reaction, keeping the subject instead of replacing it with a yellow creature. Fill the frame with an extreme close-up of a blank stunned stare: a perfectly round open mouth and enormous empty circular eyes with tiny pupils. Use simple late-1990s television anime drawing, flat color, firm dark outlines, almost no shading, and a pale soft green background.", "711-deep-fried-meme": "Render the image as a deep-fried internet meme. Overprocess it as if the picture were repeatedly screenshotted, sharpened, and JPEG-compressed: giant macroblocks, chromatic bleed, and nuclear orange, red, yellow, and cyan saturation. Crush the blacks, blow the highlights, and add sharpening halos, random starburst flares, posterized bands, coarse noise, and a slight tilt. Let the eyes glow through the damage so the result looks hideously loud and endlessly reposted, rather than lightly filtered.", "712-roll-safe": "Render the image as the Roll Safe meme photograph. Show the subject tapping one index finger to the temple and looking into the camera with a knowing, slightly crooked closed-mouth smile and one eyebrow raised. Frame a tight candid waist-up snapshot in warm indoor light, with a soft out-of-focus interior, mild grain, and slightly imperfect focus. It should feel like an inexpensive casually captured reaction photo, not a polished portrait.", "713-bernie-chair": "Render the image as the folded-chair winter meme. Sit the subject alone on a metal folding chair in a cold gray outdoor event plaza, wrapped in an oversized parka and knitted mittens with the arms crossed. Use candid news-photo framing, flat overcast light, empty asphalt, and subdued cold color, with the figure and chair filling most of the frame. Keep it awkward, mundane, and documentary, with no other people and no signs.", "714-lisa-frank": "Render the image as Lisa Frank 1990s sticker art. Use airbrushed neon gradients, glossy highlights, candy-colored outlines, and extremely saturated hot pink, purple, and turquoise. Surround the subject with rainbows, leaping dolphins, kittens, stars, sparkles, and rainbow clouds in a dense sticker-sheet layout. Keep exaggerated cheerful proportions and smooth glittering shine, with every element equally illustrated and with no photographic lighting or texture.", "715-darkest-dungeon": "Render the image as Darkest Dungeon game art. Use scratchy gothic ink, angular woodcut-like strokes, and massive areas of pure black around a grim hard-edged painted face. Set a tight low-angle hero portrait in a narrow stone corridor, where a wall torch throws pale ocher light across one side and everything else drops into graphic black. Limit color to dirty parchment tones plus one blood-red accent so it looks handmade and oppressive, rather than photographic.", "716-beksinski": "Render the image as a Zdzisław Beksiński surreal painting. Build a desolate ocher and ash-gray dream of bone-like architecture, fog, and immense decayed organic towers ribbed like weathered vertebrae. Elongate the foreground figure into an eerie draped silhouette integrated with the ruins, rendered in meticulous oil texture under diffuse sourceless light. Keep a silent dusty atmosphere, haunted and still, without gore.", "717-kaws": "Render the image as a KAWS companion-figure artwork. Turn the subject into a glossy inflated vinyl toy with a huge rounded head, a squat simplified body, oversized hands, and bold X-shaped marks drawn over the eyes. Stand the figure large against a plain muted gradient, with polished sculptural highlights and a smooth collectible surface. It should read as designed pop-art vinyl rather than a photograph, and the X marks stay purely pictorial.", "718-faith-ringgold": "Render the image as a Faith Ringgold story-quilt painting. Place a flat narrative scene in a center panel, stitched into cloth with pieced borders of colorful triangles and stylized flowers, visible seams, and irregular stitching. Use bright cloth colors, simplified figures, and compressed tilted space, with brush marks and woven texture running edge to edge. Border patterns stay nonverbal shapes only, and the result should read as a quilt painting, not a photograph.", "719-diane-arbus": "Render the image as a Diane Arbus photograph. Use 1960s medium-format black-and-white film, direct hard on-camera flash, fine grain, and sharp unsentimental detail in the characteristic square framing. Place the subject frontally in an ordinary modest room, looking straight into the lens with an unguarded, slightly strange expression. Keep the setting sparse and the flash shadow hard so the mood feels peculiar and everyday, rather than glamorous.", "720-don-bluth": "Render the image as a Don Bluth 1980s animated film frame. Use hand-inked painted cels over a lush painted background, with expressive glowing eyes, dramatic character drawing, and warm shafts of light through floating dust. Hide small detailed creatures in the deep cool shadows, and contrast that darkness with warm gold. The atmosphere should feel softly luminous and storybook-dark, like feature animation rather than a photograph or a 3D render.", "721-dragons-crown": "Render the image as a Vanillaware fantasy painting in the manner of Dragon's Crown. Use lush painterly detail, ornate costume shapes, and boldly exaggerated heroic proportions under glowing warm tavern light. Favor sumptuous gold and jewel colors, sweeping silhouettes, and a low grand viewpoint that keeps the subject large in the foreground. Surround the figure with lavish painted surroundings so the image reads as hand-painted fantasy game art, rather than a photograph.", "722-ace-attorney": "Render the image as Ace Attorney courtroom game art. Use extremely sharp angular anime lines, bold flat color, and crisp cel shadows inside a wooden courtroom. Lean the subject forward in the series' dramatic objection pose, one foreshortened index finger thrust toward the viewer. Keep a tight dynamic bust, a strong diagonal gesture, and exaggerated perspective on the pointing hand, with no speech bubbles, names, or interface graphics.", "723-charlie-and-lola": "Render the image as Lauren Child collage picture-book art, in the manner of Charlie and Lola. Combine photographic scraps and patterned paper with naive childlike drawing, quirky wobbly ink outlines, and flat cut-paper shapes. Give the subject a large loosely drawn head, tiny features, and wonky limbs, surrounded by lots of clean white space and mismatched paper perspectives. Photographic bits are craft materials inside the illustration, never a photo of the subject.", "724-dont-hug-me": "Render the image as a Don't Hug Me I'm Scared puppet scene. Build the subject as a felt puppet with yarn, stitched features, shiny plastic eyes, stubby fabric limbs, and obvious seams, placed in a bright children's-television kitchen. Let the candy-colored set begin to rot through mildew patches, sagging fabric, stained cardboard, and peeling paint. Use cheerful stage lighting with an uncanny mood so it looks like a practical craft puppet set, rather than a living person or a drawing.", "725-friedrich": "Render the image as a Caspar David Friedrich romantic oil painting. Place a large solitary figure in the foreground, seen mostly from behind, facing a vast cold landscape of fog, black pines, and a ruined abbey. Keep the light pale and directional, with gray-blue distance and a thin band of dawn. Paint smoothly, with crisp silhouettes and almost no visible brush texture, so the mood stays sublime and quiet.", "726-renoir": "Render the image as a Pierre-Auguste Renoir impressionist oil. Break every edge into feathery, comma-shaped strokes and let the face soften into rosy light. Fill a sunlit terrace with dappled greens, cream, and coral, so neighboring colors mix in the eye rather than along a hard contour. Keep the paint lush and affectionate, with no hard outlines and no photographic detail.", "727-cezanne": "Render the image as a Paul Cézanne painting. Build the figure and a tipped tabletop from short parallel strokes, so fruit, cloth, and the body are all patches of muted blue, ochre, and green. Let the perspective stay slightly wrong and the forms feel solid, heavy, and constructed. Keep the color dry and the brush visible, with no atmospheric blur and no slick finish.", "728-leger": "Render the image as a Fernand Léger machine painting. Turn the figure into tubular cylinders and flat disks, separated by heavy black contours. Use only flat primary red, yellow, and blue, plus gray and black, with industrial beams and pipes behind. Keep every shape hard-edged and mechanical, with no blending, texture, or naturalistic anatomy.", "729-koons": "Render the image as a Jeff Koons balloon sculpture photographed in a white gallery. Twist the subject into a seamless, mirror-polished inflated form, with candy-colored reflections sliding across chrome. Add a matching twisted balloon prop nearby. Use bright even gallery light and sharp focus, with no brushwork, so it reads as a giant reflective object rather than a painting.", "730-giger": "Render the image as an H.R. Giger biomechanical airbrush painting. Fuse the figure with ribbed tubes, bone-like armor, and smooth machine parts in cold graphite, bone, and oily black. Keep the rendering glassy and precise, with no visible brush marks and no gore. Set it in a dark cathedral of metal, lit by a thin rim of silver.", "731-parrish": "Render the image as a Maxfield Parrish painting. Place an idealized figure on pale rocks beneath a huge luminous cobalt sky, with theatrical golden light and crisp, almost enamel edges. Smooth the modeling until cloth and skin look glazed, and let distant mountains glow. Set that intense blue against warm amber, with no rough texture and no loose impressionist strokes.", "732-hirschfeld": "Render the image as an Al Hirschfeld ink caricature. Draw with a few continuous black lines on bare white paper, theatrical and economical, one flowing stroke for the hair and another for the gesture. Leave almost everything unshaded. Use no gray wash, no color, no background detail, and no caption.", "733-norstein": "Render the image as a Yuri Norstein cutout animation frame, in the manner of Hedgehog in the Fog. Build the scene from layered paper, soft pencil grain, and translucent sheets of fog. Keep the figure a handmade paper puppet, slightly stiff, moving through a pale forest. Use gentle blur between layers and muted gray-green, with the tenderness of stop-motion rather than slick digital animation.", "734-hosoda": "Render the image as a Mamoru Hosoda anime film frame. Use clean modern digital animation, simple expressive eyes, soft cel shading, and a bright summer suburb with sun on ordinary buildings. Keep outlines thin and consistent, colors warm and clear, and the light optimistic. No retro grain, no gothic shadow, and no photoreal texture.", "735-mizuki": "Render the image as a Shigeru Mizuki manga panel. Draw the person with simple bold cartoon ink, and place one highly detailed traditional yokai beside them, rendered with dense folkloric texture. Use flat gray tones and a nighttime Japanese street. Keep the person large and the creature secondary. No speech balloons and no lettering.", "736-obata": "Render the image as a Takeshi Obata manga illustration. Use razor-sharp ink, fine individual hair lines, and gothic black clothing shapes against a stark white or deep black interior. Light the face with hard high-contrast patches and keep every line cold, thin, and precise. Leave the background almost empty. No screentone clutter, no soft watercolor, and no readable text.", "737-ikuhara": "Render the image as a Kunihiko Ikuhara anime frame, in the manner of Revolutionary Girl Utena. Use flat theatrical color, long hard shadows, falling rose petals, and severe school-architecture geometry. Freeze the figure in a dramatic posed silhouette. Keep the palette black, white, and deep red, with no soft gradients and no realistic lighting.", "738-nihei": "Render the image as a Tsutomu Nihei manga panel. Fill the background with an enormous brutalist megastructure drawn in dense technical ink, tiny hatches, and black voids. Keep the figure large in the foreground so the architecture reads as scale behind it. Stay black and white, precise, and airless, with no pretty gray wash and no lettering.", "739-asano": "Render the image as an Inio Asano manga panel. Draw the figure with a thin nervous line over a photoreal, slightly washed-out background of an ordinary apartment. Keep the color muted, the light flat, and the mood still and deadpan, as if nothing in particular is happening. No idealized anime eyes, no action effects, and no readable signage.", "740-schuiten": "Render the image as a François Schuiten architectural comic. Draw impossible Beaux-Arts machinery and a vast station interior in precise ligne-claire ink, then wash it with muted watercolor. Keep the line thin, the perspective grand, and the figure clearly separate from the architecture. No loose sketching and no speech balloons.", "741-mattotti": "Render the image as a Lorenzo Mattotti painted comic. Build the scene in chalky pastel, with expressionist color doing the drawing: a violet night, hot orange, and heavy texture instead of clean ink. Let the face shift with the color. No hard digital outlines, no flat cel shading, and no text.", "742-vallotton": "Render the image as a Félix Vallotton woodcut. Reduce the figure to huge flat black shapes on cream paper, with only a few sharp white cuts for the face and one prop. Reduce the interior to two or three shapes. No hatching, no gray, and no color beyond black and the paper.", "743-moreau": "Render the image as a Gustave Moreau symbolist oil. Encrust the scene with jewel-like detail, gold ornament, and a mythic interior, while the figure stays dreamy and still. Let patterns cover cloth and architecture alike. Keep the paint rich and miniature-like, with no modern flat color and no casual brushwork.", "744-redon": "Render the image as an Odilon Redon pastel. Let a dreamlike head and flowers emerge from soft charcoal darkness into glowing blue and gold. Keep the edges powdery and the space undefined, as if the image were half dream and half bouquet. No hard outlines, no daylight realism, and no narrative setting.", "745-homer": "Render the image as a Winslow Homer watercolor of a rough sea. Use wet-into-wet washes, reserved paper for spray and light, and a rugged shore under a cold sky. Keep the color restrained: gray-green water, dull ochre, and a few dark notes. No oil-paint thickness, no decorative prettiness, and no text.", "746-cassatt": "Render the image as a Mary Cassatt pastel. Set an intimate domestic moment against patterned wallpaper, drawn with soft diagonal hatching and tender, close light. Keep the color gentle, the pastel touch dry and chalky, and the crop near enough that the room feels private. No hard outlines, no heroic drama, and no photographic sharpness.", "747-vallejo": "Render the image as a Boris Vallejo fantasy oil painting. Use smooth airbrushed modeling, heroic gloss, jewel colors, and a strong rim light against a mythic sky. Keep the figure monumental and the finish closer to a polished illustration than to a photograph. No text and no loose painterly mess.", "748-wrightson": "Render the image as a Bernie Wrightson pen illustration. Cover the shadows with obsessive crosshatching until the blacks feel wet and gothic, inside a highly detailed old room of wood, cloth, and clutter. Keep the line fine and the detail relentless, with light barely scratching through the ink. No color, no gore, no digital airbrush, and no lettering.", "749-whistler": "Render the image as a James McNeill Whistler nocturne. Reduce the scene to a few veiled tones of blue-gray, black, and a thin streak of gold, with the figure barely emerging from fog over dark water. Soften every edge until shapes feel breathed on rather than drawn. Keep the surface thin and elegant, with no bright local color and no crisp detail.", "750-manet": "Render the image as an Édouard Manet oil painting. Use flat modern light, broad assured strokes, and strong blacks, and set the figure close against a shallow background. Keep the gaze direct and unsentimental, and the color dry: cream, black, muted green, and a note of red. No soft academic glaze and no fussy finish.", "751-tanguy": "Render the image as an Yves Tanguy surrealist oil. Stretch a vast empty plain to a low horizon under a pale sky, and turn the foreground figure into a large smooth biomorphic form among pebble-like and bone-like objects. Cast long precise shadows. Model everything with invisible brushwork, as if the forms were polished and half alive. No architecture, no narrative action, and no thick paint.", "752-condo": "Render the image as a George Condo psychological portrait. Fracture the face into several expressions at once, with bulging eyes, clipped cartoon features, and cubist planes jammed into one head. Use raw, slightly ugly color and visible scraping. Keep the mood manic and intimate, with no prettiness and no single calm expression.", "753-calder": "Render the image as an Alexander Calder mobile. Build the subject from flat sheets of red, yellow, and blue, joined by black wire into discs, arcs, and simple limbs, hanging in open white space. Use no shading and no texture. It should feel like a playful abstract sculpture, balanced and weightless, rather than a painting of a person.", "754-giacometti": "Render the image as an Alberto Giacometti sculpture. Stretch the figure into a rough, eroded stride, pencil-thin, with a pitted bronze surface and a small heavy head. Set it alone in a gray, scratched space, as if walking out of nothing. No smooth anatomy, no color beyond bronze and gray, and no background story.", "755-mickalene": "Render the image as a Mickalene Thomas mixed-media portrait. Combine 1970s pattern, wood paneling, rhinestone sparkle, and collaged photographic texture around a posed, glamorous figure. Use hot brown, gold, emerald, and pink, with flat decorative shapes and a direct, full studio-portrait attitude. No shy lighting and no plain empty background.", "756-aoshima": "Render the image as a Chiho Aoshima superflat digital print. Use slick vector-smooth color, a cute face with large eyes, and a sugary apocalyptic landscape of candy color, waves, and eerie plants. Keep every edge perfectly clean, with no brush texture and no realistic depth. The mood should be sweet and slightly menacing at once.", "757-sorayama": "Render the image as a Hajime Sorayama chrome airbrush painting. Turn the figure into glossy metal with razor-sharp reflections, pinpoint highlights, and a soft airbrushed gradient behind. Keep the finish closer to a polished robot illustration than to a photograph. No brush marks, no matte paint, and no readable logos.", "758-mcquarrie": "Render the image as a Ralph McQuarrie concept-art painting. Use broad opaque gouache, a hazy cinematic sky, and a large figure in the foreground against a vast designed world of dust, machines, and distant structures. Set warm dust color against a cool sky. Keep it painterly but clear, like a film production painting, with no digital gloss and no comic ink.", "759-trnka": "Render the image as a Jiří Trnka puppet-film frame. Build the figure as a carved wooden puppet with a painted face, jointed limbs, and a cloth costume, standing on a small theatrical set. Use warm practical light, visible wood grain, and the slight stiffness of stop-motion. No slick CGI and no drawn cartoon line.", "760-hayashida": "Render the image as a Q Hayashida manga panel. Use scratchy, crowded ink, grimy streets, and blunt confident drawing, with grotesque cartoon extras kept secondary to the main figure. Keep the image dense, dirty, and funny-ugly, in flat gray with almost no empty space. No clean hero-comic line, no glossy color, and no lettering.", "761-sfar": "Render the image as a Joann Sfar ink-and-watercolor comic. Draw with a loose, inky, slightly clumsy line, then puddle thin watercolor in ochre, olive, and red. Leave the paper showing, the hands a little wild, and the anatomy cheerfully off. No tight digital inking, no cel shading, and no speech balloons.", "762-hasui": "Render the image as a Hasui Kawase shin-hanga woodblock print. Use flat printed color, a fine black keyblock, and a quiet landscape in rain or dusk, with the figure large in the foreground. Favor deep blue, gray-green, and a few warm lights. Show a little paper grain and misregistration. No photography and no lettering.", "763-mughal": "Render the image as a Mughal miniature painting. Use a fine outline, flat jewel color, and a figure in patterned court dress set in a shallow flowering garden. Keep the scale intimate and the detail miniature, with gold accents and no Western perspective. No calligraphy, no realistic light, and no cast shadows.", "764-thangka": "Render the image as a Tibetan thangka painting. Place a frontal, symmetrical figure in a flat sacred composition, surrounded by a halo, a lotus, stylized clouds, and a precise ornamental border. Use mineral red, gold, deep blue, and green, with fine even line and no cast shadows. No writing, no seals, and no realistic depth.", "765-brom": "Render the image as a Brom dark-fantasy oil painting. Use muddy earth color, bruised shadow, leather and iron, and a cold firelit gloom around a monumental figure. Paint with rough, heavy realism and a menacing fairy-tale mood, as if the scene were lit by one mean flame. No bright comic color, no clean digital airbrush, and no cheerful light.", "766-ryden": "Render the image as a Mark Ryden pop-surrealist painting. Give the figure an oversized porcelain head, huge glassy eyes, and tiny meticulous detail, surrounded by toys and curious little symbols on a dark ground. Paint as smooth as a toy, cute and unsettling at once. No loose brushwork and no ordinary portrait lighting.", "767-briggs": "Render the image as a Raymond Briggs colored-pencil illustration. Build the scene from soft, layered pencil hatching on textured paper, with a dumpy cozy figure in a humble domestic or snowy setting. Keep the color muted and the touch dry. No ink outlines, no digital smoothness, and no glossy light.", "768-mizuno": "Render the image as a Junko Mizuno illustration. Mix a kawaii face, big sparkling eyes, and sweet pastel color with creepy-cute details: stitches, tiny bones worn like jewelry, and frilled gothic clothes. Use clean decorative line and flat pattern. No realistic gore, no gritty manga scratch, and no photographic texture.", "769-paula-rego": "Render the image as a Paula Rego pastel painting. Draw heavy, solid, grounded figures in dense chalky pastel with firm contours and visible strokes, staged in a stark domestic interior. Keep the color muted and earthy, the light flat and theatrical, and the mood quietly unsettling, like a fairy tale told without comfort. No soft prettiness, no glossy digital finish, and no photographic detail.", "770-escher": "Render the image as an M.C. Escher black-and-white lithograph. Use precise crosshatched graphite-like shading, impossible architecture with staircases that loop back on themselves, and interlocking tessellated shapes that morph into one another toward the edges of the print. Keep the composition mathematically exact and the tones strictly black, white, and gray. No color, no loose brushwork, and no signature.", "771-russian-icon": "Render the image as a Russian Orthodox icon painted in egg tempera on a wooden panel. Use a burnished gold-leaf ground, reverse perspective, elongated solemn faces, and flat stylized drapery picked out with fine gold highlights. Let the aged varnish crack and darken slightly at the edges. No lettering, no naturalistic light, and no modern rendering.", "772-malika-favre": "Render the image as a Malika Favre minimalist illustration. Use flat vector shapes in only three or four bold colors, with dramatic negative space where figures and objects merge into the background. Keep the curves crisp, the shadows hard-edged, and the composition elegant and graphic. No gradients, no outlines, no texture, and no text.", "773-shaun-tan": "Render the image as a Shaun Tan painted illustration. Use oil and acrylic in muted ochres, rusts, and teals, with a quietly surreal suburban setting, odd shifts of scale, and a strange, gentle creature somewhere in the scene. Keep the brushwork soft and the mood melancholy and wondering. No bright cartoon color, no clean vector shapes, and no text.", "774-van-allsburg": "Render the image as a Chris Van Allsburg picture-book illustration drawn in Conté crayon. Use smooth, velvety black-and-white tones, dramatic low or high camera angles, and an uncanny stillness, as if something impossible is quietly happening in an ordinary place. Keep the forms solid and sculptural with soft, even light. No color, no outlines, and no text.", "775-roger-dean": "Render the image as a 1970s Roger Dean prog-rock album painting. Build an otherworldly landscape of floating rock islands, flowing organic arches, waterfalls pouring into the sky, and sinuous trees, with luminous airbrushed fantasy color. Keep the forms smooth and grown rather than built. No lettering, no logo, and no modern digital look.", "776-darwyn-cooke": "Render the image as Darwyn Cooke retro comic art. Use bold, confident brush ink, clean graphic shapes, and stylish 1960s mid-century design, colored in a limited, flat, warm vintage palette. Keep the staging cinematic and the figures elegant and simple. No speech balloons, no rendered gradients, and no text.", "777-toppi": "Render the image as Sergio Toppi pen-and-ink comic art. Use dense, intricate hatching and decorative linework, a fragmented, mosaic-like composition, and bold areas of untouched white paper. Let patterns and ornament flow out of the forms so the page reads as monumental design. Black ink only, no color, and no text.", "778-70s-shojo": "Render the image as a 1970s shōjo manga page. Draw huge starry eyes with many highlights and long lashes, delicate pen lines, and flowing hair, surrounded by roses, floating flowers, and sparkles on screentone backgrounds. Keep it black and white with dot screentones and a dreamy, romantic mood. No color, no speech balloons, and no text.", "779-kim-possible": "Render the image as a frame from the 2000s TV cartoon Kim Possible. Use sharp, angular character design with pointed limbs, tapered hands, and geometric hair shapes, colored with flat bright fills and simple hard shadow shapes. Keep poses energetic and backgrounds clean and stylized. No soft shading, no realistic anatomy, and no text.", "780-shin-chan": "Render the image as a frame from the anime Crayon Shin-chan. Use deliberately crude, simple character design with wobbly thick outlines, small dot eyes, simplified features, and flat colors, set against a plain, gently colored suburban background. Keep it goofy, flat, and unpolished. No detailed anime eyes, no shading, and no text.", "781-film-noir": "Render the image as a 1940s film noir still. Use high-contrast black-and-white photography, a hard low key light, venetian-blind shadows striping the scene, deep crushed blacks, drifting haze, and fine 35mm grain. Frame it with dramatic angles and a tense, fatalistic mood. No color, no modern lighting, and no text.", "782-matrix": "Render the image as a still from The Matrix. Apply a heavy sickly-green color grade, cool fluorescent light, sleek late-1990s cyberpunk cinematography, and glossy black clothing, with faint abstract green code rain glowing in the background. Keep the contrast hard and the mood cold and stylish. No warm tones and no readable text.", "783-jacques-demy": "Render the image as a still from a 1960s Jacques Demy musical. Use saturated candy pastels, with walls, costumes, and props deliberately color-coordinated, painted-set shopfronts, soft even studio light, and rich film-stock color. Keep the framing graceful and theatrical, like a sung romance. No gritty realism, no muted grading, and no text.", "784-mononoke-2007": "Render the image as a frame from the 2007 anime Mononoke. Use flattened ukiyo-e-inspired design, a visible washi paper texture laid over the entire image, bold saturated kabuki colors, and dense decorative patterns on walls, doors, and clothing. Keep the perspective flat and the mood eerie and theatrical. No 3D shading, no photographic detail, and no text.", "785-roblox": "Render the image as a Roblox game screenshot. Build every character as a blocky avatar with a cylindrical head, a simple decal face, and plain plastic materials, and every setting from basic user-built parts with default engine lighting. Keep it bright, toy-like, and slightly crude. No UI, no realistic textures, and no text.", "786-mii": "Render the image as a Nintendo Wii Mii avatar scene. Model characters as smooth, simple 3D figures with big round heads, minimal floating face parts, mitten hands, and glossy soft shading, in a bright, clean, friendly plaza. Keep everything simple and cheerful. No realistic anatomy, no UI, and no text.", "787-habbo": "Render the image as Habbo Hotel isometric pixel art. Build the scene from crisp isometric pixel furniture in a small hotel room, with blocky outlined pixel avatars and an early-2000s browser game palette, zoomed in so the main figure is large. Keep every edge aligned to the pixel grid. No anti-aliasing, no chat bubbles, no UI, and no text.", "788-digitized-fighter": "Render the image as an early 1990s digitized-sprite fighting game screen. Turn characters into filmed actors reduced to low-resolution sprites with a limited, slightly dithered palette and a hard cutout edge, placed in a moody painted stone arena. Keep the poses stiff and martial. No life bars, no UI, and no text.", "789-bobblehead": "Render the image as a bobblehead figurine product photo. Sculpt the figure in glossy painted resin with a hugely oversized head on a tiny body, a visible spring at the neck, and a round base, photographed on a car dashboard or shelf. Keep the paint slightly imperfect and the proportions comic. No realistic body proportions and no text on the base.", "790-scale-figure": "Render the image as a product photo of a 1/7 scale anime PVC figure. Sculpt everything in painted plastic, with crisp sculpted hair, glossy printed anime eyes, and a clear acrylic display base, shot with soft studio lighting on a clean backdrop and sharp macro detail. Keep the paint apps precise and the plastic sheen visible. No packaging text.", "791-papercraft": "Render the image as a photo of a cut-and-fold papercraft model. Build every form as a low-poly figure assembled from printed cardstock, with visible folds, glue tabs, slightly lifted seams, and flat printed textures, photographed on a cutting mat. Keep the facets crisp and the paper matte. No smooth curves, no plastic shine, and no text.", "792-needle-felt": "Render the image as a photo of a needle-felted wool figurine. Shape everything from soft, fuzzy wool fibers with rounded handmade forms, tiny bead eyes, and stray fibers catching the light, photographed in natural window light on a wooden table with shallow depth of field. Keep it cozy and slightly lumpy. No hard plastic, no smooth surfaces, and no text.", "793-jack-o-lantern": "Render the image as a photo of a carved jack-o'-lantern at night. Carve the subject into a large orange pumpkin using layered shaving depths, so thin areas glow bright, thicker areas glow dim, and cut-through holes blaze with candlelight, set on a dark porch with autumn leaves. Keep it warm and handmade. No paint and no text.", "794-mola": "Render the image as a Guna mola textile panel. Build the image in reverse appliqué of layered bright cotton, cutting through to reveal red, orange, black, and green layers, with parallel outline channels echoing every shape, maze-like filler patterns, and tiny visible hand stitches. Photograph it flat. No painted shading and no text.", "795-scrimshaw": "Render the image as a scrimshaw engraving. Incise the image in fine, ink-filled lines and delicate stippling on an antique curved bone with a warm, yellowed polish, with nautical motifs such as ships and waves in the margins, photographed on dark wood. Keep it monochrome black ink and hand-scratched. No color and no text.", "796-ansel-adams": "Render the image as an Ansel Adams large-format black-and-white photograph. Use zone-system tonality from rich deep black to luminous white, crystal-clear detail, dramatic skies, and monumental wilderness such as granite cliffs, with a heroic, still composition. No color, no grain, no soft focus, and no text.", "797-slit-scan": "Render the image as a slit-scan photograph. Smear time across the frame so moving forms stretch, warp, and repeat into fluid bands, with sharp streaks where motion paused, while static parts of the scene stay crisp. Keep the colors natural and the distortion purely optical. No painted effects and no text.", "798-sand-animation": "Render the image as a sand animation on a lightbox. Draw the scene with fingertips in dark sand on a glowing amber light table, with soft grainy edges, light shining through thin areas, scattered loose grains, and a hand just entering the frame. Keep it monochrome sand and warm light. No color and no text.", "799-pin-art": "Render the image as a pin art toy photo. Form the image as a 3D relief pressed into hundreds of blunt silver metal pins in a clear plastic frame, with shiny pin tips catching the light and the relief built purely from pin depth. Shoot it as a close-up product photo. No color painted on the pins and no text.", "800-frutiger-aero": "Render the image as a Frutiger Aero scene from mid-2000s tech advertising. Use glossy aqua glass bubbles, translucent reflective orbs, water droplets, lens flares, and a bright green hill under a clean blue sky, with a cheerful stock-photo mood. Keep surfaces shiny and optimistic. No interface text, no logos, and no gritty texture.", "801-waynes-world": "Render the image as a still from the 1992 comedy Wayne's World. Set it in a cheap public-access cable show filmed in a suburban basement, with wood paneling, a drum kit, electric guitars, a saggy couch, and harsh flat studio lights. Keep the early-90s film grain and goofy rock-fan energy, with casual mugging straight into the camera. No signs with readable text.", "802-back-to-the-future": "Render the image as a still from the 1985 film Back to the Future. Use warm 1980s Amblin cinematography and film grain, a small-town square at night beneath a big clock tower, a stainless-steel gull-wing time machine car glowing with blue light, and twin trails of fire burning on the asphalt. Keep the mood adventurous and nostalgic. No text and no license plate lettering.", "803-hitchhikers-guide": "Render the image as an animated 'Guide' entry from the 2005 film The Hitchhiker's Guide to the Galaxy. Use flat, clean 2D vector motion graphics with rounded, simplified iconographic figures, a soft limited palette of muted teal, warm orange, cream, and brown, subtle drop shadows, and a tidy infographic layout like an animated encyclopedia entry, with small cartoon planets and spaceships. Keep it friendly, witty, and calm. No outlines, no gradients, no letters, and no numbers.", "804-max-headroom": "Render the image as a frame from 1980s Max Headroom TV. Give figures a glossy, plastic-smooth, faux-computer-generated look with sculpted, shiny hair, placed in front of a rotating colored line grid on black, with video stutter, scanlines, and analog broadcast glitches. Keep it slick and uncanny. No real 3D realism and no text.", "805-aerobics-video": "Render the image as a frame from a 1980s home aerobics VHS tape. Use a neon-lit studio with mirrors and a glossy floor, high-cut leotards, leg warmers, terry headbands, and big cheerful mid-stretch poses, with soft VHS blur, color bleed, and tracking noise. Keep it bright and relentlessly upbeat. No on-screen text.", "806-untitled-goose-game": "Render the image as a scene from Untitled Goose Game. Use flat pastel 3D with no outlines and simple untextured shapes, soft even light, and a gentle high camera angle over a tidy English village garden, with a mischievous white goose somewhere in the scene. Keep characters simple and nearly faceless. No textures, no UI, and no text.", "807-cabbage-patch": "Render the image as a photo of a Cabbage Patch Kids doll. Give figures a soft-sculpture vinyl face with round cheeks, small close-set eyes, and a tiny smile, yarn hair, and a soft stuffed cloth body, shot like 1980s toy photography against a soft backdrop. Keep it cuddly and slightly homely. No packaging and no text.", "808-blythe-doll": "Render the image as a photo of a custom Blythe doll. Give figures an oversized head with huge glassy eyes, a tiny body, and a delicate hand-painted face-up, styled in miniature clothes and props, shot with soft window light and dreamy shallow depth of field. Keep it pretty and slightly uncanny. No text.", "809-clay-render": "Render the image as an untextured gray clay 3D render. Give every surface the same matte, uniform gray material, with soft ambient occlusion, gentle studio light, and fine sculpted detail, like a 3D modeling preview before texturing. Keep it monochrome and clean. No color, no textures, and no text.", "810-rice-paddy-art": "Render the image as Japanese rice paddy art seen from a drone. Plant the picture across a giant field using rice varieties with different leaf colors, such as deep purple, yellow, white, and green, so it reads only from above, with tiny farm roads and houses at the edges in summer daylight. Keep the edges slightly organic, as living plants. No text.", "811-high-speed-flash": "Render the image as a high-speed flash photograph in the manner of Harold Edgerton. Freeze one instant with a microsecond strobe, so liquids hang in crisp crowns of droplets and flying fragments are suspended in midair, against a pure black background with sharp specular highlights. No motion blur and no text.", "812-gond": "Render the image as a Gond painting from central India. Draw bold, flat figures, animals, and trees, each filled entirely with patterns of fine dots, dashes, and lines in bright acrylic colors on a plain background. Keep the forms decorative and rhythmic. No shading, no perspective, and no text.", "813-photogram": "Render the image as a cameraless photogram. Lay forms directly on photographic paper so they appear as glowing white silhouettes on deep black, with soft gray gradations where objects are translucent and fuzzy edges where they lifted off the paper. Black-and-white gelatin silver only. No camera perspective and no text.", "814-early-gan": "Render the image as a 2018-era GAN-generated image. Let the details melt: smeary, asymmetric features, mismatched accessories, forms dissolving into a blotchy painterly background, objects merging into each other, and low-resolution artifacts, while the overall layout stays plausible at a glance. Keep it eerily almost-right. No text.", "815-sasek": "Render the image as a 1960s Miroslav Šašek travel picture-book illustration. Use cheerful gouache and loose ink lines, flat bright color shapes, quirky stylized city architecture, and bustling, slightly comic passersby. Keep it witty, sunny, and observational. No text and no signs.", "816-lobel": "Render the image as an Arnold Lobel picture-book illustration from the Frog and Toad era. Use gentle pencil lines and soft hatching with watercolor in muted greens, browns, and pale yellows, in a cozy, quiet setting with understated humor. Keep the palette limited and the mood warm. No bright color and no text.", "817-scheffler": "Render the image as an Axel Scheffler picture-book illustration from The Gruffalo era. Use bold, slightly wobbly black outlines, rounded friendly shapes, rich saturated colored inks with crayon texture, and lively wide-eyed animals in a lush woodland. Keep it warm and funny. No text.", "818-gurney": "Render the image as a James Gurney Dinotopia painting. Use luminous, realistic oil painting with warm golden light, careful perspective, and atmospheric haze, set in a classical sunlit city where people and dinosaurs live peacefully side by side. Keep it plausible and wondrous. No text.", "819-benton": "Render the image as a Thomas Hart Benton regionalist painting. Make figures sinuous and rubbery, and let rolling hills, buildings, and clouds curve and swell with rhythmic energy, painted in egg tempera with oil glazes in earthy greens, ochres, and blues. Keep it sculptural and full of motion. No flat graphic shapes and no text.", "820-totally-spies": "Render the image as a frame from the 2000s animated series Totally Spies!. Use elongated, anime-influenced character design with huge glossy eyes, long thin limbs, and sleek fashion outfits, colored in flat bright fills with simple shadow shapes, in a glamorous, glossy setting. Keep poses confident and dynamic. No text.", "821-lucky-luke": "Render the image as a Lucky Luke Franco-Belgian comic panel. Use clean, confident ink lines, flat bright colors, lanky caricatured figures, and a humorous Wild West setting with dusty desert light, in the classic 1960s bande dessinée look. Keep the staging clear and comic. No speech balloons and no text.", "822-tarsila": "Render the image as a Tarsila do Amaral Brazilian modernist painting. Use smooth, rounded, simplified forms with exaggerated proportions such as enlarged limbs and small heads, a stylized sun, cactus, and tropical plants, and soft tonal gradients in pink, green, blue, and ochre. Keep the landscape flat and dreamlike. No text.", "823-butter-sculpture": "Render the image as a state fair butter sculpture. Carve everything from pale yellow butter with a smooth, creamy surface and visible tool marks, displayed inside a refrigerated glass case under cool showcase lighting, with faint reflections of fair visitors in the glass. Keep it monochrome butter. No signs and no text.", "824-takahashi": "Render the image as a frame of 1980s Rumiko Takahashi anime from the Urusei Yatsura and Ranma ½ era. Use round faces, big expressive eyes, bouncy hair shapes, and simple, clean cel colors with soft painted backgrounds, staged with comic energy in an everyday Tokyo setting. No text.", "825-auerbach": "Render the image as a Frank Auerbach painting. Build the forms from extremely thick impasto oil paint in heavy ridges and gouges, dragged out with broad loaded strokes until the surface is almost sculptural, in raw ochres, umbers, and strong cadmiums. Keep the drawing urgent and the likeness emerging from the paint. No smooth blending, no fine detail, and no text.", "826-fragonard": "Render the image as a Rococo painting by Jean-Honoré Fragonard. Use feathery, quick pastel brushwork, frothy garden foliage, billowing silks in pink, powder blue, and cream, gilded details, and airy, flattering light, with a playful, flirtatious mood. No hard edges, no dark drama, and no text.", "827-hammershoi": "Render the image as a Vilhelm Hammershøi interior painting. Show a quiet, nearly empty room with gray walls, white-painted doors, and pale window light, in a muted palette of grays, blacks, and soft ochre, with figures often seen from behind and an air of profound stillness. Keep the paint soft and matte. No bright color, no clutter, and no text.", "828-poortvliet": "Render the image as a Rien Poortvliet illustration from the book Gnomes. Use delicate naturalist watercolor and fine pencil in earthy woodland colors, with field-guide detail, cozy burrows under tree roots, mushrooms, and small forest creatures. Keep it gentle, observant, and storybook-real. No bold outlines and no text or labels.", "829-beskow": "Render the image as an Elsa Beskow Swedish picture-book illustration. Use soft watercolor with fine ink outlines, flower meadows and birch forests, gentle decorative symmetry, and small nature spirits, in a sweet early-20th-century storybook design. Keep the colors fresh and clear. No heavy shadows and no text.", "830-eh-shepard": "Render the image as an E.H. Shepard illustration in the manner of the Winnie-the-Pooh books. Use light, sketchy pen lines with soft, pale watercolor washes and plenty of untouched white paper, in a gentle English woodland with a wistful, playful mood. No heavy color, no dense backgrounds, and no text.", "831-crepax": "Render the image as Guido Crepax 1960s Italian comic art. Use elegant black-and-white ink with sinuous lines, a fragmented page of many narrow panels and extreme close-ups of eyes, hands, and objects, and fashionable mod design, in a dreamlike sequence. No color, no speech balloons, and no text.", "832-allred": "Render the image as Mike Allred pop comic art from the Madman era. Use bold, clean outlines, bright flat pop colors with subtle printed dot textures, retro 1960s mod and space-age design, and quirky, stylish figures with cheerful energy. No realistic rendering, no speech balloons, and no text.", "833-doug": "Render the image as a frame from the 1991 Nickelodeon cartoon Doug. Use simple, rounded character design with unusual skin colors, big noses, minimal detail, thin outlines, and flat pastel colors, set in a calm, plain suburban town. Keep it gentle and slightly awkward. No shading and no text.", "834-miami-vice": "Render the image as a still from the 1980s TV series Miami Vice. Use pastel linen suits over T-shirts, neon pink and turquoise, palm trees and Art Deco hotels at sunset, glossy sports cars, and soft 35mm TV film, with a slow-motion, effortlessly cool mood. No modern grading and no text.", "835-90s-sitcom": "Render the image as a 1990s multi-camera sitcom still. Use a cozy, brightly lit studio set such as a coffee house or apartment with a big couch, flat, even three-point studio lighting, a slightly soft broadcast video look, and big, readable comedic reactions. No cinematic shadows and no signs with text.", "836-grindhouse": "Render the image as a 1970s grindhouse exploitation film still. Damage the print with heavy scratches, dust, burns, jumpy frame lines, and faded color drifting toward magenta, with gritty, saturated drive-in cinematography and tough low-angle hero shots. No clean digital look, no title cards, and no text.", "837-the-shining": "Render the image as a still from the 1980 film The Shining. Use perfectly symmetrical one-point perspective, a wide-angle lens, long hotel corridors or vast lounges, bold geometric carpet patterns in orange, red, and brown, and cold, even light, with an eerie, frozen stillness. No handheld feel and no text.", "838-french-new-wave": "Render the image as a still from a 1960s French New Wave film. Use black-and-white handheld 35mm with natural available light, real Paris streets and cafés, casual, spontaneous framing, characters glancing straight into the lens, and soft film grain. Keep it effortlessly cool and unpolished. No color and no text.", "839-dezaki": "Render the image as an Osamu Dezaki anime frame from the 1970s and 1980s. Turn the key moment into a dramatic 'postcard memory' freeze frame painted with chalky pastel texture, with strong light flares, split-screen or repeated framings, and intense close-ups with sharp highlights in the eyes. No modern digital gloss and no text.", "840-professor-layton": "Render the image as Professor Layton game art. Use European-flavored anime characters with long limbs, small eyes, and quirky silhouettes, set against soft, painted watercolor-like backgrounds of quaint old towns, in a warm, muted palette with a gentle mystery mood. No UI and no text.", "841-neopets": "Render the image as early-2000s Neopets website art. Make figures cute, chunky cartoon creatures with thick dark outlines, big shiny eyes, and soft airbrushed digital shading, posed against a simple pastel gradient backdrop with a glossy, collectible look. No UI and no text.", "842-potato-head": "Render the image as a photo of a Mr. Potato Head toy. Build figures from a glossy brown plastic potato body with oversized plug-in parts such as eyes, mouth, ears, hair, and accessories, photographed in bright toy-room light. Keep the plastic shine and the plug-in seams visible. No realistic anatomy and no packaging text.", "843-latch-hook": "Render the image as a 1970s latch hook rug. Build the picture from thick, shaggy tufts of acrylic yarn knotted on a canvas grid, so shapes are fuzzy and slightly blocky, in a retro palette of orange, brown, gold, and avocado green, photographed hanging on a wood-paneled wall. No fine detail and no text.", "844-pressed-flowers": "Render the image as pressed flower art. Assemble the picture entirely from real dried, flattened petals, leaves, ferns, and grasses arranged on cream paper, with delicate translucent overlaps and faded natural color, framed simply under glass. No paint, no drawn lines, and no text.", "845-lubok": "Render the image as a Russian lubok folk print. Cut the image as a crude woodcut with bold black lines and hand-color it with flat blotches of red, yellow, and green that slip outside the lines, using naive proportions, decorative flowers, and humorous folk storytelling. No shading and no lettering.", "846-trail-camera": "Render the image as a trail camera photo. Shoot it as a motion-triggered wildlife camera in the woods, at night with an infrared flash that turns everything grayscale with glowing eyes, or by day with washed-out color, using harsh flat light, motion blur, and low-quality sensor noise. No timestamp and no text.", "847-memphis-group": "Render the image as 1980s Memphis Group design. Build the scene from playful postmodern furniture and objects with clashing primary and pastel colors, terrazzo and squiggle laminate patterns, black-and-white grids, and chunky geometric blocks, styled like a design-magazine photo. No muted tones and no text.", "848-fractal-flame": "Render the image as a fractal flame render. Draw everything as glowing filaments of light swirling in self-similar curls and logarithmic spirals, with smoke-like luminous gradients in rich orange, gold, and violet on black, so forms emerge from the flame. No solid surfaces and no text.", "849-michelangelo": "Render the image as a Michelangelo fresco on a Sistine ceiling. Build a monumental clothed figure with sculptural weight, heavy drapery, and a twisting pose, set among painted architecture and a strip of sky. Use fresco color: dusty rose, stone gray, dull gold, and soft plaster. Model the form as carved marble seen through thin paint. No nude display, no photographic texture, and no lettering.", "850-velazquez": "Render the image as a Diego Velázquez court portrait. Paint with silvery Spanish light, a deep palace interior, and a loose loaded brush that stays precise in the face and freer in the cloth. Use black, warm gray, dull gold, and a little red. Keep the figure large, still, and psychologically direct, with air around the body. No hard studio flash, no modern sharpness, and no lettering.", "851-jacques-david": "Render the image as a Jacques-Louis David neoclassical history painting. Stage the figure like an actor in a Roman relief: hard directional light, crisp folds, a stern gesture, and a shallow architectural space. Use sober red, stone, and cold ivory, with an enamel-smooth finish and almost no romantic blur. No impressionist dabs, no soft atmosphere, and no lettering.", "852-ingres": "Render the image as a Jean-Auguste-Dominique Ingres portrait. Draw a flawless continuous contour, then fill it with porcelain-cool color and almost no visible brush. Stretch the pose into a graceful, slightly impossible curve, and polish skin and silk until they look enameled. Use icy blue, pale gold, and black. No broken color, no rough impasto, and no lettering.", "853-delacroix": "Render the image as an Eugène Delacroix romantic oil painting. Whip the scene into motion with hot reds, stormy blue-green, and flashing gold, loose swirling strokes, and theatrical light. Let drapery and hair stream, and set a large foreground figure against smoke or a rearing animal. No neoclassical calm, no tiny detail everywhere, and no lettering.", "854-bruegel": "Render the image as a Pieter Bruegel the Elder panel. Keep one monumental peasant figure in the foreground, then pack the rest with a sharp, earthy village: tiny workers, snow or harvest, dogs, barrels, and crooked roofs, all in deep focus. Use dull red, ochre, gray-green, and lead white. Paint with Northern precision, not Italian grace. No demons, no soft focus, and no lettering.", "855-hokusai": "Render the image as a Katsushika Hokusai woodblock. Use bold imported Prussian blue, a heavy black keyblock, and great curling wave or mountain contours, with Mount Fuji small in the distance. Draw the figure large and graphic, with the energetic shorthand of Hokusai manga rather than a quiet beauty print. Flat color, crisp registration, aged paper. No soft rain haze, no photographic depth, and no lettering.", "856-futurism": "Render the image as an Italian Futurist painting in the manner of Umberto Boccioni. Shatter a striding figure into force-lines, repeated transparent positions, and metal-like facets, so speed is the subject. Those repeats are motion trails of the same two arms and two legs, never extra limbs. Use bronze, scarlet, and electric yellow. No calm portrait lighting and no lettering.", "857-malevich": "Render the image as a Kazimir Malevich Suprematist composition. Reduce the figure to a few large flat planes, a circle, a bar, and a cross, floating on an open white field with no horizon. Use only black, red, yellow, and a little blue, with hard edges and no shading. Keep the figure readable and large. No grid filling the whole canvas, no texture, and no lettering.", "858-de-kooning": "Render the image as a Willem de Kooning Woman painting. Attack the figure with slashing brushloads of flesh pink, acid green, cadmium yellow, and black, so the body stays frontal and huge while the features smear and reassemble. Leave drips, skips, and raw canvas. One torso, two arms, two legs. No polite portrait finish, no pure drip field without a figure, and no lettering.", "859-leibovitz": "Render the image as an Annie Leibovitz editorial photograph. Build a theatrical but believable set, with painterly color, a large subject, and light that feels placed by hand: a soft key, a rim, and a rich shadow. Use saturated but natural color, fine detail, and a magazine-cover sense of occasion. No plastic hyper-saturation, no snapshot mess, and no logos or lettering.", "860-avedon": "Render the image as a Richard Avedon portrait. Isolate the subject on a seamless white ground, in black and white, with a hard beauty light that shows every line and a shadow sharp enough to cut. Keep the framing plain and the expression unguarded, as if caught mid-breath. No scenery, no color, no soft glamour diffusion, and no lettering.", "861-weegee": "Render the image as a Weegee press photograph. Shoot at night with a brutal on-camera flash, a deep black background, and blown highlights on the face. Use coarse grain, a slightly tilted frame, and the feeling of a tabloid printed fast. The subject stays large and close. No cinematic color grade, no soft window light, and no caption text.", "862-sherman": "Render the image as a Cindy Sherman film still. Dress the scene as a constructed cinematic moment from a 1950s or 1970s movie: one character, period styling, motivated light, and a hint of a plot just outside the frame. Use color film grain and a slightly staged gaze. It is one person, not a collage. No fashion-ad polish, no snapshot party, and no titles or lettering.", "863-goldin": "Render the image as a Nan Goldin snapshot. Use direct on-camera flash in a small lived-in room, saturated 1980s color negative, messy bedding or bathroom tile, and a tender unposed closeness. Keep the subject large, with the harsh flash falling off into a dim cluttered background. No crime-scene sensationalism, no studio lighting, and no lettering.", "864-glaser": "Render the image as a Milton Glaser Push Pin illustration. Draw a flowing black contour and fill the figure with rainbow bands, decorative curls, and flat unexpected color, like a 1960s poster that never needed type. Use playful historic curves updated with pop flatness. No photorealism, no corporate grid, and no words or logos.", "865-rand": "Render the image as a Paul Rand modernist graphic. Reduce the subject to a few witty flat shapes and a clever negative space, in red, black, blue, and warm paper white. Keep the edges razor clean, the humor visual, and the composition as simple as a logo while the figure stays readable and large. No gradients, no texture, no letters, and no slogan.", "866-fairey": "Render the image as a Shepard Fairey screenprint. Limit the palette to cream, black, and a flat red, with coarse halftone, wheatpaste wrinkles, and a high-contrast stencil portrait on a worn wall. Use propaganda-poster geometry and a star or bar as pure shape, never as a mark that contains words. No full-color photography and no lettering.", "867-peak": "Render the image as a Bob Peak movie poster painting. Combine a realistic face with graphic streaks, slashing color, and 1960s compositional diagonals, as if several moments were painted into one sheet. Use hot orange, teal, and gold gouache over a dark ground. Keep the figure large. No title type, no billing block, no credits, and no logos.", "868-eisner": "Render the image as a Will Eisner comics moment, one large panel rather than a page of panels. Use brush-and-ink wash, rain, wet streets, theatrical black shadows, and a dramatic low or high angle in a dense city. Keep the figure large and the storytelling silent. No digital gloss, no speech balloons, and no logos or lettering.", "869-takahata": "Render the image as an Isao Takahata film frame in the manner of The Tale of the Princess Kaguya. Draw with visible colored pencil and pale watercolor on textured paper, delicate line, lots of untouched white, and a gentle hand-drawn wobble. Keep the figure large and the world airy. No cel shading, no digital cleanup, and no lettering.", "870-urasawa": "Render the image as a Naoki Urasawa seinen manga panel. Use precise realistic ink, careful gray tones, grounded everyday clothing, and a real city, with a face drawn in thriller clarity rather than sparkle. Keep the figure large and the moment quiet and tense. No superhero anatomy, no magical effects, no screentone clutter, and no speech balloons or lettering.", "871-clamp": "Render the image as a CLAMP manga illustration from the 1990s. Draw extremely ornate linework, huge detailed eyes, long ribbons, feathers, and filigree costumes, with elegant elongated hands and a decorative sparkle. Use jewel tones and clean ink. Keep the figure large. No gritty scratch, no chibi proportions, and no speech balloons or lettering.", "872-aboriginal": "Render the image as an Australian Aboriginal dot painting in the Western Desert tradition. Build the figure large from dotted fields of earth pigment: ochre, white clay, charcoal, and a little red, with concentric circles, tracks, and country patterns around it. Use a flat overhead map-like space and no outline drawing. No European perspective, no neon color, and no writing.", "873-ultrasound": "Render the image as a medical ultrasound. Place a large recognizable figure inside the fan-shaped scan, built from speckled gray echoes on black, with a bright curved near field and a fading far field. Show a cup and a small pastry as bright echoes beside the figure. No calipers, no measurements, no labels, and no photographic color.", "874-sonar": "Render the image as a sonar display. Draw the figure large in a circular ping image, green phosphor on black, with concentric range rings and a soft rotating sweep. Speckle the field and let the subject read as a strong return, with a cup as a smaller bright blip. No numeric readouts, no vessel names, and no interface chrome.", "875-lidar": "Render the image as a lidar point cloud. Build a large figure from sparse measured points floating in black space, denser on the face and cup, thinner at the edges, colored by height in a false-color ramp. Keep the points unconnected, with no solid skin and no mesh. No scanner interface and no text.", "876-satellite": "Render the image as a false-color satellite image. See the figure from directly above as a huge landform, in the unnatural bands of infrared agriculture imagery: hot red, black water, pale soil, and blocky sensor pixels. Keep the subject large and readable. No borders, no scale bar, no place names, and no labels.", "877-magic-eye": "Render the image as a Magic Eye autostereogram. Fill the sheet with a repeating noisy pattern of small motifs, and hide one large figure inside the depth so the silhouette is the only subject. The pattern itself stays abstract. No letters, no captions, and no obvious outline drawn on top.", "878-hologram": "Render the image as a transmission hologram. Float a large transparent figure in a black void, with rainbow diffraction fringes sliding across the form, speckled laser grain, and a faint second angle as the plate shifts. Let the cup catch a thin spectral edge. No solid studio lighting, no opaque skin, and no text.", "879-lenticular": "Render the image as a lenticular print. Interleave two poses of the same single figure under vertical plastic ridges, so the picture wants to flip. Each pose has a normal body, two arms and two legs. Show the ribbed surface and a slight misregistration. No second person and no lettering.", "880-weather-radar": "Render the image as a weather-radar frame. Paint the figure large as a precipitation core, in neon green, yellow, and red, on a dark field with faint range rings. Use soft radar blobs rather than hard ink, and a smaller cell for a cup. No city names, no legend, and no station logo.", "881-flip-dot": "Render the image as a flip-dot display. Build the whole picture from a grid of small round discs, each flipped to yellow or black, the way an old bus sign works. Keep the figure large and the disc grid obvious, including a cup made of discs. No destination words, no letter-shaped arrows, and no smooth pixels.", "882-kathakali": "Render the image as a Kathakali performer. Paint the face green with white ridge borders and red lips, and build a huge flared skirt, gold ornaments, and a dramatic dance pose under stage light. Keep the figure monumental. The makeup is flat and graphic and the costume is heavy cloth and bells. No candid photograph and no lettering.", "883-peking-opera": "Render the image as a Peking opera performer. Paint the face in flat symbolic blocks of color, add an elaborate headdress and a heavy embroidered costume, and freeze a staged gesture in theatrical light. Keep the figure large and the makeup graphic. No backstage candid, no photographic fashion light, and no lettering.", "884-henna": "Render the image as a mehndi design. Draw a large figure in raised dark henna paste on a warm skin-toned ground, using fine floral lines, paisley, and dotted fill, the way a bridal pattern covers a palm. Keep the linework crisp and handmade. No photographic portrait lighting, no colored ink, and no writing.", "885-kente": "Render the image as Kente cloth. Weave the figure large from bright narrow strips of geometric blocks in gold, green, red, and blue, with the rib of real loom cloth and sharp color changes at every thread. The person is the textile, not a model standing in front of it. No printed fake pattern, no photo blur, and no symbols that read as words.", "886-inuit-stonecut": "Render the image as a Kinngait stonecut print. Cut bold flat shapes as if cut from a stone block, in black and one or two stone-ink colors such as green and brick red, with a large simplified figure and a symmetrical animal nearby. Leave bare paper around the forms. No modeled shading, no fine hatching, and no lettering.", "887-mukimono": "Render the image as mukimono, Japanese vegetable carving. Carve a large figure from daikon, carrot, and cucumber, with thin petal cuts, translucent edges, and the wet shine of a garnish blown up to monumental scale. Keep the knife facets and a few curled peels visible. No clay, no plastic, and no text.", "888-sandcastle": "Render the image as a sandcastle sculpture. Pack wet sand into a large figure with drip-tower architecture, shell marks, and a carved cup at the base, on a beach in hard daylight. Show crumbly texture and hand-patted walls. No plastic toys as the subject and no writing in the sand.", "889-pinata": "Render the image as a piñata. Build a large figure from fringed tissue paper over a hollow form, in hot party colors, with layered cuts, a rope, and a few wrapped candies showing at a split seam. Keep the fringe as the surface, not smooth papier-mache. No logos and no lettering.", "890-yarn-bomb": "Render the image as yarn bombing. Knit and crochet a large figure in chunky mismatched yarn, with stripes and bobbles, stretched over a street fixture so the stitches and cable patterns are the picture. Daylight, urban, handmade, with a knitted cup in the same wool. No printed fabric, no smooth render, and no text.", "891-magic-lantern": "Render the image as a magic-lantern slide. Paint the scene on glass in translucent mineral colors, with a round projection vignette, soot specks, a hair in the gate, and the soft glow of an oil lamp thrown on a screen. Keep the figure large. No modern lens flare and no captions.", "892-suminagashi": "Render the image as suminagashi. Float black and indigo sumi ink on water in concentric combs, then let a large figure appear in the marbled veins, with gold size lines and the cockled look of transferred paper. Keep the rings watery and broken where the ink resisted. No hard outlines, no opaque poster color, and no calligraphy.", "893-flannelgraph": "Render the image as a flannelgraph. Cut the figure and a cup from thick colored felt and stick them on a fuzzy board, with simple shapes, visible fuzz, and soft shadows where the felt lifts. Keep the figure large and the look as plain as a Sunday-school board. No embroidery, no realistic faces, and no words.", "894-netsuke": "Render the image as a netsuke seen in macro. Fill the frame with a boxwood toggle carved as a large figure, polished, with undercuts, a cord hole, and tiny tool marks, so the carving reads huge. No wide shot of a tiny object on a table, no plastic shine, and no text.", "895-blacklight": "Render the image as a 1970s blacklight poster. Paint in fluorescent magenta, acid green, and electric blue that glow against a near-black ground, with psychedelic contours and a large figure. The only light is the ultraviolet glow, so unpainted areas drop out to pure black. No daylight, no readable words, and no logo.", "896-foil-stamp": "Render the image as a foil-stamped print. Deboss a large figure into matte black paper and fill the impression with metallic gold and copper foil, catching one hard specular streak. Leave the paper tooth visible outside the foil, and keep the bite of the stamp sharp. No ink rainbow, no lettering, and no glitter."};
